window.CBF_SUCHE=[{"t": "Ackerspec", "u": "pages/Ackerspec.html", "x": "Ackerspec Field Type Default Meaning --- --- --- --- count IntRange 1 How many of these the settlement wants, as a range. crops <br Crop list text none Name of a crop list (CropListDef). On a field it gives the crop, on a pen the growth on the ground, drawn by weight. plants only serves as a fallback when the list yields nothing. fence true/false false On a field: whether it gets a fence and a gate. Pens are always fenced; the value is not read there. plants list of text none The plant layer, for gardens and fields. tags <br Categories list of PieceCategoryDef none Categories of the houses this field is laid around. Fields go to houses of these categories first. Empty on a district field takes the categories of its budget. weight number 1 How likely this is picked over its siblings. Belongs on the settlement, not on the piece. Back to the field reference"}, {"t": "Annexes", "u": "pages/Annexes.html", "x": "Annexes An annex is a small piece that docks onto a larger one: a stable at the inn, a woodshed at the farmhouse, a beer garden behind the tavern. The house stays the same piece, the annexes make each copy of it look different. 1. Houses of a village. 2. Annexes : a pen, a hay store and a woodshed, docked at different sides. How it works 1. The generator places the buildings of a district. 2. Then it goes through the houses once more. For every house whose category names annex categories, it looks at the house's annex anchors in random order. 3. At each anchor it rolls Chance per anchor . On success it tries the annexes of the allowed categories in an order drawn by weight, and places the first one that fits. 4. It stops when the house has At most annexes. An annex belongs to the same district and ward as its house. It is placed after all normal buildings, so it only takes space that is still free. The anchor name Annexes use a Connect anchor. Pick the name Anbau from the name menu of the anchor. What the house needs - A Connect anchor named Anbau on its outer wall. Mark one cell per possible annex spot, on the wall cell itself. The cell outside must be free of the house. - Its category (or a parent) lists the Annex categories that may attach. See Categories. The + next to them opens a picker with search and mod filter. What the annex needs - A Connect anchor named Anbau on the side that faces the house. Two ways: - Shared wall: the anchor lies on a wall cell of the annex. The annex then uses the house wall as its own. Both cells must be walls. - Touching only: the anchor lies one cell outside the annex's shape. The annex then stands right next to the house with its own wall. - A category that a house names as annex category. - An annex without a door of its own counts as open. It is entered from outside, like a shed or a pen. An annex category with its pieces. The red Connect anchor either lies on the shared wall or one cell outside the shape: Where an annex is not placed - outside the ward of its house, or on ground that is already used: roads, other buildings, reserved space - next to a door of the house - next to a building other than its house - against the town wall, except in the old town (the innermost ward) - too close to the map edge If no annex fits at an anchor, that anchor stays empty. The house is built in any case. Settings Setting Where Default Effect --- --- --- --- Annex categories category of the house none Which annexes may attach. Empty takes the list of the parent. Chance per anchor category of the house 0.6 Chance that an annex is tried at one anchor. At most category of the house 2 Annexes per house. Repeatable category of the annex no Allows the same annex more than once on one house. Full wall category of the annex no The annex only attaches where it covers the whole wall side of the house, end to end, for example a lean-to along the full length. Overhang category of the annex 0 With Full wall: how many cells the annex reaches past the house wall at each end. Weight piece or category of the annex 1 Order among the annexes. Below 1 it is also the chance that the annex is tried at all. Tips - Give a house several annex anchors on different sides. With the default of at most 2, the house then looks different from copy to copy. - Keep annexes small, 3 to 8 cells a side. Large annexes rarely find room between the houses. - Annex categories do not have to be ordered in a settlement plan. If a plan orders them anyway, they are also built as buildings of their own."}, {"t": "BudgetEntry", "u": "pages/BudgetEntry.html", "x": "BudgetEntry Field Type Default Meaning --- --- --- --- count IntRange 1 How many of these the settlement wants, as a range. filler true/false false Counts as met at half its minimum. When free space is filled at the end, it may also go beyond its maximum. lootTable text none The loot table (LootTableDef) used here. On an anchor it fills the containers. On an object list member it replaces the anchor's table. On a content zone or a settlement budget entry it fills content zones. Not used on dungeon profiles. withIdeo list of text none Memes or precepts (defNames). The entry is only built when the owning faction's ideoligion has at least one of them. Needs Ideology. withoutIdeo list of text none Memes or precepts (defNames). The entry is not built when the owning faction's ideoligion has one of them. Needs Ideology. piece PieceDef none One specific piece by defName. It wins over a category in the same entry. In wall lists the name may also be a category. tag <br Category (old) PieceCategoryDef none The first category. Kept so old files still load; write categories instead. unique <br Only once true/false false At most one per settlement. weight number 1 How likely this is picked over its siblings. Belongs on the settlement, not on the piece. Back to the field reference"}, {"t": "BuildableRuleDef", "u": "pages/BuildableRuleDef.html", "x": "BuildableRuleDef XML node: <CustomBaseFramework.BuildableRuleDef . Also takes the common fields defName , label and description . Field Type Default Meaning --- --- --- --- fileContains list of text none Parts of a file name. A building without an architect category whose def file name contains one of them goes into category. Case is ignored. category text none The architect category (DesignationCategoryDef defName) that matching buildings go into. It is created if it does not exist. categoryLabel text none Label of category when it has to be created. order whole number 0 Menu order of category when it has to be created. rank whole number 0 Order in which the rules are checked. Lower comes first, the first match wins. Back to the field reference"}, {"t": "Categories", "u": "pages/Categories.html", "x": "Categories A category groups pieces, for example HouseSmall, Workshop or Military. Settlements order categories, not single pieces, and the generator picks a piece from the category. Placement rules live on the category, so every piece in it follows them. 1. List of categories with the number of pieces. The chips show all, only used or only empty ones. 2. Tiles of every piece in the category. Click one to open it in the Piece tab. 3. Rules on the right, in sections. 4. Used in lists every settlement that orders this category. Click a line to open the settlement. Inheritance A category can have a Parent . Two things follow from it: - Rules go down. A rule set to inherits takes the value of the parent, and so on up the chain. The tooltip says where a value comes from. - Pieces go up. A piece in a child category also counts as a piece of the parent. A settlement that orders the parent may get pieces of the children. - Merging stays within the own category. Two houses share a wall only if their own first category is the same. A parent never joins them: a brewery and a tannery ordered through Workshop stay apart. Placement - Wall contact : pieces of this category prefer to stand against the town wall. The piece field At the wall controls this more finely and wins. - Weight : how often pieces of this category are drawn when several fit. - Road access and Needs no road : with Road access set to No and Needs no road ticked, buildings of this category need no road and may be entered from every side, like a farm or a market. Needs no road is the zone Outside in XML; Inside and Center do nothing. Where a building stands is decided by the budget of its ward, not by the category. How many buildings of a category a settlement gets is set by its entries in the Settlement tab, where they stand by the district. Location Where a group of buildings lies in the town is set per district in the Settlement tab, see Settlements. Categories have no location values. Blocks - Block limit : how many buildings of this category at most form one block, wall to wall. Empty means 3. - Merge : whether buildings join their neighbors into one block. Yes forces it, No forbids it. Annexes An annex is a small piece that docks onto a house: a stable, a woodshed, a beer garden. 1. Annex categories : which annexes may attach to pieces of this category. Empty takes the list of the parent. 2. Chance per anchor : the chance that an annex is built at one annex anchor of the house. Default 0.6. 3. At most : annexes per house. Default 2. 4. Repeatable : set on the category of the annex. Yes allows the same annex several times on one house. The house marks where annexes go with a Connect anchor named Anbau on its outer wall. The annex has one too. How the generator places them and what an annex needs is explained on Annexes."}, {"t": "Compatibility", "u": "pages/Compatibility.html", "x": "Compatibility Content for other mods Custom Base Framework brings its own buildings and styles for some mods, designed for their things. They load only when that mod is active, and most of them work with both Medieval Overhaul and Vanilla Factions Expanded - Medieval 2. Mod Folder Content --- --- --- Medieval Overhaul 1.6/Mods/MedievalOverhaul Village, market town, large market town and fortress city, and a tribal camp for neolithic factions. Houses, workshops, castle, churches, military buildings, market squares, tournament ground, small gap houses for narrow lanes. Huts get wood stoves and changing beds (straw, fur, wicker, log). Two quests, see Quests. Vanilla Factions Expanded - Medieval 2 1.6/Mods/VFEMedieval2 The same four settlement types with their own buildings, rebuilt from the Medieval Overhaul set with base game and VFE Medieval 2 things only. Works without Medieval Overhaul. Living houses use the self-refilling shelf of VFE Medieval 2, armories and treasuries have shelves with their own loot tables. none 1.6/Defs Modern village, modern town, large town and metropolis from base game things only, for industrial, spacer and ultra factions. Spacer defence nests and bunkers (the mech shield only with Royalty), two fallback gatehouses of stone and wood, modern wall profiles with sandbag lines. World Domination 2.0 1.6/Mods/WorldDomination , WorldDomination MO , WorldDomination VFEM2 Outposts for the World Domination types: farm, logging and mining camps, production and slave villages, war camps. Modern ones always, medieval ones with Medieval Overhaul or VFE Medieval 2, see Settlement types. Their buildings (granary, sawmill, charcoal kiln, smelter, barracks, slave pen, command tent and more) also load without World Domination. Dark Ages: Crypts and Tombs inside the Medieval Overhaul and VFE Medieval 2 pieces Three extra cemeteries each for Medieval Overhaul and VFE Medieval 2, with sarcophagi, burial urns, gargoyles, heroic memorials, sepulchral braziers and candles from Dark Ages. With Dark Ages active they replace the plain cemeteries; without it the plain ones are built. A Rim of Ice and Fire 1.6/Mods/RIAF , RIAF MO Holdings of the houses built by CBF: hamlet, small market town and small fortress for Medieval Overhaul, on 150 maps. See A Rim of Ice and Fire. Some settlement plans serve every faction of a tech level. Medieval factions from other mods get medieval towns, modern factions get modern towns, without patches. Multiplayer The planner is deterministic. It stops after a counted amount of work, not after a time limit. With Multiplayer ( rwmt.Multiplayer ) it runs without a clock, and the same seed gives the same settlement on every machine. Mods that reshape the map Map Designer and Geological Landforms work together with CBF. They shape every other map as usual, but they do not act while CBF generates a settlement, so rivers, cliffs and terrain features do not cut through the town. Mods with settlements of their own World Domination 2.0 World Domination turns the world into a living map, similar to RimWar with outposts: - Faction settlements have a tier: T1 outpost, T2 town, T3 fortress, T4 citadel. - They gain strength every day, grow, found new settlements, build roads, send traders and raid each other and you. - Under pressure, factions pack weak settlements into a T4 hub. T4 settlements can have mortars and anti-air. - Your own World Domination outposts (farm, mine, research and so on) have no map of their own. When one is attacked, World Domination builds a fixed defence map. CBF leaves these alone. Who does what World Domination keeps running the world: tiers, strength, growth, sabotage, mortars, reinforcements from nearby settlements and conquest. CBF takes over only the map of a settlement when you first arrive there: the layout, the defenders and the loot. For defenders and loot CBF uses the values and tables of World Domination, so its balance stays as its author set it. Build World Domination settlements with CBF in the mod settings switches this. On by default. Off: CBF leaves World Domination settlements alone. CBF also respects the map generation switch World Domination has per faction under Allegiances in World Setup. The setting and the WD fields in the Settlement tab only appear when World Domination is active. Which settlement type is built - CBF only picks among the settlement types the faction may get anyway. Tech level and faction rules stay the same. - Among these it takes the one whose WD tier matches the settlement, otherwise the closest one. - Every World Domination settlement also has a type that depends on its tier: logging, mining or farming camp at tier 1, production or slave village at tier 2, town at tier 3, citadel at tier 4, and vanguard camps at any tier. Among the plans of the right tier, CBF prefers those whose WD type lists this type, then those without a type. - If a settlement grows to a higher tier, its type changes as long as its map has not been generated yet. - Settlement types assigned by hand in the world editor stay as they are. The shipped types: village 1, market town 2, large market town 3, fortress city 4, for Medieval Overhaul and VFE Medieval 2 alike. Modern village 1, modern town 2, large town 3, metropolis 4. Outposts for the World Domination types come on top, see Settlement types. The modern types serve industrial, spacer and ultra factions alike; the garrison picks spacer units by tech level. Defenders The garrison of a CBF settlement follows its World Domination strength. A settlement at full strength has the defenders of its settlement type. A weakened one has fewer, but never less than a minimum per tier: Tier Minimum --- --- T1 50 % T2 60 % T3 75 % T4 90 % A settlement that escalated late in the game gets up to 25 % more defenders. Loot World Domination has a loot budget per tier, counted in silver market value: T1 1650, T2 4050, T3 6500, T4 9750. The goods come from its own tables for the settlement's type (farming, logging, mining, fortress, citadel, otherwise a default table per tier). The strength of the settlement does not change the budget. CBF fills the containers of a World Domination settlement in this order: 1. Themed containers keep their own CBF loot table: armouries, churches, libraries and the like. Together they may use up to half of the budget. 2. General storage gets the rest of the budget: chests, crates, sacks, barrels and empty shelves whose loot table counts as general storage (the storage, hut, house, kitchen, farm and pantry tables of CBF). Instead of their CBF table they get goods from the World Domination table, rolled by its weights. Containers are picked at random across the whole settlement, one stack per container and pass, at most 3000 silver per stack, until the budget is used up. 3. Medieval Overhaul containers that show their content (wine racks, treasure shelves, ore barrels, weapon racks, market stalls) keep Medieval Overhaul's own loot. World Domination goods would not match their look. When the budget is used up, the remaining general containers stay empty and show their empty look. If CBF cannot read the World Domination tables, the normal CBF loot is used. Loot in World Domination settlements in the mod settings mixes both kinds, 50% by default: Setting Result --- --- 100% Every container keeps its planned CBF or Medieval Overhaul loot. World Domination loot is off. 50% About half of the containers keep their planned loot. The others share the full World Domination budget, evenly, and each gets at least one item. 0% As described above: the general containers get the full World Domination budget. The World Domination budget is never reduced: below 100% you always find at least as much as at 0%, plus the planned loot of the containers that keep it. The share is drawn per container, so the mix differs from settlement to settlement. No WD loot on a settlement type switches World Domination loot off for that type regardless of the slider. For modders: a loot table with wdBudget set to true counts as general storage, see the field reference. The map - World Domination reveals the whole map of its own settlements on entry. It does not do this for CBF settlements. You explore them like any other map. - After the fight, Search site under Orders marks every closed container for opening, see Settlement types. Empire Vassal colonies of Empire get no CBF settlements, are no quest givers for CBF quests and are not listed under settlements per faction. A Rim of Ice and Fire CBF builds the settlements of all factions, including the eight castle factions of A Rim of Ice and Fire. Their RIAF castles are not generated. RIAF's own places (fiefs, banner calls, duels, ruins) stay with RIAF. Only the main seat of each house, the first settlement of its faction such as Winterfell, gets a large type (large market town or fortress city). All other seats become villages or market towns. Seat names stay as they are. The villages, towns and garrisons of the houses (holdings) are built by CBF too, when a settlement type with a matching RIAF holding exists. CBF builds the houses and walls. Defenders, levies, residents and stores stay with A Rim of Ice and Fire; the stores go to a free spot near the map centre. Without a matching type, A Rim of Ice and Fire builds the holding itself. CBF ships a hamlet, a small market town and a small fortress for Medieval Overhaul, made for 150 maps. Switch it off with Build A Rim of Ice and Fire holdings with CBF in the mod settings. Other mods with their own map generator Settlements that another mod builds with its own map generator keep it. CBF then also leaves their map size and world map icon alone. Both are part of Leave settlements of other settlement mods alone in the mod settings, on by default. Credits Thanks to Fabricator General (Blackrussian) for testing the mod and for many compatibility hints from his large mod list."}, {"t": "ContentZoneSpec", "u": "pages/ContentZoneSpec.html", "x": "ContentZoneSpec Field Type Default Meaning --- --- --- --- category text none On a loot entry: a ThingCategoryDef from which a random fitting thing is drawn. On a content zone: the object list (LootBuildingCategoryDef) whose containers go into the zone. chance number 1 Chance from 0 to 1. On a legend entry: that the thing is placed in a cell, or that the anchor is filled or manned. On a content zone: that it is filled at all. On a loot entry it only counts in always. count IntRange 1 How many of these the settlement wants, as a range. lootTable text none The loot table (LootTableDef) used here. On an anchor it fills the containers. On an object list member it replaces the anchor's table. On a content zone or a settlement budget entry it fills content zones. Not used on dungeon profiles. symbol text none The character that stands for this entry in the layers. Only the first character counts. Back to the field reference"}, {"t": "CropEintrag", "u": "pages/CropEintrag.html", "x": "CropEintrag Field Type Default Meaning --- --- --- --- plant text none The plant (ThingDef defName). Things that are not plants are skipped. weight number 1 How likely this is picked over its siblings. Belongs on the settlement, not on the piece. Back to the field reference"}, {"t": "CropListDef", "u": "pages/CropListDef.html", "x": "CropListDef XML node: <CustomBaseFramework.CropListDef . Also takes the common fields defName , label and description . Field Type Default Meaning --- --- --- --- plants list of CropEintrag none The plant layer, for gardens and fields. Back to the field reference"}, {"t": "Dekospec", "u": "pages/Dekospec.html", "x": "Dekospec Field Type Default Meaning --- --- --- --- count IntRange 0 How many of these the settlement wants, as a range. max whole number 0 How many the settlement may have at most. piece PieceDef none One specific piece by defName. It wins over a category in the same entry. In wall lists the name may also be a category. spacing IntRange 7~12 Distance along the lane between two decor pieces, in cells, as a range. tag <br Category (old) PieceCategoryDef none The first category. Kept so old files still load; write categories instead. weight number 1 How likely this is picked over its siblings. Belongs on the settlement, not on the piece. Back to the field reference"}, {"t": "DistrictSpec", "u": "pages/DistrictSpec.html", "x": "DistrictSpec Field Type Default Meaning --- --- --- --- entries list of BudgetEntry none On a loot table: the weighted pool that rolls draws from. On a district: its budget, which pieces or categories it wants and how many. fields list of Ackerspec none Field types. Only Suburb and Rural grow fields. On a district the fields are laid by its own houses. label text none The name shown on the world map and in the settlement tooltip. The defName stays as it is. mines list of Minenspec none Mines of this district. Each mine starts at a building of the district and runs as a tunnel with chambers into the rock. name text Default Name of the entry. On a district it links fields and pens to its houses and must be unique. On a pen it appears in messages. On a ward it is replaced by the ward kind, on a field it is not read, and in wall lists piece is written instead. pens list of Gehegespec none Animal pen types. Only the Rural ward builds pens. On a district they go near its own houses. prefCenter <br Toward the center number 0 How strongly this wants to be near the center. prefGate <br Toward the gate number 0 How strongly this wants to be near the gate. prefMain <br Along the main road number 0 How strongly this wants to sit on the main road. prefWall <br Toward the wall number 0 How strongly this wants to be near the wall. Back to the field reference"}, {"t": "DungeonProfileDef", "u": "pages/DungeonProfileDef.html", "x": "DungeonProfileDef XML node: <CustomBaseFramework.DungeonProfileDef . Also takes the common fields defName , label and description . Field Type Default Meaning --- --- --- --- mapSize <br Map size whole number 175 How large the map is that this settlement is generated on. style StyleDef none Old field. It only still picks the wall profile and the standard materials. Does not restrict piece selection. Back to the field reference"}, {"t": "Factions", "u": "pages/Factions.html", "x": "Factions Which faction gets which settlement A plan reaches a faction in one of two ways: 1. The plan names the faction or its faction category under Factions . 2. The plan ticks For all factions of these levels and the faction's tech level is among them. A plan that names no faction and does not tick For all factions of these levels is only used for places without a faction, such as quest sites. A faction that has plans of its own through the first way never gets the general plans of the second. Excluded keeps a faction away from a plan in any case. Faction overview Faction overview below the settlement list shows every faction in the game with the plans it gets and the chance of each one. Click a faction to open its window: assign plans, change their weight, and save all changed plans at once. Takeover Every faction that has a matching plan gets its bases from the generator. No patch is needed. Only the settlement step of the map is swapped, so faction bases keep their rocks and power lines. - A faction with no matching plan gets RimWorld's own settlement, never an empty map. - Players can switch single factions back to RimWorld's own bases in the mod settings, see Mod settings. - Merge patches in the settlement's General section, row Takeover , combines patch files the editor wrote into one."}, {"t": "Field reference", "u": "pages/Field-Reference.html", "x": "Field reference Every field you can write in the XML of Custom Base Framework, generated from the code and the English language files. Do not edit these pages by hand, the next run overwrites them. To change a text, change it in Languages/English/Keyed , then the editor shows it too. Definitions - BuildableRuleDef - CropListDef - DungeonProfileDef - UnitSwapDef - HelpDef - MapStepsDef - LootBuildingCategoryDef - LootTableDef - MaterialListDef - PawnListDef - PieceCategoryDef - PieceDef - RuinDef - SettlementPlanDef - StyleDef - WallProfileDef Parts used inside definitions - Ackerspec - BudgetEntry - ContentZoneSpec - CropEintrag - Dekospec - DistrictSpec - Garnisonseintrag - Gehegespec - HafenEintrag - HafenSpec - LegendEntry - LegendVariant - LootEntry - LootMember - MaterialRole - Minending - Minenspec - PawnListEntry - PlacementRules - Stadtteil - Tauscheintrag - Techschritt - TerrainWeight - WallEntry"}, {"t": "Garnisonseintrag", "u": "pages/Garnisonseintrag.html", "x": "Garnisonseintrag Field Type Default Meaning --- --- --- --- count IntRange 1 How many of these the settlement wants, as a range. list text none The pawn list (PawnListDef) these pawns are drawn from. post Garnisonsposten (Inside, Walkway, Gate) Inside Where the pawns stand: Inside on lanes, squares and open ground, Walkway on the walkway at open embrasures, Gate at the gates. Without such cells they stand inside. Back to the field reference"}, {"t": "Gehegespec", "u": "pages/Gehegespec.html", "x": "Gehegespec Field Type Default Meaning --- --- --- --- count IntRange 1 How many of these the settlement wants, as a range. crops <br Crop list text none Name of a crop list (CropListDef). On a field it gives the crop, on a pen the growth on the ground, drawn by weight. plants only serves as a fallback when the list yields nothing. herd IntRange 3~7 How many animals live in each pen, as a range. At most one per 12 inner cells, at least one. name text Pen Name of the entry. On a district it links fields and pens to its houses and must be unique. On a pen it appears in messages. On a ward it is replaced by the ward kind, on a field it is not read, and in wall lists piece is written instead. pawns <br Animals text none On a spawn anchor: one pawn kind (defName) for the spot, used when pawnList is empty. With neither, a regular defender takes the spot. On a pen: the pawn list (PawnListDef) its animals come from; empty leaves the pen empty. plants list of text none The plant layer, for gardens and fields. terrain <br Floor text none The floor layer. One character per cell, resolved through the legend. weight number 1 How likely this is picked over its siblings. Belongs on the settlement, not on the piece. Back to the field reference"}, {"t": "Getting started", "u": "pages/Getting-Started.html", "x": "Getting started Switch the editor on The editor is hidden by default so players do not stumble over it. - Mod settings: Options Mod settings Custom Base Framework tick Editor in the architect menu . A category CBF base editor appears in the architect menu. Its button opens the editor. - Development mode: with development mode on, the debug menu has an entry Open editor under Custom Base Framework. The editor needs a game with a map. A fresh colony is fine, nothing you do in the editor changes the colony. Your own mod Everything you save goes into one mod, shown at the top right of the window. 1. Mod shows the mod you work in. Click it for the menu: - New mod creates a mod in your Mods folder with the folders RimWorld needs and Custom Base Framework as dependency, with a link to its Workshop page. It is enabled and loads on the next start. - Open mod switches to a mod you made earlier. Only mods in your local Mods folder are listed. Mods from the Steam Workshop are read only. - Show mod folder opens it in the file explorer. - Export all areas exports every piece area on the current map in one go. It appears when the map has at least two areas. The editor adds the mods your content needs to the mod's About.xml by itself, whenever you save something that uses them. A settlement type may use pieces from any mod: when you save it, those mods are added to About.xml as dependencies, so players are asked to install them. The window 1. Tabs. Piece, Settlement, Categories, Loot, Pawn, Unit swap. The button at the bottom left switches between icons and names. 2. Back and forward. Every jump between entries is remembered, also across tabs. 3. Undo and redo. Also Ctrl+Z and Ctrl+Y, as long as no text field has the focus. 4. Saved or Unsaved. Hover it to see what is not saved yet, click it to jump there. 5. List. Entries with an error glow red. A yellow bar at the left edge marks unsaved changes. 6. Columns. Drag the border between two columns to change their width, double-click it to reset. The widths are remembered per tab. Most rows have a tooltip. The ? buttons next to some sections open a longer help text. Number fields take a comma or a point as decimal separator. If a file of your mod changes outside the editor while you have unsaved changes, the editor asks which version to keep. Pickers Whenever you pick a thing, a pawn kind or a list, a picker opens: 1. Search in names and labels. 2. Mod filter when the entries come from more than one mod. For lists and tables, a ... button next to each entry opens a preview of what is in it. For pawn lists the ... button opens the list for editing. Categories are picked the same way. The mod filter there also separates the subfolders of a mod, for example the Medieval Overhaul content of CBF."}, {"t": "HafenEintrag", "u": "pages/HafenEintrag.html", "x": "HafenEintrag Field Type Default Meaning --- --- --- --- count IntRange 1 How many of these the settlement wants, as a range. piece text none One specific piece by defName. It wins over a category in the same entry. In wall lists the name may also be a category. tag <br Category (old) text none The first category. Kept so old files still load; write categories instead. Back to the field reference"}, {"t": "HafenSpec", "u": "pages/HafenSpec.html", "x": "HafenSpec Field Type Default Meaning --- --- --- --- entries list of HafenEintrag none On a loot table: the weighted pool that rolls draws from. On a district: its budget, which pieces or categories it wants and how many. minRiverWidth whole number 10 Smallest river width in cells that counts for a harbor. The sea always counts. range whole number 45 How far from the settlement harbor pieces may stand, in cells. Back to the field reference"}, {"t": "HelpDef", "u": "pages/HelpDef.html", "x": "HelpDef XML node: <CustomBaseFramework.HelpDef . Also takes the common fields defName , label and description . Field Type Default Meaning --- --- --- --- imageWidth number 0 Largest width of the images on this help page, in pixels. 0 lets them fill the window width. images list of text none Texture paths of the images shown below the help text, in this order. Missing textures are skipped. Back to the field reference"}, {"t": "Overview", "u": "Open%20Guide.html", "x": "Custom Base Framework Custom Base Framework (CBF) builds faction settlements, villages and camps from hand-drawn building pieces. A generator plans the layout on the actual map: walls, gates, roads, districts, fields and pens. The pieces fill it. Everything is data. You draw pieces on the map, group them into categories and describe settlements as rings of wards with districts inside. The generator does the rest. Every building differs A piece is a template, not a fixed building. Each time it is placed, it comes out differently: - Interior. Beds, tables, storage and decoration come from object lists with chances. Many things have alternatives, and materials follow the map. - Annexes. Houses get side parts docked on: a shed, a stable, a storage area or a small yard. They change the outline of the building, so the town does not look assembled from the same blocks even from a distance. See Annexes. - Placement. Rotation, mirroring, the neighbouring buildings, the roads and the place in the town differ each time. You may recognise a house you have seen before, but inside and outside it looks different. More than 500 pieces ship with the mod, buildings and annexes together. What you need - RimWorld 1.6 and Harmony. - Custom Base Framework. - The mods your pieces use. Pieces that name things from a missing mod are skipped, not built with holes. CBF brings its own buildings for Medieval Overhaul and Vanilla Factions Expanded - Medieval 2, and a vanilla style for modern towns. For some mods there are extra pieces designed for their things: cemeteries with Dark Ages: Crypts and Tombs, outposts for World Domination 2.0 and holdings for A Rim of Ice and Fire. Cemeteries and outposts come for Medieval Overhaul and for VFE Medieval 2, the holdings for Medieval Overhaul. See Compatibility. Adding and removing - Running game: CBF can be added to a running game. Faction bases you have not visited yet are built by CBF when you first visit them. Bases you already visited stay as they are. - Removing: first use Remove CBF data from this save in the mod settings, then save, quit and remove the mod. See Mod settings. Editor guide The guide follows the tabs of the editor. 1. Getting started: switch the editor on, create a mod of your own, find your way around the window. 2. Piece : draw and edit pieces, set anchors, and add annexes that dock onto houses. 3. Settlement : settlement types with wards, walls, districts, fields and pens, and which factions get them. 4. Categories : group pieces and give them rules. 5. Loot : object lists, loot tables, containers and crop lists, and material lists for walls, doors, gates and roads. 6. Pawn : pawn kinds and pawn lists for garrisons and inhabitants. 7. Unit swap : let each faction send its own units. Playing - Shipped settlements: example towns, strength and performance. - Quests: the quests that come with Medieval Overhaul. - Mod settings: settlements per faction, error reports, removing CBF from a save. - Compatibility: content for other mods, World Domination, Multiplayer and more. Modding For modders who write XML by hand or want to know what the editor writes. - Your own content mod: folder layout, how the defs connect, wall profiles, styles, ruins. - Settlement types in XML - Pieces and categories in XML - Loot in XML - Pawns in XML - Quests in XML - Field reference: every field of every def."}, {"t": "LegendEntry", "u": "pages/LegendEntry.html", "x": "LegendEntry Field Type Default Meaning --- --- --- --- anchor text none Name of the anchor. Wall ties the cells to the town wall, Anbau marks where an annex may attach, Outside marks the outer side of a wall strip or gatehouse. Other names only label the anchor. anchorKind <br Kind AnchorKind (Connect, Fill, Spawn, Object) Connect What the anchor does. Connect only marks cells, see anchor. Fill places a container from objects and fills it from lootTable. Object places something from objects without the anchor's loot table. Spawn places pawns. biomeLeather true/false false On a thing entry: the stuff is replaced by the leather of a wild animal of the biome, chosen once per piece. chance number 1 Chance from 0 to 1. On a legend entry: that the thing is placed in a cell, or that the anchor is filled or manned. On a content zone: that it is filled at all. On a loot entry it only counts in always. count IntRange 1 Stack size of the thing placed at this cell, for items. Buildings ignore it. ideoBuilding true/false false On an Object anchor: places a building of the faction's ideoligion, such as an altar or ritual spot. Needs Ideology. lootForeign true/false false Leaves the loot to the mod that brings the container. The anchor's loot table is ignored. Without objects the anchor places nothing. lootTable text none The loot table (LootTableDef) used here. On an anchor it fills the containers. On an object list member it replaces the anchor's table. On a content zone or a settlement budget entry it fills content zones. Not used on dungeon profiles. onTop true/false false Places the anchor's object on top of what stands there, instead of clearing the cells first. Anchors in anchorsOver always do this. objects text none The object list (LootBuildingCategoryDef) a Fill or Object anchor places its buildings from. Without it an Object anchor stays empty. pawnCount <br Pawn count IntRange 1 How many pawns, as a range. On a spawn anchor at least one. On an object list, how many wait in a container that has any. pawnKind PawnKindDef none A pawn of this kind (PawnKindDef) is spawned in the cell instead of a thing. pawnList <br Pawn list text none Who can be hiding inside. Only works in containers with an inside, not on open shelves. pawns <br Animals text none On a spawn anchor: one pawn kind (defName) for the spot, used when pawnList is empty. With neither, a regular defender takes the spot. On a pen: the pawn list (PawnListDef) its animals come from; empty leaves the pen empty. required true/false false Only has an effect on a Connect anchor named Wall. The piece must then stand at the town wall. role text none Name of a building role, such as wall, floor or door. CBF puts the matching thing from the material list there when no thing or terrain is set. roof RoofDef none Where a roof is built. rotation text North Facing of the thing as drawn: North, East, South or West. It turns with the piece. Default North. stuff <br Material ThingDef none Material of the thing. On a variant, empty keeps the material of the entry. Empty or unfitting material falls back to the default material. symbol text none The character that stands for this entry in the layers. Only the first character counts. temperature number -9999 Target temperature in degrees Celsius for things with a thermostat, such as heaters and coolers. Empty leaves their own setting. terrain <br Floor TerrainDef none The floor layer. One character per cell, resolved through the legend. thing ThingDef none The thing (ThingDef defName) this entry stands for: placed in the cell, given as loot, or placed as a building. variants list of LegendVariant none Alternatives drawn per cell by weight. The entry's own thing or terrain, if set, takes part with weight 1. Back to the field reference"}, {"t": "LegendVariant", "u": "pages/LegendVariant.html", "x": "LegendVariant Field Type Default Meaning --- --- --- --- stuff <br Material ThingDef none Material of the thing. On a variant, empty keeps the material of the entry. Empty or unfitting material falls back to the default material. terrain <br Floor TerrainDef none The floor layer. One character per cell, resolved through the legend. thing ThingDef none The thing (ThingDef defName) this entry stands for: placed in the cell, given as loot, or placed as a building. weight number 1 How likely this is picked over its siblings. Belongs on the settlement, not on the piece. Back to the field reference"}, {"t": "Object lists and loot", "u": "pages/Loot.html", "x": "Loot The Loot tab has five parts, switched at the top of the list: Lists , Tables , Containers , Crops and Material lists . Material lists have a page of their own: Material lists. Changes reach the file only when you save. Object lists An object list names the structures a Loot or Object anchor may place: shelves, crates, market stalls. 1. Structures of the list. Pick structures adds more. 2. Table : the loot table that fills this structure. Mods that handle their own lootables show \"by the mod\". 3. When emptied : what the structure becomes after it has been searched. 4. Pawn inside on the right: a chance for someone hiding in it, hostile or of a faction. 5. Used in : the pieces whose anchors use this list. The preview behind the ... button in the Piece tab works the same way: changes there can be undone, and a Save button appears while the list has unsaved changes. Loot tables A loot table decides what comes out. Tables can draw from other tables, so small tables add up to big ones. 1. Entries : an item, another table or a whole category of things. Each has a weight, a count, quality and condition. Share shows the chance that follows from the weights. 2. + Item , + Table , + Category add entries. 3. Rolls and Chance on the right. The chance is checked once for the whole table: if it fails, the table gives nothing. If empty is what comes when nothing else does. 4. Selected entry : kind, Up to tech for categories, stuff, depth and what happens If missing (skip, a fallback, or silver with the entry's count). 5. Sample rolls the table once and shows the result. Affects Below the entries the tab shows where this table ends up: the tables that draw it, the settlements that reach it, and tiles of every piece that uses it. The anchors that use the table are highlighted in the tiles, the tooltip names them. Containers A container is a building that holds loot and changes its look once emptied, for example a stocked shelf that becomes an empty one. 1. Full and empty image side by side. 2. After emptying : the empty image, another building it Becomes , or it Vanishes . 3. Edit container opens the container window to change it. Crop lists A crop list is a weighted list of plants for fields and pens. Change it once and every field that names it follows. 1. Plants with weight and share. Pick plants opens the plant picker. 2. Used in : the fields and pens that name this list. Click to open the settlement. 3. Fields and pens pick the list in the Settlement tab, row Crop list ."}, {"t": "LootBuildingCategoryDef", "u": "pages/LootBuildingCategoryDef.html", "x": "LootBuildingCategoryDef XML node: <CustomBaseFramework.LootBuildingCategoryDef . Also takes the common fields defName , label and description . Field Type Default Meaning --- --- --- --- kind <br Kind LootDarstellung (Fill, Empty) Fill On a ward: which part of the settlement it is, Town, Suburb or Rural. Castle is accepted but not planned as a part of its own. On an object list: Fill fills the containers, Empty places them without loot or occupants. members list of LootMember none Which buildings belong to this loot category. pawnChance <br Pawn chance number 0 Chance per container that someone waits inside, 0 to 1. 0 means never. pawnCount <br Pawn count IntRange 1 How many pawns, as a range. On a spawn anchor at least one. On an object list, how many wait in a container that has any. pawnHostile <br Turns hostile true/false false The pawn attacks once the container is opened. Animals go manhunter, people go berserk. pawnFaction <br Faction text none Which faction the occupant belongs to. Without one it goes berserk instead, which reads as a trapped animal rather than an ambush, and they attack each other too. pawnList <br Pawn list text none Who can be hiding inside. Only works in containers with an inside, not on open shelves. Back to the field reference"}, {"t": "LootEntry", "u": "pages/LootEntry.html", "x": "LootEntry Field Type Default Meaning --- --- --- --- category text none On a loot entry: a ThingCategoryDef from which a random fitting thing is drawn. On a content zone: the object list (LootBuildingCategoryDef) whose containers go into the zone. chance number 1 Chance from 0 to 1. On a legend entry: that the thing is placed in a cell, or that the anchor is filled or manned. On a content zone: that it is filled at all. On a loot entry it only counts in always. count IntRange 1 How many of these the settlement wants, as a range. countOverDepth SimpleCurve none Curve that multiplies count by depth. Settlements have no depth, so only the value at 0 counts. depthRange <br Depth IntRange 0~99 Entries outside this depth range are never drawn. Settlements have no depth and count as 0, so a minimum above 0 means never. fallbackThings list of text none Things tried in order when thing does not exist and onMissing is Fallback. hitPoints <br Condition % FloatRange 1~1 Condition of the loot as a share of its hit points, as a range. 1 means undamaged. maxTechLevel <br Tech level at most TechLevel (Undefined, Animal, Neolithic, Medieval, Industrial, Spacer, Ultra, Archotech) Undefined Category entries only: things above this tech level are left out. Empty means no limit. onMissing <br If missing OnMissing (Skip, Fallback, Silver) Skip What happens when thing does not exist. Skip drops the entry, Fallback tries fallbackThings, Silver gives silver instead. quality QualityRange Awful~Legendary Quality range for things that have quality, drawn evenly between the two ends. requiresAnyMod list of text none The entry only counts if at least one of these mods (packageId) is active. stuff <br Material text none Material of the thing. On a variant, empty keeps the material of the entry. Empty or unfitting material falls back to the default material. table text none A nested loot table, rolled tableRolls times. It wins over category and thing. tableRolls IntRange 1 How often the nested table is rolled, as a range. thing text none The thing (ThingDef defName) this entry stands for: placed in the cell, given as loot, or placed as a building. value FloatRange 0~0 Category entries only: market value range a thing must fall in. 0~0 means no limit. weight number 1 How likely this is picked over its siblings. Belongs on the settlement, not on the piece. weightOverDepth SimpleCurve none Curve that replaces weight by depth. Settlements have no depth, so only the value at 0 counts. weightOverWealth SimpleCurve none Curve over the wealth of the player's home map. Its value multiplies weight. Back to the field reference"}, {"t": "LootMember", "u": "pages/LootMember.html", "x": "LootMember Field Type Default Meaning --- --- --- --- stays true/false false Places the building itself so the loot lies visibly on it, instead of a closed shell that looks the same. It is not swapped out once emptied. foreign true/false false The building is placed and then left alone. Loot, occupants and the empty image come from the mod that brings it; a loot table here has no effect. empty <br Empty building text none Takes the place of the container once a player has cleared it out. Only buildings of the same footprint can be chosen. lootTable text none The loot table (LootTableDef) used here. On an anchor it fills the containers. On an object list member it replaces the anchor's table. On a content zone or a settlement budget entry it fills content zones. Not used on dungeon profiles. thing text none The thing (ThingDef defName) this entry stands for: placed in the cell, given as loot, or placed as a building. Back to the field reference"}, {"t": "LootTableDef", "u": "pages/LootTableDef.html", "x": "LootTableDef XML node: <CustomBaseFramework.LootTableDef . Also takes the common fields defName , label and description . Field Type Default Meaning --- --- --- --- always list of LootEntry none Entries taken every time the table is rolled, each subject to its own chance. They do not count against rolls. emptyFallback <br Fallback text none Loot table rolled instead when this one yields nothing. entries list of LootEntry none On a loot table: the weighted pool that rolls draws from. On a district: its budget, which pieces or categories it wants and how many. maxEntriesPerResolve whole number 16 Upper limit on the items one roll of this table can give. Default 16. rollChance <br Roll chance number 1 Chance that the table draws from entries at all, 0 to 1. always is not affected. rolls IntRange 1~3 How many times entries is drawn from, as a range. Default 1~3. rollsOverDepth SimpleCurve none Curve that replaces rolls by depth. Settlements have no depth, so only the value at 0 counts. uniqueEntries true/false false Each entry can be drawn at most once per roll of the table. wdBudget true/false false With World Domination 2.0 active, containers with this table are filled from the World Domination loot budget of the settlement instead of the table. Back to the field reference"}, {"t": "MapStepsDef", "u": "pages/MapStepsDef.html", "x": "MapStepsDef XML node: <CustomBaseFramework.MapStepsDef . Also takes the common fields defName , label and description . Field Type Default Meaning --- --- --- --- fromTechLevel list of Techschritt none Map generation steps that only run on CBF maps when the map faction reaches the given tech level. Only read from the def named CBF MapSteps. suppress list of text none Map generation steps skipped on CBF maps. If CBF builds nothing there, they are run afterwards. Only read from the def named CBF MapSteps. Back to the field reference"}, {"t": "Material lists", "u": "pages/Material-Lists.html", "x": "Material lists A material list decides what the generator uses for the parts it builds itself: walls where no piece draws one, doors in the town wall, gates without a gatehouse, fences, roads, bridges, roofs and so on. Every settlement type names one material list. There is no hidden default: a settlement type without a usable material list is not built. The lists are edited in the Loot tab, part Material lists . Shipped lists List For --- --- Base Parent of the others. Base game things, with Medieval Overhaul walls where that mod is active. Medieval Overhaul Medieval Overhaul settlements. VFE Medieval 2 VFE Medieval 2 settlements. Modern Modern towns. A list can have a parent . Roles the list does not define itself are taken from the parent. Inherited roles are shown greyed; Override copies one into the list so it can be changed. Roles Each row is one role. A role can appear in several rows with different conditions. Role Used for --- --- Wall, Inner wall Walls the generator builds where no piece draws one. Door Doors in the town wall, the walkway, towers and posterns. Gate Gate passages without a gatehouse. Embrasure Embrasures in the town wall. Pillar, Window Pillars and windows of generated walls. Road, Road edge Roads, when the ward names no roads of its own. Interior floor, Fine floor, Rough floor, Dirt floor Floors the generator lays. Field soil, Crop Soil and plants of fields. Bridge Bridges over water. Roof, Thin roof Roofs. Fence, Fence gate, Pen marker Pens. Light, Furniture, Container Things for roles a piece asks for. Conduit, Conduit under roads, Conduit under water Power lines of modern towns. For each row: - Things , Stuff , Floors , Roofs : candidates in order. The first one that exists is used. Stuff can be MapStone or MapWood , the stone or wood of the map. - Applies to : pieces or categories this row is meant for, for example a palisade door only for palisade walls, palisade towers and palisade gates. - Ward : the row only applies in this ward, for example a different road for the old town, the suburb and the countryside. - Critical : if a critical role finds nothing, the whole list is unusable and marked red. Applies to and Ward only act on walls, roads and floors the generator draws itself. Inside buildings they do nothing; buildings take the material of the map as designed. When several rows of the same role fit, the most specific one wins: a row whose Applies to matches beats a row with a matching Ward , which beats a row without conditions. In the settlement type The General section of the Settlement tab has the row Material list . The row turns red when the settlement type names no list or an unusable one. The ... button opens the list. For writing material lists in XML, see Your own content mod."}, {"t": "MaterialListDef", "u": "pages/MaterialListDef.html", "x": "MaterialListDef XML node: <CustomBaseFramework.MaterialListDef . Also takes the common fields defName , label and description . Field Type Default Meaning --- --- --- --- parent MaterialListDef none The category this one inherits from. Fields left unset here come from it. roles list of MaterialRole none Which thing or terrain each building role gets. Back to the field reference"}, {"t": "MaterialRole", "u": "pages/MaterialRole.html", "x": "MaterialRole Field Type Default Meaning --- --- --- --- chain <br Inherits from list of text none The full chain, from the nearest parent outwards. criticality Criticality (Optional, Critical) Optional Critical makes the whole material list unusable when this role finds nothing. Optional, the default, just leaves the role empty. forPieces list of text none Pieces or categories this role entry is meant for, for example palisade walls and palisade towers. A match beats a ward match. Only for walls, roads and floors the generator draws itself, not inside buildings; buildings take the material of the map as designed. pawnChain list of text none Pawn kinds tried in order for this role. The first one that exists is used. role text none Name of a building role, such as wall, floor or door. CBF puts the matching thing from the material list there when no thing or terrain is set. roofChain list of text none Roofs tried in order for this role. The first one that exists is used. district text none Ward this role entry is meant for: Town, Suburb or Rural. Empty applies everywhere. Only for walls, roads and floors the generator draws itself, not inside buildings; buildings take the material of the map as designed. stuffChain list of text none Materials tried in order for things made from stuff. MapStone and MapWood take the stone or wood of the map. Without a match the default material is used. tagSearch text none Used when no chain entry exists. AnyWall, AnyDoor, AnyContainer and AnyLight find a thing, AnyHardFloor and AnySoil a terrain. terrainChain list of text none Terrains tried in order for this role. The first one that exists is used. terrainMix list of TerrainWeight none Weighted list of terrains mixed for this role. It wins over terrainChain; if none of them exists, the chains are used. Back to the field reference"}, {"t": "Minending", "u": "pages/Minending.html", "x": "Minending Field Type Default Meaning --- --- --- --- count IntRange 1 How many of these the settlement wants, as a range. contents ThingDef none Items put into or next to the thing, for example ore in a cart. list LootBuildingCategoryDef none Object list (LootBuildingCategoryDef) the thing is drawn from, instead of one fixed thing. amount IntRange 0 How many of the items in contents. location text Chamber Where the thing stands: Chamber, Tunnel or Entrance. stuff <br Material ThingDef none Material of the thing. On a variant, empty keeps the material of the entry. Empty or unfitting material falls back to the default material. thing ThingDef none The thing (ThingDef defName) this entry stands for: placed in the cell, given as loot, or placed as a building. Back to the field reference"}, {"t": "Minenspec", "u": "pages/Minenspec.html", "x": "Minenspec Field Type Default Meaning --- --- --- --- vein ThingDef none Ore rock (a mineable ThingDef) set into the rock along the mine. veinChance number 0 Share of rock cells along the mine that become ore, 0 to 1. width IntRange 1~2 Width of the tunnel in cells. chunks IntRange 0 Number of rock chunks left in the mine. count IntRange 1 How many of these the settlement wants, as a range. things list of Minending none Thing replacements for this faction. Each entry swaps the thing in from for the thing in to. levels IntRange 1 How deep the tunnels branch: 0 only the main tunnel, 1 side tunnels off it, 2 side tunnels off those. chamberSize IntRange 4~6 Edge length of the chambers in cells. chambers IntRange 2 Number of chambers along the tunnel. length IntRange 15~20 Length of the tunnel in cells. name text Mine Name of the entry. On a district it links fields and pens to its houses and must be unique. On a pen it appears in messages. On a ward it is replaced by the ward kind, on a field it is not read, and in wall lists piece is written instead. support ThingDef none Thing set as a support along the tunnels, for example a wooden pillar. Empty sets none. supportSpacing IntRange 5~8 Distance in cells between two supports along a tunnel. tags <br Categories list of PieceCategoryDef none Categories of the houses this field is laid around. Fields go to houses of these categories first. Empty on a district field takes the categories of its budget. Back to the field reference"}, {"t": "Mod settings", "u": "pages/Mod-Settings.html", "x": "Mod settings Options Mod settings Custom Base Framework. Editor - Editor in the architect menu shows the editor as its own category in the architect menu. Without it the editor opens only in development mode, debug menu entry Open editor . - Open editor guide opens this guide. In the editor, Guide at the bottom of the left bar and in the title bar does the same. Settlements per faction Lists every faction that gets its bases from Custom Base Framework, from any mod. Choose factions shows all of them in a window of their own: - Search by faction or mod name. - Sorted by mod or Sorted by name changes the order. - All on and All off switch every faction at once. Untick a faction to give it RimWorld's own bases again. Bases that were already visited stay as they are. Building ground Reserve building ground before the rock : before RimWorld places the rock, the edge of the mountain recedes where the settlement will stand, only as far as it needs to fit. Off means the settlement has to make do with the terrain as grown and can fail on rocky tiles. Other settlement mods - Leave settlements of other settlement mods alone , on by default. Settlements that another mod builds with its own map generator keep it. Vassal colonies of Empire get no CBF settlements and no CBF quests. - Build World Domination settlements with CBF , on by default, only shown with World Domination active. CBF replaces the World Domination layouts and picks the settlement type by tier. Off: CBF leaves them alone. - Loot in World Domination settlements , a slider below it, 50% by default. 100% keeps the planned CBF or Medieval Overhaul loot in every container and switches World Domination loot off. 0% gives the general containers World Domination's loot budget, as World Domination would. In between, that share of the containers keeps its planned loot, the rest shares the full World Domination budget and each gets at least one item. There is never less loot than at 0%. See Compatibility. - Build A Rim of Ice and Fire holdings with CBF , on by default, only shown with A Rim of Ice and Fire active. CBF builds holdings that have a matching settlement type. Off: A Rim of Ice and Fire builds its holdings itself. See Compatibility. Make everything buildable Gives every building without an architect category one, so it can be placed from the architect menu instead of crafted and installed. Things without build cost are skipped. Takes effect after a restart. Open build orders for these buildings are cancelled when you switch it off or remove CBF, and the materials are returned. Error report If a settlement cannot be generated, CBF writes a report with the terrain and the log, and a vanilla settlement is placed instead. A window opens and offers to upload the report. - Upload latest error report in the settings does the same later. - Uploaded are two files: Bericht.txt (mod list, settlement type, seed, log excerpt) and Gelaende.gel (the terrain of the map). No save games. - The link is public. The files are deleted automatically after 30 days. - Copy links copies the links together with a short summary of the error. Post them in a comment on the Steam Workshop page. Remove CBF from this save Remove CBF data from this save is available in a loaded game. It removes CBF quests, map data, containers and settlement plans. Settlements remain, containers are emptied onto the ground. It cannot be undone. Afterwards save at once, quit the game and only then remove the mod. As long as CBF stays active, the world map and new maps bring CBF data back."}, {"t": "Loot in XML", "u": "pages/Modding-Loot.html", "x": "Modding: loot, object lists, containers and crops This page covers the XML side of loot in Custom Base Framework (CBF). The editor side is described on Loot. Every field is listed on the field reference pages linked at the end. How loot gets into a settlement Loot always starts at an anchor in a piece. 1. A piece has a legend entry with anchorKind Fill or Object . 2. The anchor names an object list in objects and, for fill anchors, a loot table in lootTable . 3. CBF picks one structure from the object list that fits the anchor area and places it. 4. The loot table is rolled and the result goes into that structure or onto it. A real anchor from a Medieval Overhaul piece: xml <li <symbol 4</symbol <anchor Barrel</anchor <anchorKind Fill</anchorKind <objects CBF MO Barrel</objects <lootTable CBF MO Loot Larder</lootTable </li <li <symbol 5</symbol <anchor Shelf</anchor <anchorKind Fill</anchorKind <objects CBF MO HeraldryShelf</objects <lootForeign true</lootForeign </li Node Meaning --- --- anchorKind Fill places a structure and loot. Object places a structure only, the anchor's lootTable is ignored. objects defName of a LootBuildingCategoryDef . lootTable defName of a LootTableDef . Used for every structure of the list unless a member brings its own table. lootForeign The anchor places the structure and leaves filling to the mod that owns it. The anchor's lootTable is ignored. chance Chance that the anchor is used at all. If it fails, the anchor cells are cleared, unless onTop is set. onTop The structure goes on top of what is already there. The cells are not cleared. A fill anchor with neither object list nor loot table stays empty and logs a warning. A fill anchor with a loot table but no object list drops the loot loose on the floor near the anchor. Pieces can also have content zones ( contentZones , see ContentZoneSpec). They use the same object lists and loot tables. A zone without its own lootTable falls back to the lootTable of the settlement's BudgetEntry for that category. Object lists Node: <CustomBaseFramework.LootBuildingCategoryDef . An object list names the structures an anchor may place. xml <CustomBaseFramework.LootBuildingCategoryDef MayRequire=\"DankPyon.Medieval.Overhaul\" <defName CBF MO Barrel</defName <members <li DankPyon HideoutRusticBarrelClosed</li <li DankPyon HideoutCrate1x1c</li </members </CustomBaseFramework.LootBuildingCategoryDef A member can be a plain defName, as above, or a block with more nodes: xml <CustomBaseFramework.LootBuildingCategoryDef MayRequire=\"DankPyon.Medieval.Overhaul\" <defName CBF MO WeaponRack</defName <members <li <thing DankPyon HideoutRackSword</thing <foreign true</foreign </li <li <thing DankPyon HideoutRackSpear</thing <foreign true</foreign </li <li <thing DankPyon HideoutRackMixed</thing <foreign true</foreign </li </members </CustomBaseFramework.LootBuildingCategoryDef Member nodes Node Meaning --- --- thing defName of the structure. lootTable A loot table for this member only. It replaces the anchor's table. If the table does not exist, the anchor's table is used and a warning is logged. empty What it becomes once emptied. On a shelf or other open structure, this building takes its place. On a shell (see below), only its picture is used. Must have the same footprint as thing , otherwise a warning is logged and the swap does not happen. stays The structure itself is placed and stays as it is. Loot lies visibly on it or goes into it. offen is read as the same node. foreign CBF places the structure and does nothing else. Loot, occupants and the empty look come from the mod that owns it. A shipped example of stays : xml <CustomBaseFramework.LootBuildingCategoryDef MayRequire=\"DankPyon.Medieval.Overhaul\" <defName CBF MO WritingSet</defName <members <li <thing DankPyon OnTop Scribe Book</thing <stays true</stays </li <li <thing DankPyon OnTop Scribe ScrollAndInkwell</thing <stays true</stays </li <li <thing DankPyon OnTop Scribe StackPaper</thing <stays true</stays </li </members </CustomBaseFramework.LootBuildingCategoryDef What gets placed What CBF places depends on the kind of building and the member flags. - CBF container ( thingClass is CustomBaseFramework.Building LootContainer ): placed as is. It gets the loot table and rolls it when it spawns. - Other container (any Building Casket , for example a Medieval Overhaul lootable chest) without stays or foreign : CBF places a shell of the same size instead. The shell looks like the original, takes its label and description, and holds the loot inside until it is opened. If the member has empty , the shell shows that building's picture once opened. - Storage (a shelf, Building Storage ): placed as is. The loot lies on its cells, respecting the shelf's storage filter and items per cell. - Anything else , for example a table: placed as is. The loot lies on its cells. - With foreign , or on an anchor with lootForeign , none of the above applies. The structure is placed and left alone. Loot on a structure's cells takes the cell with the fewest items. A shelf allows its maxItemsInCell , anything else one stack per cell. When every cell is full, the rest of that item is not created. Items a shelf does not accept go onto free floor within 4 cells of the anchor, as does loot that does not fit into a container. What it becomes when emptied For structures that hold loot on their cells, CBF checks every 250 ticks whether the loot is gone. - With empty : the structure is replaced by the empty building, same position and rotation. - Without empty , on a shelf: the shelf stays. - Without empty , on anything else: the structure vanishes. - With stays or foreign : nothing is checked, the structure stays. - Structures owned by the player, or on a map owned by the player, are never swapped or removed. CBF containers handle this themselves, see Containers. Pawn inside An object list can hide someone in its containers. This only works for buildings with an inside (caskets and CBF containers), not on shelves. xml <CustomBaseFramework.LootBuildingCategoryDef <defName MyMod TrappedChest</defName <members <li MyMod Chest</li </members <pawnList MyMod ChestAnimals</pawnList <pawnChance 0.15</pawnChance <pawnCount 1</pawnCount <pawnHostile true</pawnHostile </CustomBaseFramework.LootBuildingCategoryDef Node Meaning --- --- pawnList defName of a PawnListDef. pawnChance Chance per container. Default 0 , so nobody is inside unless set. pawnCount How many, as a range. pawnHostile The pawn attacks once the container is opened. Animals go manhunter, people go berserk. A pawn of a faction hostile to the player fights for its faction instead. Handled by CBF containers and shells. pawnFaction FactionDef of the occupant. Without it, a non-hostile person joins the settlement's faction. In a CBF container, a hostile occupant acts as a guard. Opening the container first releases the guard. The container cannot be searched while the guard is alive and outside. Kind kind takes Fill (default) or Empty . With Empty the structures are placed without loot and without occupants, and Medieval Overhaul lootables do not fill themselves either. Object size and anchor area The anchor area is the set of cells drawn with the anchor symbol. Only members that fit into the free cells of that area can be picked. A member may be placed turned by 90 degrees if its rotatable is true. - Among the members that fit, the pick is weighted by footprint. A 2x1 shelf is twice as likely as a 1x1 crate. - At an anchor, CBF places the structure as close to the middle of the area as it fits. - Rotation and stuff come from the placeholder building drawn under the anchor, if there is one and the stuff fits. - Water cells never count as free. - If no member fits: with a loot table, the loot is dropped loose near the anchor. Without one, the drawn placeholder stays and a warning is logged. In dev mode, CBF warns when only one member of a list ever fits an area, because then the same structure always appears. Other mods Use MayRequire on the list or on a member so the XML loads without that mod. xml <CustomBaseFramework.LootBuildingCategoryDef <defName CBF Vanilla ShieldGenerator High</defName <members <li MayRequire=\"Ludeon.RimWorld.Royalty\" <thing ShieldGeneratorMortar</thing <stays true</stays </li </members </CustomBaseFramework.LootBuildingCategoryDef A member whose thing does not exist is skipped silently. If no member is left, the list is empty and every anchor that uses it is cleared and stays empty. If the list itself does not exist, the anchor stays empty and a warning is logged. Loot tables Node: <CustomBaseFramework.LootTableDef . A loot table decides what comes out. Entries can be a single item, another table or a whole thing category. A table of tables: xml <CustomBaseFramework.LootTableDef <defName CBF MO Loot Dwelling</defName <label Wohnhaus</label <rolls 2~4</rolls <uniqueEntries true</uniqueEntries <emptyFallback CBF MO Loot Minimal</emptyFallback <entries <li <weight 20</weight <table CBF MO Goods Bread</table </li <li <weight 16</weight <table CBF MO Goods Cheese</table </li <li <weight 12</weight <table CBF MO Goods Textiles</table </li <li <weight 8</weight <table CBF MO Goods Wine</table </li </entries </CustomBaseFramework.LootTableDef A table of items: xml <CustomBaseFramework.LootTableDef <defName CBF Vanilla Goods Garments</defName <label Gewand</label <rolls 1~2</rolls <entries <li <weight 20</weight <thing Apparel BasicShirt</thing <count 1~3</count <quality Awful~Good</quality <hitPoints 0.4~0.9</hitPoints <onMissing Skip</onMissing </li <li <weight 12</weight <thing Apparel Jacket</thing <count 1~2</count <quality Awful~Good</quality <hitPoints 0.4~0.9</hitPoints <onMissing Skip</onMissing </li <li MayRequire=\"Ludeon.RimWorld.Royalty\" <weight 2</weight <thing Apparel Corset</thing <count 1</count <quality Normal~Excellent</quality <onMissing Skip</onMissing </li </entries </CustomBaseFramework.LootTableDef Table nodes Node Meaning --- --- rolls How many entries are drawn from entries . Default 1~3 . rollChance Chance that the draw happens at all. One check for the whole draw, not per roll. If it fails, only always entries come. Default 1 . uniqueEntries Each entry can be drawn at most once per draw. Default false . maxEntriesPerResolve Drawing stops once this many picks have been made. Default 16 . always Entries that are taken every time, each after its own chance check. They do not use a draw. entries Entries drawn by weight . emptyFallback Another table, rolled if this one produced nothing. It may not point to itself. Nested tables and fallbacks go at most 4 levels deep. Deeper levels produce nothing. Entry kinds Each entry sets exactly one of thing , table or category . An entry in entries with none of them is a config error. If more than one is set, table wins over category , and category wins over thing . Kind Node What happens --- --- --- Item thing That ThingDef. Table table The named table is rolled tableRolls times (default 1 ) and everything it gives is added. Category category A ThingCategoryDef. One thing from the category and its subcategories is picked, weighted by the thing's generateCommonality . A category entry, written for this page: xml <li <weight 4</weight <category Drugs</category <maxTechLevel Medieval</maxTechLevel <value 5~60</value <count 1~3</count </li For categories, only things the game allows in random item sets are used. maxTechLevel drops things above that tech level. value drops things whose base market value lies outside the range; the default 0~0 means no limit. If nothing is left, the entry gives nothing. Things made from stuff get a random fitting stuff. Entry nodes Node Meaning --- --- weight Weight against the other entries in entries . Default 1 . An entry with weight 0 is never drawn. chance Only used in always : the chance that the entry is taken. Default 1 . count Stack count as a range. Counts above the stack limit are split into several stacks. quality Quality range, for example Poor~Good . Picked evenly within the range. Only for things that have quality. Default Awful~Legendary . hitPoints Condition as a fraction of max hit points, for example 0.5~1.0 . Default 1~1 . stuff Stuff for a thing made from stuff. If it is missing or not a stuff, the thing's default stuff is used. maxTechLevel Category entries only, see above. value Category entries only, see above. tableRolls Table entries only, see above. weightOverWealth A curve over the wealth of the player's home map. The weight is multiplied by it. requiresAnyMod A list of packageIds. The entry is only used if at least one of them is active. onMissing What happens if thing does not exist: Skip , Fallback or Silver . Default Skip . fallbackThings Used with onMissing Fallback . The first one that exists replaces thing . The fields depthRange , weightOverDepth , countOverDepth and rollsOverDepth read a depth. Settlements always have depth 0. An entry with depthRange starting above 0 never appears, and CBF logs a warning for it. When a mod is missing There are three ways to handle things from other mods. 1. MayRequire on the li . The entry is not loaded at all. This is what the shipped tables use. 2. requiresAnyMod . The entry loads but is ignored unless one of the mods is active. 3. onMissing for a thing that does not exist: - Skip : the entry is dropped and a warning is logged once. - Fallback : the first existing defName from fallbackThings is used. If none exists, the entry is dropped. - Silver : silver is used instead, with the entry's count . xml <li <weight 6</weight <thing MyMod Candle</thing <count 2~5</count <onMissing Fallback</onMissing <fallbackThings <li DankPyon Candle</li <li Candle</li </fallbackThings </li A table or category entry that points to something that does not exist is dropped with a warning. If no entry of a table is usable, the table stays empty and CBF logs it. An anchor that names an empty table places its structure without loot. Containers A CBF container is a building that holds loot inside, looks different once searched, and can turn into another building or vanish. Use it when a plain shelf is not enough. CBF ships an abstract base, CBF BeutebehaelterBase , in Defs/CustomBaseFramework/ThingDefs/CBF Beutebehaelter.xml . It sets thingClass to CustomBaseFramework.Building LootContainer , the contents tab and the usual building values. Inherit from it and add a size, a graphic and the extension. xml <ThingDef ParentName=\"CBF BeutebehaelterBase\" <defName CBF Container Shelf</defName <label stocked shelf</label <description A shelf someone left behind, still holding what its owner kept there. Once emptied, it is just a shelf again.</description <size (2,1)</size <graphicData <texPath Things/Building/Furniture/Shelf</texPath <graphicClass Graphic Multi</graphicClass <drawSize (3,2)</drawSize </graphicData <modExtensions <li Class=\"CustomBaseFramework.LootContainerExtension\" <emptyBuilding Shelf</emptyBuilding </li </modExtensions </ThingDef Inheritance only works from a mod that loads after CBF. Otherwise copy the base into your own XML. Extension nodes CustomBaseFramework.LootContainerExtension : Node Meaning --- --- emptyGraphic A GraphicData shown once the container has been searched. It stays the same container. emptyBuilding A ThingDef that replaces the container once searched. Needs the same footprint, otherwise the container stays and a warning is logged. destroyWhenEmpty The container vanishes once searched, leaving nothing behind. searchSound A SoundDef played when it is opened. searchEffect A FleckDef shown when it is opened. effectSize Size of that fleck. Default 1.5 . emptyBuilding and destroyWhenEmpty exclude each other; setting both is a config error. Empty picture only: xml <modExtensions <li Class=\"CustomBaseFramework.LootContainerExtension\" <emptyGraphic <texPath Things/Building/Ruin/RuinedShelf</texPath <graphicClass Graphic Multi</graphicClass <drawSize (2.75,2.75)</drawSize </emptyGraphic </li </modExtensions How it behaves - The picture changes when the container is opened, not when it runs out of items. - The loot table comes from the anchor or the member, never from the container def. A container placed by hand stays empty. In dev mode with god mode, a gizmo lets you fill it from any table for testing. - The contents are rolled once, when the container spawns. - A shell takes its empty picture from the member's empty building, or else from an empty graphic declared in the original building's own mod extension. A shell never turns into another building. - A container of your own takes its empty picture from emptyGraphic . Shelves with visible loot To show loot lying on a vanilla or modded shelf, do not use a CBF container. Put the shelf in an object list. CBF places the shelf itself and lays the items on its cells. When the items are gone, the shelf stays, or turns into the member's empty building. xml <CustomBaseFramework.LootBuildingCategoryDef <defName CBF VFEM2 Shelf</defName <members <li Shelf</li </members </CustomBaseFramework.LootBuildingCategoryDef A mod's own self-filling shelf is marked foreign , so CBF does not touch its contents: xml <CustomBaseFramework.LootBuildingCategoryDef <defName CBF VFEM2 AutoShelf</defName <members <li <thing VFEM2 AutofillShelf</thing <foreign true</foreign </li </members </CustomBaseFramework.LootBuildingCategoryDef Medieval Overhaul lootables Medieval Overhaul lootables fill themselves when they spawn. CBF blocks that while it places one with a CBF loot table, and on lists of kind Empty . With foreign , or on an anchor with lootForeign , the lootable keeps its own loot. Crop lists Node: <CustomBaseFramework.CropListDef . A crop list is a weighted list of plants for fields and pens. CBF ships none, so this example is written for this page. xml <CustomBaseFramework.CropListDef <defName MyMod Crops Grain</defName <label grain</label <plants <li <plant Plant Rice</plant <weight 3</weight </li <li <plant Plant Corn</plant <weight 2</weight </li <li <plant Plant Potato</plant </li </plants </CustomBaseFramework.CropListDef Node Meaning --- --- plant defName of a plant. Entries that do not exist or are not plants are skipped with a warning. weight Weight against the other plants. Default 1 . A weight of 0 or less is a config error and the entry is skipped. A list without plants is a config error. A field or pen names the list in crops . Fields sit in fields of the settlement or of a district, pens in pens . xml <fields <li <name Acker</name <count 1~2</count <crops MyMod Crops Grain</crops <plants <li Plant Rice</li </plants </li </fields If crops names a list that exists and has at least one usable plant, the list is used and plants is ignored. If the list does not exist or has no usable plant, the field falls back to plants , and each of them is equally likely. Tips - Build small tables of goods and combine them in larger tables with table entries. The shipped tables work this way. - Give large tables an emptyFallback , so a structure is never empty just because a roll failed. - Use MayRequire on every entry and member from another mod. A list whose members are all missing clears its anchors. - Make anchor areas the size of the structures you want. If only one member fits, the same structure always appears. - Draw a placeholder building under each anchor. It sets the rotation and stuff of what is placed. - Mark a mod's own lootables foreign if their author's loot should stay. Leave the flag off to use your own table. - Use the Sample button in the Loot tab to roll a table before testing in game. Field reference - LootTableDef - LootEntry - LootBuildingCategoryDef - LootMember - CropListDef - CropEintrag - ContentZoneSpec - Ackerspec - Gehegespec"}, {"t": "Your own content mod", "u": "pages/Modding-Overview.html", "x": "Modding overview This page is for modders who write XML for Custom Base Framework (CBF) by hand or want to know what the editor writes. It covers the folder layout of a content mod, how the defs connect, and the def types that have no page of their own in the guide: wall profiles, styles, ruins, map steps, buildability rules, help texts and dungeon profiles. The last sections explain how CBF takes over faction bases and draws world icons. Every field is listed in the Field reference. This page shows how the fields are used. Your own content mod A content mod for CBF is a normal RimWorld mod. It needs no C and no patches. Folder layout MyCBFContent/ About/ About.xml LoadFolders.xml 1.6/ Defs/ CustomBaseFramework/ CustomBaseDefs/ Buildings/ WallParts/ LootDefs/ PawnKinds/ PlanDefs/ WallProfileDefs/ Mods/ MedievalOverhaul/ Defs/ CustomBaseFramework/ Textures/ World/ WorldObjects/ CBF/ MyTown.png Only About/About.xml and one Defs folder are required. The subfolder names under Defs are a convention. RimWorld reads every XML file under Defs , whatever the folder is called. About.xml The mod depends on CBF and loads after it: xml <?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"utf-8\"? <ModMetaData <name My CBF Content</name <author Me</author <packageId Me.MyCBFContent</packageId <supportedVersions <li 1.6</li </supportedVersions <modDependencies <li <packageId EvilEyeGuy.CustomBaseFramework</packageId <displayName Custom Base Framework</displayName </li </modDependencies <loadAfter <li EvilEyeGuy.CustomBaseFramework</li </loadAfter <description Content for Custom Base Framework.</description </ModMetaData loadAfter matters. A def can only inherit with ParentName from a parent in a mod that loads earlier. CBF's abstract parents, such as CBF Ruin Basis , are only usable when your mod loads after CBF. LoadFolders.xml for other mods Content that needs another mod can go into a subfolder that only loads when that mod is active. CBF does this for Medieval Overhaul and Vanilla Factions Expanded - Medieval 2: xml <?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"utf-8\"? <loadFolders <v1.6 <li /</li <li 1.6</li <li IfModActive=\"DankPyon.Medieval.Overhaul\" 1.6/Mods/MedievalOverhaul</li <li IfModActive=\"OskarPotocki.VFE.Medieval2\" 1.6/Mods/VFEMedieval2</li </v1.6 </loadFolders / loads Textures and Languages from the mod root. 1.6 loads 1.6/Defs and 1.6/Patches . Each conditional line loads its own Defs and Languages folders. MayRequire For single defs or single list entries, RimWorld's MayRequire attribute is enough. On a def node it skips the whole def when the mod is missing. On a list item it skips only that item, for example a member of an object list: xml <members <li MayRequire=\"DankPyon.Medieval.Overhaul\" <thing DankPyon RusticShelf</thing </li <li <thing Shelf</thing </li </members See Modding-Settlements for MayRequire on settlement types. World icons World map icons go into Textures/World/WorldObjects/CBF/ of your mod. A settlement type names one in worldTexture . The editor lists every texture in that folder from all active mods. See World icons below. What the editor writes for you The in-game editor handles most of this. See Getting started. - New mod creates the mod in your Mods folder with About/About.xml (dependency on CBF, loadAfter CBF) and the folders 1.6/Defs/CustomBaseFramework/CustomBaseDefs/Buildings , .../WallParts , .../DungeonRooms , 1.6/Defs/CustomBaseFramework/Layouts , 1.6/Defs/CustomBaseFramework/LootDefs and 1.6/Patches . - When you save content that uses other mods, the editor adds those mods to About.xml. - When it saves a settlement type, it writes MayRequire on the def node for the mods the type needs. - If your mod already has a LoadFolders.xml with IfModActive lines, a new settlement type that needs one of those mods is saved into that mod's folder. The editor does not write LoadFolders.xml and does not copy world icon images. Add those by hand. How the defs connect SettlementPlanDef (settlement type) worldTexture ............ world icon styles .................. StyleDef - WallProfileDef techLevels, factionTags . which factions use it wards (Stadtteil) ....... rings from the middle outwards walls, gatehouses ..... wall pieces for this ring districts ............. building programme of the ring categories .......... PieceCategoryDef pieces ............ PieceDef anchors ......... object lists, LootTableDef, PawnListDef - Settlement types, wards and districts: Modding-Settlements - Pieces, categories and anchors: Modding-Pieces - Loot tables and loot containers: Modding-Loot - Pawn lists, garrisons and unit swap: Modding-Pawns - Quest sites and ruins in quests: Modding-Quests The rest of this page covers the defs on the side of that tree. Wall profiles A WallProfileDef describes a wall ring as a cross section. Each segment is a piece one cell wide or one cell high. The generator builds the wall line from these segments. A profile can also name a tower and a gatehouse piece. Example From CBF's Medieval Overhaul folder, labels translated: xml <CustomBaseFramework.WallProfileDef <defName WallProfile Stone 3</defName <label stone wall, drawn</label <style Standard</style <depth 3</depth <segments <li TownWall</li <li TownWall</li <li TownWall</li <li TownWall Embrasure</li </segments <tower Tower</tower <gatehouse CBF MO GateHouse Small</gatehouse <gatehouseOutward West</gatehouseOutward </CustomBaseFramework.WallProfileDef <CustomBaseFramework.WallProfileDef <defName WallProfile Palisade 1</defName <label palisade, drawn</label <style Rustical</style <depth 1</depth <segments <li Palisade</li </segments <tower PalisadeTower</tower </CustomBaseFramework.WallProfileDef Nodes Node Meaning --- --- style The style this profile belongs to. Used to find a profile for a settlement type. depth Thickness of the wall ring in cells. Must be 1 , 3 or 5 . Default 3 . segments PieceDef names of the cross sections. Each piece must be 1 cell wide or 1 cell high, otherwise it is skipped with a warning. A segment shorter than depth leaves the missing columns empty. tower PieceDef for towers on the wall. It sits on the centre line of the wall, so it must be square with an odd size. Otherwise it is ignored and the generator builds its own round tower. gatehouse PieceDef placed with its centre on each gate. Both sides must be odd. Otherwise it is ignored and a plain gate is built. gatehouseOutward The side of the drawn gatehouse piece that faces out of the settlement: North , East , South or West . Default South . outwardIsLow Default orientation of segments. true means the south end of a vertical strip, or the west end of a horizontal strip, faces outwards. A segment piece that defines its outer side itself overrides this. Default true . usable Set false to switch the profile off. CBF also sets it to false when no segment is valid. A profile needs at least one segment, otherwise the def reports a config error. How a profile is chosen 1. The settlement type's styles are tried in order. For each style its fallback chain is walked. The first usable profile with that style and the wanted depth wins. 2. If none matches, the first usable profile with the wanted depth is taken. A warning is logged, and the material then comes from that profile's style. 3. If no profile has that depth, the one with the nearest depth is taken. Standard wall fills the remaining columns. A settlement type or a ward can also list its own wall pieces in walls . These replace the profile's segments. Tower, gatehouse and orientation still come from the chosen profile. See Modding-Settlements. Styles StyleDef A style is only a name with an optional fallback: xml <CustomBaseFramework.StyleDef <defName Standard</defName <label standard</label </CustomBaseFramework.StyleDef <CustomBaseFramework.StyleDef <defName Rustical</defName <label rustic</label <fallback Standard</fallback </CustomBaseFramework.StyleDef fallback is used when nothing is found for this style. The chain may not loop and may not be deeper than 16 steps. What a style decides today A style only picks the wall profile of a settlement type. It does not restrict which pieces are built, and it has nothing to do with materials. Which factions use a settlement type is decided by factionTags and techLevels on the SettlementPlanDef, not by the style. See How CBF takes over faction bases. A settlement type lists its styles in order: xml <styles <li Rustical</li <li Standard</li </styles The older single field style still loads. If styles is empty, style is used as its only entry. Material lists A MaterialListDef decides what the generator uses for the parts it builds itself: walls, doors, gates, fences, roads, bridges, roofs and so on. Every settlement type must name one in materialList . There is no hidden default: without a usable list the settlement type is not built. The editor has its own part for them, see Material lists. Trimmed from the shipped base list: xml <CustomBaseFramework.MaterialListDef <defName CBF MaterialList Base</defName <label base</label <roles <li <role WALL</role <criticality Critical</criticality <chain <li MayRequire=\"DankPyon.Medieval.Overhaul\" DankPyon CastleWall</li <li Wall</li </chain <stuffChain <li MapStone</li <li BlocksGranite</li <li WoodLog</li </stuffChain <tagSearch AnyWall</tagSearch </li <li <role BRIDGE</role <terrainChain <li Bridge</li </terrainChain </li </roles </CustomBaseFramework.MaterialListDef A list for your own mod usually inherits from the base list and only overrides what differs. This example is not shipped: xml <CustomBaseFramework.MaterialListDef <defName MyMod Materials</defName <label my mod</label <parent CBF MaterialList Base</parent <roles <li <role DOOR</role <forPieces <li Wall Pallisade</li <li CBF MO PalisadeTower A</li </forPieces <chain <li DankPyon RusticDoor</li </chain <stuffChain <li WoodLog</li </stuffChain </li <li <role FLOOR ROAD</role <district Rural</district <terrainChain <li PackedDirt</li </terrainChain </li </roles </CustomBaseFramework.MaterialListDef Node What it does --- --- parent Another material list. Roles not defined here are taken from the parent. roles One entry per role and condition. role The role name, such as WALL , DOOR , GATE , FENCE , FLOOR ROAD , BRIDGE , ROOF . criticality Critical makes the whole list unusable when this role finds nothing. Default Optional . forPieces Pieces or categories this entry is meant for. district The ward this entry is meant for: Town , Castle , Suburb or Rural . chain Things in order; the first that exists is used. stuffChain Stuff in order. MapStone and MapWood take the stone or wood of the map. terrainChain , terrainMix Floors in order, or a weighted mix. roofChain Roofs in order. pawnChain Pawn kinds in order, for roles that place pawns. tagSearch Last resort: a thing with this building tag. When several entries of the same role fit a cell, the most specific wins: a forPieces match beats a district match, which beats an entry without conditions. The cell's ward and the wall, tower and gatehouse pieces of that ward decide what matches. Ruins A RuinDef turns a generated settlement into a raided or destroyed one: breaches, burnt rooms, corpses, reduced loot. CBF uses ruins for quest sites. A ruin reaches a map either from a quest node or from a CustomBaseFramework.CBFSiteExtension on a SitePartDef. See Modding-Quests. CBF's shipped ruin inherits from the abstract CBF Ruin Basis , which only sets occupation to Occupier . Some fields are left out here: xml <CustomBaseFramework.RuinDef ParentName=\"CBF Ruin Basis\" <defName CBF Ruin Raided</defName <label raided</label <wallDemolition 0.15</wallDemolition <clustering 0.7</clustering <breaches 1~3</breaches <gateDestroyed 0.5</gateDestroyed <buildingDamage 0.1~0.5</buildingDamage <fires 0~3</fires <lootFactor 0.4</lootFactor <lootWithOccupiers 0.5</lootWithOccupiers <residentCorpses 4~10</residentCorpses <attackerCorpses 2~6</attackerCorpses <prisoners 0~3</prisoners </CustomBaseFramework.RuinDef Node Meaning --- --- attackSide Side the attack came from. Random if not set, unless the quest sets one. clustering How strongly the damage gathers towards the attack side. 0 spreads it evenly. Default 0.6 . breaches Number of breaches in the wall. gateDestroyed Chance per gate to be destroyed. wallDemolition Chance scale for torn down walls. buildingDamage Share of hit points buildings lose. turretsDestroyed Chance scale for destroyed turrets. burnMarks Chance scale for burnt out rooms. fires Number of fires still burning. rubble Chance scale for rubble. fieldsRavaged Chance per crop plant to be destroyed. livestockGone Chance per animal to be gone. lootFactor Multiplier on the loot left in the settlement. Default 1 . lootWithOccupiers Share of the loot moved to the occupiers. residentCorpses , attackerCorpses Corpses of residents and attackers. rotDays Age of the corpses in days. prisoners Residents held prisoner by the occupiers. occupation Who holds the site: None , Builder (the builders) or Occupier (occupiers). Default Occupier . Prisoners only exist with Occupier . occupationChance Multiplier on the number of defenders. Default 1 . Map steps MapStepsDef lists vanilla or mod map generation steps that CBF skips on maps it builds itself. Only the def named CBF MapSteps is read. To add steps, patch that def instead of writing a new one. xml <CustomBaseFramework.MapStepsDef <defName CBF MapSteps</defName <suppress <li Roads</li <li ScatterRuinsSimple</li <li AncientTurret</li <li DankPyon BattlefieldRemains</li </suppress <fromTechLevel <li <step SettlementPower</step <techLevel Industrial</techLevel </li </fromTechLevel </CustomBaseFramework.MapStepsDef Node Meaning --- --- suppress GenStepDef names skipped on CBF maps. fromTechLevel Steps that only run when the map's faction has at least this tech level. Each entry has step (GenStepDef name) and techLevel (default Industrial ). The filter only applies to maps whose generator contains a CBF step. If CBF then builds nothing on the map, the skipped steps are run afterwards. A patch from your own mod: xml <Patch <Operation Class=\"PatchOperationAdd\" <xpath /Defs/CustomBaseFramework.MapStepsDef[defName=\"CBF MapSteps\"]/unterdruecken</xpath <value <li MyMod ScatterCamps</li </value </Operation </Patch Buildability rules BuildableRuleDef is only read when the mod setting Make everything buildable is on. See Mod settings. The setting gives every building without an architect category one. The rules decide which category. xml <CustomBaseFramework.BuildableRuleDef <defName CBF Buildable Ruins</defName <rank 30</rank <category CBF RuinFurniture</category <categoryLabel Ruin Furn.</categoryLabel <order -102</order <fileContains <li Buildings Ruins</li <li Buildings Ruined</li </fileContains </CustomBaseFramework.BuildableRuleDef Node Meaning --- --- fileContains Parts of the XML file name the building was defined in. Case is ignored. category DesignationCategoryDef to put matching buildings in. Created if it does not exist. categoryLabel Label of the category when CBF creates it. order Order of the created category in the architect menu. rank Rules are checked by rank , lowest first. The first match wins. Buildings that match no rule go into the category CBF Baubar (label \"CBF extra\"). Buildings without build cost are skipped. Help texts HelpDef holds the texts behind the help buttons in the editor. Each button asks for a fixed defName, for example CBF Help Wards . xml <CustomBaseFramework.HelpDef <defName CBF Help Walls</defName <label Walls</label <description The wall pieces this ward is built from.</description </CustomBaseFramework.HelpDef Node Meaning --- --- label , description Title and text of the help window. images Texture paths of images shown in the window. imageWidth Maximum image width. 0 uses the window width. A content mod does not need these. New HilfeDefs get no button. Translations go through DefInjected as usual. Dungeon profiles DungeonProfileDef exists in the code, but CBF ships none and nothing assigns one to a world object at the moment. Its fields are listed in DungeonProfileDef. Do not build content on it yet. How CBF takes over faction bases No patch is needed to make CBF build bases for a faction. CBF patches RimWorld so that a non-player faction settlement uses CBF's map generator CBF Base Faction when: - at least one usable settlement type matches the faction, or a type was assigned to this settlement by hand in the editor, - the faction is not switched off in the mod settings, - the settlement's WorldObjectDef has no map generator of its own, - the settlement is not in space, - no other mod's generator is known to take over this faction. CBF adds its world object comp to the vanilla Settlement and Site defs itself. Faction matching For each faction, CBF collects the settlement types in two groups: 1. Own types. factionTags contains the faction's defName or its categoryTag . 2. General types. forAllFactions is true and techLevels contains the faction's tech level. Types that list the faction in excludeFactions are skipped. If the faction has any own types, only those are used. General types are only used for factions without own types. CBF's modern town, for example, sets forAllFactions and the tech levels Industrial , Spacer and Ultra , so every such faction without own types gets it. A faction with no matching type keeps its vanilla base. Details of the plan fields are on Modding-Settlements. World icons A settlement type sets its world map icon with worldTexture : xml <worldTexture World/WorldObjects/CBF/CBF ModernTown</worldTexture - The path is relative to Textures and has no file extension. - The icon is tinted in the faction colour. - It replaces the faction icon only for settlements CBF builds, and only if the faction is not switched off. - If the texture is missing, the faction icon stays and a warning is logged. CBF ships its icons in Textures/World/WorldObjects/CBF , for example CBF City , CBF MarketTown and CBF Metropolis . Your own icons in the same folder of your mod show up in the editor's picker next to them. Tips - Load after CBF. Without it ParentName on CBF's abstract defs fails. - Draw a wall profile for every depth your settlement types use. The fallback to another depth works but logs a warning. - Towers need a square, odd size. Gatehouses need odd width and height. - Put MayRequire on every def or entry that names content from another mod, or move it into an IfModActive folder. - Check the log after loading. CBF reports broken references once per def and says what it does instead. - The full list of fields for every def type is in the Field reference."}, {"t": "Pawns in XML", "u": "pages/Modding-Pawns.html", "x": "Modding: pawns, garrisons and unit swap This page shows how to write the people and animals of a CBF settlement in XML. For the editor tabs see Pawns, Unit swap and Settlements. Every field is listed in the Field reference. Who lives in a settlement A CBF settlement gets its pawns from four sources. - Garrison per ward. Each ward of a settlement type can name pawn lists with a count and a post. These are the soldiers of that ward. - Defender points. A ward without garrison entries takes its share of the settlement type's defenderPoints . Those pawns come from the faction's own pawn group makers. - Pawn anchors in pieces. A piece can mark cells where pawns stand: a guard in a barracks, a trader at a stall, a crew at a catapult. - Pen animals. A pen in a ward or district names a pawn list of animals. All humanlike pawns pass through unit swap before they spawn. A neutral archer in a list can therefore become the archer of the faction that owns the settlement. Pawns are only placed for settlements of a non-player faction. Pawn lists A pawn list is a weighted list of pawn kinds with a role and a radius. Garrisons, pawn anchors, pens and object lists all name pawn lists by defName . Example From LootDefs/CBF PawnLists.xml : xml <CustomBaseFramework.PawnListDef <defName CBF Vanilla Garrison</defName <label garrison</label <role Guard</role <radius 10</radius <members <li <kind CBF Modern Rifleman</kind <weight 4</weight </li <li <kind CBF Modern Stormer</kind <weight 2</weight </li <li <kind CBF Modern Sniper</kind <weight 1</weight </li <li <kind CBF Modern Grenadier</kind <weight 1</weight </li <li <kind CBF Modern Officer</kind <weight 1</weight </li <li <kind CBF Modern Spacer Rifleman</kind <weight 4</weight </li <li <kind CBF Modern Spacer Sniper</kind <weight 1</weight </li <li <kind CBF Modern Spacer Stormer</kind <weight 2</weight </li <li <kind CBF Modern Spacer Grenadier</kind <weight 1</weight </li <li <kind CBF Modern Spacer Officer</kind <weight 1</weight </li </members </CustomBaseFramework.PawnListDef A list that depends on another mod uses MayRequire , on the whole def or on single members: xml <CustomBaseFramework.PawnListDef <defName CBF Pawns GateGuard</defName <label gate guards</label <role Guard</role <radius 2</radius <members <li <kind Town Guard</kind <weight 10</weight </li <li MayRequire=\"DankPyon.Medieval.Overhaul\" <kind DankPyon Guard</kind <weight 10</weight </li </members </CustomBaseFramework.PawnListDef Nodes members holds the entries. Each li has: - kind : defName of a PawnKindDef. A kind that is not loaded is skipped silently. - weight : how often this kind is drawn compared to the others. Default 1 . A weight of 0 never draws. role decides what pawns from this list do when they come from a pawn anchor. Default Civilian . Value Editor label Behavior at a pawn anchor --- --- --- Guard Guard Joins the defense of the ward the anchor stands in. Sentry Post Holds a post around the anchor within radius . Gunner Gunner Mans artillery around the anchor within radius . Crews stay at their gun. Civilian Civilian Defends the point within radius , but may flee on its own. In a garrison the role only decides one thing: pawns of all roles except Civilian are generated capable of violence. The post of the garrison entry decides where they stand. radius : how far pawns from an anchor spread around it. Default 4 . Values below 1 count as 1 . Tech level of the settlement A settlement has a tech level. It is the tech level of the owning faction if the settlement type lists it in techLevels . Otherwise it is the highest level in techLevels . A settlement type without techLevels has no tech level. CBF works out a tech level for each pawn kind from its gear: required apparel, required implants and the weapons its weaponTags and weaponMoney allow. The highest tech level found counts. When a garrison or a pawn anchor draws from a list: 1. Members above the settlement's tech level are left out. 2. Of the rest, only the members with the highest tech level stay in the pool. 3. Members whose tech level cannot be determined always stay in the pool. 4. One kind is drawn from the pool by weight. If no member fits, the whole list is used and the log gets a warning. Without a settlement tech level the whole list is used. In the example above, an industrial settlement draws only the CBF Modern industrial soldiers. A spacer settlement draws only the CBF Modern Spacer kinds. One list therefore serves both ages. Pens and object lists draw from the whole list without this filter. techLevel on the pawn list switches the filter off. Set to a tech level such as Neolithic , the list counts as that level and garrisons and pawn anchors draw from the whole list. Use it when the gear gives the wrong level, for example tribal warriors wearing medieval leather. If techLevel is above the settlement's tech level, the list is still used and the log gets a warning. Checks - A list without members is a config error. - A negative weight or radius is a config error. - A list whose members are all missing logs a warning. Anchors that name it stay empty. - A Civilian list that contains a humanlike kind with combat power of 80 or more logs a warning. Fighters belong in Guard , Sentry or Gunner . Garrisons A garrison belongs to a ward inside wards of a SettlementPlanDef. Example From Mods/MedievalOverhaul/.../PlanDefs/CBF Plan FortressCity New.xml , shortened: xml <CustomBaseFramework.SettlementPlanDef MayRequire=\"DankPyon.Medieval.Overhaul\" <defName CBF Plan FortressCity New</defName <label fortress city (MO)</label <defenderPoints 400~700</defenderPoints <defendersOnWall 0.28</defendersOnWall <wards <li <kind Town</kind <name OldTown</name <garrison <li <list CBF MO Garrison Walkway</list <count 16~21</count <post Walkway</post </li <li <list CBF MO Garrison Gate</list <count 5~8</count <post Gate</post </li <li <list CBF MO Garrison Inside</list <count 8~13</count <post Inside</post </li </garrison </li <li <kind Rural</kind <name Countryside</name <garrison <li <list CBF MO Garrison Inside</list <count 3~5</count <post Inside</post </li </garrison </li </wards </CustomBaseFramework.SettlementPlanDef Nodes Each li in garrison has: - list : defName of a pawn list. A missing list logs a warning and the entry stays empty. - count : how many pawns, as a range. Default 1 . - post : where they stand. Default Inside . Value Editor label Where the pawns go --- --- --- Inside Inside Streets, squares and yards of the ward. They defend the ward together. Walkway Wall Wall walk cells next to an open embrasure. Each one guards its stretch of wall. Gate Gate Gate cells. Each one holds a post at the gate. If a ward has no wall walk at an open embrasure, Walkway pawns stand inside instead. The same goes for Gate without gate cells. Garrison and defender points - A ward with at least one garrison entry does not use the defender points. Its garrison and its anchor pawns defend it. - A ward without garrison entries takes a share of defenderPoints , weighted by its size. Of that share, the part defendersOnWall goes onto the wall walk, if the ward has open embrasures. - The defender pawns come from the faction's group maker named in defenderGroupKind . If that is empty or the faction has none, CBF tries Settlement , then Combat . - Pawns from anchors that name their own list or kind come on top. They are not counted against the defender points. The editor shows a point value for each garrison: the average combat power of the list, weighted, times the middle of count . This value is for orientation only. Lords Every ward defends itself. Garrison pawns on the wall and at the gate each get their own duty. All other defenders of a ward, including inside garrison, point defenders and guard pawns from anchors, share one lord for that ward. They do not gather in the center of the settlement. Pawn anchors in pieces A pawn anchor is a legend entry of a PieceDef with anchorKind set to Spawn . The anchor sits in the middle of the cells that carry its symbol. Example From a vanilla barracks piece: xml <legend <li <symbol 8</symbol <anchor Commander</anchor <anchorKind Spawn</anchorKind <pawnList CBF Vanilla Garrison</pawnList <pawnCount 1</pawnCount </li <li <symbol k</symbol <anchor SoldiersA</anchor <anchorKind Spawn</anchorKind <pawnList CBF Vanilla Garrison</pawnList <pawnCount 2~3</pawnCount </li </legend A house with residents: xml <li <symbol Y</symbol <anchor Spawn</anchor <anchorKind Spawn</anchorKind <pawnList CBF Vanilla Villager</pawnList </li Nodes - anchor : name of the anchor. Used in log messages. - anchorKind : Spawn for a pawn anchor. - pawnList : defName of a pawn list. The list's tech filter, role and radius apply. - pawns : defName of a single pawn kind. Used when pawnList is empty. These pawns are always capable of violence and join the defense of their ward. - pawnCount : how many pawns, as a range. Default 1 . A count below 1 counts as 1. - chance : chance that the anchor is used at all. Default 1 . If an anchor names neither pawnList nor pawns , it takes pawns from the settlement's defender points. It stays empty if no defender points were rolled, for example when every ward has a garrison. Such an anchor also stays empty if it lies in a room without a way outside. If pawnList names a list that does not exist and pawns is empty, the anchor stays empty and the log names it. Anchor limits Two fields of the SettlementPlanDef cap the pawns from anchors: - maxCivilians : default 30 . Once this many non-fighters are placed, further civilian anchors are skipped. - maxSoldiers : default 40 . Once this many fighters are placed, further fighting anchors are skipped. Gunner anchors are never skipped. Garrison pawns placed before count toward these limits. When a limit is active, anchors are handled in random order, gunner anchors first. 0 removes a limit. Single pawns in the legend A normal legend entry can also carry pawnKind instead of thing . It places one pawn of that kind on each cell with the symbol, owned by the settlement's faction and passed through unit swap. It gets no role, radius or lord from CBF. For defenders, a pawn anchor is the better choice. Pawns in containers An object list (LootBuildingCategoryDef) can put pawns into its containers: pawnList , pawnChance , pawnCount , pawnHostile and pawnFaction . These draw from the whole list without the tech filter. See Loot. Pen animals A pen names a pawn list in pawns . The list should hold animals. Humanlike kinds are not placed. xml <pens <li <name Pen</name <count 1~1</count <herd 3~7</herd <pawns Hardanimals 1</pawns <fence true</fence <terrain Soil</terrain <plants <li Plant Haygrass</li </plants <weight 1</weight </li </pens Each pen draws one kind from the list and places herd animals of it. Small pens get fewer: about one animal per twelve cells of pen. The animals belong to the settlement's faction. Unit swap Pawn lists and pieces name general units and things. An UnitSwapDef says what one faction uses instead. A noble house then sends its own knights and hangs its own banners, without a copy of each list and piece per faction. Example From Einheitentausch/CBF MO House Hesse.xml , shortened: xml <CustomBaseFramework.UnitSwapDef MayRequire=\"DankPyon.Medieval.Overhaul\" <defName CBF MO House Hesse</defName <label Haus Hesse</label <faction DankPyon NobleHouseSavage</faction <priority 0</priority <units <li <from DankPyon Amboisse Knight</from <to DankPyon Hesse Knight</to </li <li <from CBF MO Knight</from <to DankPyon KnightHesse</to </li <li <from CBF MO Pikeman</from <to DankPyon StandardHesse</to </li <li <from DankPyon Medieval Arbalester</from <to DankPyon Hesse Archer</to </li <li <from DankPyon Medieval Arbalester</from <to DankPyon Medieval Arbalester</to </li <li <from Villager</from <to DankPyon Hesse Peasant</to </li </units <things <li <from DankPyon BannerAmboise</from <to DankPyon BannerHesse</to </li <li <from DankPyon ShieldHeater Amboise</from <to DankPyon ShieldHeater Hesse</to </li </things </CustomBaseFramework.UnitSwapDef The two lines for DankPyon Medieval Arbalester have the same weight. Half of the arbalesters become Hesse archers, the other half stay arbalesters. Nodes - faction : defName of the FactionDef the rule is for. - priority : priority, a whole number. Default 0 . - units : swaps for pawn kinds. - things : swaps for things placed by pieces. - neutral : pawn kinds that the fallback must not replace. See below. Each li in units and things has: - from : defName of the unit or thing in the list or piece. - to : defName of what the faction uses instead. - weight : weight when several targets exist for one from . Default 1 . Entries with 0 or less are ignored. A def needs faction or neutral , otherwise it is a config error. Priority Several defs can bring rules for the same faction, also from several mods. For each from , only the entries with the highest priority count. Entries of equal priority from different defs are merged and drawn together by weight. To override the rule of another mod, give yours a higher priority. A target that is not loaded is skipped. If no target of a rule is loaded, a unit falls back as if there were no rule, and a thing stays as it is. The editor saves each rule as its own file in Defs/CustomBaseFramework/Einheitentausch of the chosen mod. Files of other mods are never changed. Without a rule When a faction has no rule for a humanlike unit, CBF picks a replacement itself: 1. The candidates are the faction's own humanlike kinds: every kind in its pawn group makers (options, guards, traders, carriers) and its basicMemberKind . 2. If the unit is already one of them, it stays. 3. Only candidates that match the unit's isFighter are used. 4. Candidates with the same way of fighting are preferred: ranged, melee or unarmed, judged by the weapons the kind can carry. 5. Of those, the one with the closest combat power wins. Animals and things are never swapped without a rule. The log notes each fallback swap once. Neutral units Units listed in neutral are not touched by the fallback. Explicit rules for them still apply. CBF uses this for its own modern soldiers, so that every faction that settles in a modern settlement keeps them: xml <CustomBaseFramework.UnitSwapDef <defName CBF Modern Neutral Units</defName <label neutral modern soldiers</label <neutral <li CBF Modern Rifleman</li <li CBF Modern Sniper</li <li CBF Modern Stormer</li <li CBF Modern Spacer Rifleman</li </neutral </CustomBaseFramework.UnitSwapDef Where swap applies - Garrison pawns. - Pawns from pawn anchors, both pawnList and pawns . - Pawns from pawnKind in a legend entry. - Things placed by pieces, for things . Defender pawns from defenderPoints already come from the faction's own group makers. Tips - Put all ages into one list and let the tech filter choose. A gear-less kind without weapons has no tech level and is drawn at every level. - Use Civilian only for people who should run. Fighters in a civilian list trigger a warning. - Give each ward of a big settlement its own garrison. A ward with a garrison is fully under your control; one without depends on the faction's group makers. - Wall posts need open embrasures. Without them the wall garrison stands inside. - Name general units in lists and pieces and write one swap rule per faction, instead of copying lists. - Put MayRequire on lists and rules for other mods, and on single members. - Techschritt is not part of unit swap. It belongs to MapStepsDef and holds back a map generation step below a tech level. Field reference - PawnListDef, PawnListEntry - Garnisonseintrag, Stadtteil, SettlementPlanDef - PieceDef, LegendEntry, Gehegespec - LootBuildingCategoryDef - UnitSwapDef, Tauscheintrag - MapStepsDef, Techschritt"}, {"t": "Pieces and categories in XML", "u": "pages/Modding-Pieces.html", "x": "Pieces and categories in XML A piece is one building or one stretch of wall. It is stored as a CustomBaseFramework.PieceDef : a set of character grids plus a legend that says what each character means. The editor writes these files for you, see Pieces. You can also write them by hand. This page shows how. Categories group pieces. They are stored as CustomBaseFramework.PieceCategoryDef and carry the rules that all their pieces share, see Categories. All field names on this page are the exact XML names. The full list of fields is on the reference pages linked at the end. A small house This is CBF Vanilla Dwelling Small A , shipped with CBF in 1.6/Defs/CustomBaseFramework/CustomBaseDefs/Freigabe Vanilla/ . It is shown unchanged except for a shorter legend. xml <Defs <CustomBaseFramework.PieceDef <defName CBF Vanilla Dwelling Small A</defName <label CBF Vanilla Dwelling Small A</label <schemaVersion 2</schemaVersion <categories <li CBF Vanilla HouseSmall</li </categories <style CBF ModernVanilla</style <eaves true</eaves <legend <li <symbol 1</symbol <anchor Kitchen</anchor <anchorKind Fill</anchorKind <objects CBF Vanilla Shelf Small</objects <lootTable CBF Vanilla Loot Kitchen</lootTable </li <li <symbol 2</symbol <anchor CabinetA</anchor <anchorKind Fill</anchorKind <objects CBF Vanilla Shelf Small</objects <lootTable CBF Vanilla Loot Dwelling</lootTable </li <li <symbol 8</symbol <anchor ResidentsA</anchor <anchorKind Spawn</anchorKind <pawnList CBF Vanilla Villager</pawnList <chance 0.3</chance </li <li <symbol B</symbol <thing HiddenConduit</thing </li <li <symbol F</symbol <thing Door</thing <stuff WoodLog</stuff </li <li <symbol Q</symbol <thing DiningChair</thing <stuff WoodLog</stuff <rotation East</rotation </li <li <symbol S</symbol <thing Wall</thing <stuff BlocksGranite</stuff </li <li <symbol Y</symbol <thing Bed</thing <stuff WoodLog</stuff <rotation South</rotation </li <li <symbol ^</symbol <roof RoofConstructed</roof </li <li <symbol a</symbol <thing ShelfSmall</thing <stuff WoodLog</stuff <rotation North</rotation </li <li <symbol h</symbol <terrain TileGranite</terrain </li <li <symbol w</symbol <terrain WoodPlankFloor</terrain </li </legend <mask <li </li <li </li <li </li <li </li <li </li <li </li <li </li <li ....</li </mask <terrain <li wwwwwwwww</li <li whhhwwwww</li <li whhhwwwww</li <li whhhwwwww</li <li wwwwwwwww</li <li wwwww....</li <li wwwww....</li <li wwwww....</li </terrain <things <li SSSSSSSSS</li <li Sg..Q..RS</li <li S...Qj.RS</li <li Sa.L....S</li <li SSFSSSFSS</li <li S..ES....</li <li SY.aS....</li <li SSSSS....</li </things <things2 <li BBBBBBBBB</li <li B.......B</li <li B.......B</li <li B.......B</li <li BBBBBBBBB</li <li B...B....</li <li B...B....</li <li BBBBB....</li </things2 <roof <li ^^^^^^^^^</li <li ^^^^^^^^^</li <li ^^^^^^^^^</li <li ^^^^^^^^^</li <li ^^^^^^^^^</li <li ^^^^^....</li <li ^^^^^....</li <li ^^^^^....</li </roof <anchors <li .........</li <li .........</li <li .........</li <li .1...8...</li <li .........</li <li .........</li <li ...2.....</li <li .........</li </anchors </CustomBaseFramework.PieceDef </Defs The shortened legend leaves out E , L , R , g and j . In the real file they are an end table, a lamp, two more chairs, a stove and a table. The grids Every grid is a list of rows. One character is one cell. - The first row is the north edge of the piece. The first character of a row is the west edge. - . and a space mean an empty cell. Nothing is done there on that layer. - Every other character is looked up in the legend. In the content grid it is looked up in contentZones instead. - All grids of a piece should have the same number of rows and the same row length. The load check reports it when they do not. - The size of the piece is the largest grid. There is no separate size field. Grid What it holds --- --- mask Which cells belong to the piece. Any character other than . counts. With clearFootprint on, only these cells are cleared before building. Without a mask, every cell that is filled on any layer counts. terrain The floor. things The load-bearing layer: walls, doors, furniture, everything a cell is built from. The generator reads this layer to find walls and doors. things2 A second pass for things that share a cell with the first layer, for example a conduit under a wall or a torch on it. things3 A third pass for the rare cell that carries three things. plants Plants. They get a random growth and are skipped on water and on cells with a building. roof Where a roof goes. anchors Anchor cells, see Anchors. anchorsOver A second anchor grid. Fill and Object anchors here place their objects on top of what already stands, without clearing the cells. content Old loot zones, resolved through contentZones . New pieces use Fill anchors instead. In the house above, the conduit B in things2 runs under the walls S of things . With a single layer the wall would need a gap for it. The legend Each <li in legend gives one character a meaning. symbol must be exactly one character. Field Effect --- --- thing A ThingDef placed on this cell. Multi-cell things are placed once, from the cell you mark. stuff The material. If it does not fit the thing, the default material is taken and a warning is logged. rotation North , East , South or West . Default North . It turns with the piece. terrain A TerrainDef for the floor layer. roof A RoofDef for the roof layer. A roof cell whose entry names no roof gets RoofConstructed . pawnKind A PawnKindDef. A pawn of the settlement's faction is spawned on this cell. role A building role such as WALL or DOOR instead of a fixed def. CBF takes the thing from the material list of the settlement type, see Roles. chance Chance from 0 to 1. On things, floors and roofs it is rolled per cell. count Stack size for a stackable thing, as a range such as 5~20 . temperature Target temperature for a cooler or heater, in degrees Celsius. variants Alternative things or floors for this character, see below. anchor and the fields after it Make the entry an anchor, see Anchors. An entry must have at least one of role , thing , terrain , pawnKind , roof or anchor . Otherwise the load check reports it. Two things change in the game, not in the file. Stone blocks as material are swapped for a stone that exists on the map tile. Floors named after a stone, such as TileGranite , are swapped the same way. A shipped example for temperature : xml <li <symbol K</symbol <thing Cooler</thing <rotation South</rotation <temperature -19</temperature </li Variants variants lets one character stand for several things or floors. Each cell picks one by weight, from the cell position and the settlement seed. xml <li <symbol w</symbol <terrain WoodPlankFloor</terrain <variants <li <terrain TileGranite</terrain <weight 0.5</weight </li <li <terrain CarpetRed</terrain <weight 0.5</weight </li </variants </li - A variant takes thing , stuff , terrain and weight . Default weight is 1. - If the entry itself names a thing or terrain, that counts as one more choice with weight 1. Here the planks win half of the cells. - A variant's stuff is only used together with its thing . - Variants apply to the terrain and the thing layers, not to plants. This example is written for illustration, it is not from a shipped piece. Piece fields Field Effect --- --- schemaVersion The file format. The current version is 2. Older files are upgraded on load. categories The categories of the piece. The first is the primary one: on a conflict its rules win, then its parents, then the next category. tag The old single category. Still read when categories is missing. Write categories . type Building (default), WallPart or DungeonRoom . Gatehouses and wall segments are WallPart . template true makes the piece a copy source for the editor. It is never placed. allowRotation Default true . With false the piece is always built as drawn. allowMirror Default true . Mirroring flips the piece east to west. clearFootprint Default true . Removes what stands on the piece's cells before building. Water cells and pawns are left alone. placement Overrides the category rules for this one piece, see below. style Old field. It does not restrict where the piece is used. eaves Places eaves from Architecture of the Rim on outer walls and doors. Does nothing without that mod. placement xml <placement <atWall Preferred</atWall </placement <placement <weight 0.3</weight </placement - atWall says how much the piece wants to touch the town wall: Forbidden , Avoid , Allowed , Preferred , StronglyPreferred or Required . Default is Allowed . When it is Allowed or missing, wallContact of the category decides. - weight says how often this piece is picked over others that fit. It falls back to the category weight, then 1. - zones , needsRoadAccess and countPerSettlement also exist here and follow the same rules as on the category. Anchors An anchor marks cells where something happens after the piece is built. An anchor is a legend entry with an anchor name. Its cells are drawn in the anchors grid with the entry's symbol. - Neighboring cells with the same symbol form one anchor area. - anchorKind is Connect , Fill , Object or Spawn . Default is Connect . - The editor shows Fill as Loot and Spawn as Pawn. - chance on an anchor is rolled once per anchor area. Connect Connect anchors tie the piece to its surroundings. The name decides what for. Name Meaning --- --- Wall The town wall joins the piece here. A gatehouse has two, one on each side where the wall runs in. A normal building with a Wall anchor merges into the wall. Outside Marks the side that faces out of town. A gatehouse needs it, otherwise the generator does not know which way to turn the gate. Annex An annex docks here, see Annexes. Water Harbor pieces only: the water side of a jetty, boat shed or fishing hut. Parts that stand over the water use bridge floor. See Settlement types in XML. required set to true only has an effect on a Connect anchor named Wall . The piece is then only placed against the town wall. A gatehouse, trimmed from CBF Vanilla Gatehouse Wood : xml <CustomBaseFramework.PieceDef <defName CBF Vanilla Gatehouse Wood</defName <schemaVersion 2</schemaVersion <categories <li WallGateHouse</li <li CBF Vanilla Gatehouse</li </categories <type WallPart</type <legend <li <symbol 7</symbol <anchor GuardSouth</anchor <anchorKind Spawn</anchorKind <pawnList CBF Vanilla Garrison</pawnList <pawnCount 1~2</pawnCount </li <li <symbol A</symbol <anchor Outside</anchor </li <li <symbol O</symbol <thing Wall</thing <stuff WoodLog</stuff </li <li <symbol W</symbol <anchor Wall</anchor </li <li <symbol g</symbol <thing Door</thing <stuff WoodLog</stuff </li </legend <things <li OOOOO</li <li O...O</li <li OOOOO</li <li .g.g.</li <li OOOOO</li <li O...O</li <li OOOOO</li </things <anchors <li ..W..</li <li .....</li <li .....</li <li A....</li <li .....</li <li ..7..</li <li ..W..</li </anchors </CustomBaseFramework.PieceDef The rows are shortened from the real 9 by 5 piece. The wall runs in at the north and south W . The gate passage is the row with the two doors g . A lies on the west side, so west is outside. The category WallGateHouse is what makes the generator treat the piece as a gatehouse. If a wall piece has no Outside anchor, the field drawnOutward ( North , East , South or West ) names the outer side instead. Fill A Fill anchor places an object from an object list and fills it from a loot table. xml <li <symbol 1</symbol <anchor Kitchen</anchor <anchorKind Fill</anchorKind <objects CBF Vanilla Shelf Small</objects <lootTable CBF Vanilla Loot Kitchen</lootTable </li - objects names a LootBuildingCategoryDef , an object list. One object that fits the area is placed. - lootTable names a LootTableDef . Without it the object stays empty, as decoration. - lootForeign set to true leaves the contents to the mod that brings the object. CBF adds no loot then. - Without an object list, a Fill anchor with a loot table puts loose loot on one of its cells. - Draw a placeholder under the anchor in the things grid, as the shelf a under anchor 1 in the house. The placed object takes the rotation and material of the placeholder. Without one, everything faces north. See Loot for object lists and loot tables. Object An Object anchor places an object from an object list with the anchor's chance , without loot. xml <li <symbol a</symbol <anchor Dishes</anchor <anchorKind Object</anchorKind <objects CBF MO Tableware</objects <chance 0.8</chance <onTop true</onTop </li onTop set to true keeps what already stands on the cells and places the object on top, here dishes on a table. Anchors in the anchorsOver grid always behave this way. Spawn A Spawn anchor marks where pawns wait when the settlement is manned. xml <li <symbol 8</symbol <anchor ResidentsA</anchor <anchorKind Spawn</anchorKind <pawnList CBF Vanilla Villager</pawnList <chance 0.3</chance </li - pawnList names a PawnListDef , see Pawns. - pawns names a single PawnKindDef instead. The editor calls it Animals. - pawnCount is the number of pawns, as a range. Default 1. Roles A legend entry can name a role instead of a fixed def. CBF takes the thing from the material list of the settlement type when the settlement is built, see Material lists. xml <li <symbol S</symbol <role WALL</role </li <li <symbol F</symbol <role DOOR</role </li <li <symbol w</symbol <role FLOOR INTERIOR</role </li - Role names used by CBF include WALL , WALL INNER , DOOR , GATE , PILLAR , WINDOW , FLOOR INTERIOR , FLOOR FINE , FLOOR ROUGH , FLOOR DIRT , ROOF , LIGHT , FURNITURE , CONTAINER and FENCE . - Cells with the role DOOR or GATE count as doors for the generator, like a thing that is a door. - Stone walls and floors take the stone of the map. So one piece drawn with roles can be built in stone in one settlement type and in wood in another. The pieces shipped with CBF draw their things directly. Things from other mods Legend entries for things from another mod carry MayRequire , as in this shipped annex for Medieval Overhaul: xml <CustomBaseFramework.PieceDef <defName CBF MO Annex Privy</defName <schemaVersion 2</schemaVersion <categories <li CBF MO Annex Rural</li </categories <placement <weight 0.3</weight </placement <legend <li <symbol +</symbol <anchor Annex</anchor <anchorKind Connect</anchorKind </li <li MayRequire=\"DankPyon.Medieval.Overhaul\" <symbol F</symbol <thing DankPyon RusticDoor</thing <stuff WoodLog</stuff </li <li MayRequire=\"DankPyon.Medieval.Overhaul\" <symbol O</symbol <thing DankPyon LogWall</thing <stuff DankPyon RawWood</stuff </li <li <symbol ^</symbol <roof RoofConstructed</roof </li <li MayRequire=\"DankPyon.Medieval.Overhaul\" <symbol b</symbol <thing DankPyon EmptyBucket</thing <stuff WoodLog</stuff </li <li MayRequire=\"DankPyon.Medieval.Overhaul\" <symbol e</symbol <terrain DankPyon EarthenFloor</terrain </li </legend <mask <li </li <li </li <li </li <li </li </mask <terrain <li eee</li <li eee</li <li eee</li <li eee</li </terrain <things <li OOO</li <li ObO</li <li O.F</li <li OOO</li </things <roof <li ^^^</li <li ^^^</li <li ^^^</li <li ^^^</li </roof <anchors <li ...</li <li +..</li <li ...</li <li ...</li </anchors </CustomBaseFramework.PieceDef When the mod is missing, RimWorld drops those legend entries. CBF then checks which characters have no entry left: - A character in the things grid without an entry, outside any anchor cell, means the building would have holes. The piece is not used at all and a warning is logged once. - A character without an entry in terrain , roof , things2 , things3 , plants , or in things under an anchor, only leaves those cells empty. The piece is still used. Objects placed through an object list are optional. Members whose thing does not exist are dropped. If the list ends up empty, the anchor stays empty and the rest of the piece is built. The rule for your own pieces: draw walls, doors and everything the building needs directly. Put devices, furniture and decoration that only some mods bring into an object list behind an anchor. The annex above also shows the Annex anchor. It lies on the west wall cell, so the annex shares its wall with the house. It could also lie one cell outside the shape, then the annex stands next to the house with its own wall. Categories A category from the Medieval Overhaul set, shipped in 1.6/Defs/CustomBaseFramework/CBF Freigabe Kategorien.xml : xml <CustomBaseFramework.PieceCategoryDef <defName CBF MO Farmstead</defName <label Bauernhof</label <zones <li Outside</li </zones <needsRoadAccess false</needsRoadAccess <blockMax 1</blockMax <annexes <li CBF MO Annex Yard</li <li CBF MO Annex Rural</li </annexes <annexMax 3</annexMax </CustomBaseFramework.PieceCategoryDef <CustomBaseFramework.PieceCategoryDef <defName CBF MO Apothecary</defName <label Apotheke</label <parent CBF MO Workshop</parent <zones <li Inside</li </zones <needsRoadAccess true</needsRoadAccess <annexes <li CBF MO Annex Town</li </annexes <annexChance 0.5</annexChance <annexMax 2</annexMax </CustomBaseFramework.PieceCategoryDef Inheritance - parent names the category this one inherits from. The chain may be up to 16 deep and must not loop. - A field that is not set takes the value of the parent, then its parent, and so on. Most category fields are optional for this reason. - A piece in a child category also counts as a piece of every parent. A settlement that orders CBF MO Workshop can get a CBF MO Apothecary piece. - blockMax and merge are read on each category itself, not inherited. Placement rules Field Effect --- --- weight How often pieces of this category are picked when several fit. wallContact Pieces prefer to stand against the town wall. Used when the piece's atWall is Allowed or missing. zones Inside or Outside . needsRoadAccess Must be reachable from a road. wallAccess The pieces give access to the town wall, like a barracks built against it. The generator closes their open sides and counts their cells when it places wall doors. A piece whose category has needsRoadAccess set to false and Outside in zones counts as open. It can be entered from every side, like a farm field. Counts and uniqueness come from the settlement plan, locations from the districts, see Settlements. Blocks - blockMax is the largest number of buildings of this category that join into one block, wall to wall. -1 (the default) uses the general block limit, which is 3 unless the settlement sets another. - merge set to true forces neighbors of this category to share walls. false forbids it. Two buildings only merge when their first category is the same. Annexes Field Set on Effect --- --- --- annexes the house category Which categories may dock on as annexes. Empty takes the list of the parent. annexChance the house category Chance per Annex anchor that an annex is tried. Default 0.6. annexMax the house category Annexes per house at most. Default 2. annexMultiple the annex category true allows the same annex more than once on one house. A category becomes an annex category by being named in some annexes list. Annex pieces without a door count as open. How annexes are placed is on Annexes. Tips - Start from a shipped piece of similar size and change it. The editor's template list works the same way. - Keep one symbol per meaning. Legend symbols are case sensitive, so a and A are two entries. - Use . for empty cells, not spaces. Both work, but dots keep rows readable and equally long. - Give every Fill and Object anchor a rotated placeholder in things . - A gatehouse needs the category WallGateHouse , the type WallPart , two Wall anchors and one Outside anchor. - Mark a cell that belongs to the piece but should keep the map's own ground with the floor CBF EmptyCell . It is not placed in the game. - Run Check in the editor after hand edits. It runs the same checks as the game on load. - Loading errors and skipped pieces are written to the log once per piece. Field reference - PieceDef, LegendEntry, LegendVariant - PlacementRules, ContentZoneSpec, SocketSpec - PieceCategoryDef - StyleDef, TerrainWeight - All fields"}, {"t": "Quests in XML", "u": "pages/Modding-Quests.html", "x": "Modding quests CBF has quest nodes and components to send the player to a CBF settlement that is built for a quest. The two shipped quests use them, see Quests. Their files are the reference: - 1.6/Mods/MedievalOverhaul/Defs/Quests/CBF MO Quest RaidedSite.xml (raided settlement) - 1.6/Mods/MedievalOverhaul/Defs/Quests/CBF MO Quest ContestedSite.xml (contested settlement) - CBF MO Quest SiteParts.xml , CBF MO Quest Orte.xml and CBF MO Quest Ruinen.xml in the same folder All classes are in the namespace CustomBaseFramework . Overview A CBF quest site needs four defs: Def Purpose --- --- SitePartDef Carries CBFSiteExtension (which plans, which ruin) and optionally CBFOccupierExtension (which enemy factions). WorldObjectDef The site. Uses a CBF map generator and adds the quest components. RuinDef Optional. Damage, corpses and who garrisons the settlement. QuestScriptDef The quest. Uses the QuestNode CBF nodes. The settlement plan must have siteTags , see SettlementPlanDef. Plans without siteTags are never used for sites. Site setup WorldObjectDef xml <WorldObjectDef ParentName=\"Site\" <defName CBF MO Quest ContestedSite</defName <label settlement under attack</label <mapGenerator CBF Base Faction</mapGenerator <comps <li Class=\"CustomBaseFramework.WorldObjectCompProperties CBFSiteGoods\" <goodwillPer1000Silver 1.5</goodwillPer1000Silver <ownedFromStart true</ownedFromStart </li <li Class=\"CustomBaseFramework.WorldObjectCompProperties CBFAttack\" / </comps </WorldObjectDef - mapGenerator must be a CBF map generator. The shipped quests use CBF Base Faction . - CBF patches WorldObjectCompProperties Plan into the vanilla Site def. Defs with ParentName=\"Site\" inherit it. Without it QuestNode CBFSite logs a warning and does nothing. - Sites with a CBF map generator get the background map generation on arrival like faction settlements. CBFSiteExtension On the SitePartDef . Field Type Default Meaning --- --- --- --- ruin RuinDef none Ruin applied after the settlement is built. Used when QuestNode CBFSite sets no ruin . plan SettlementPlanDef none A fixed plan. Used if it is usable . planTags list of text none Plans whose siteTags contain one of these. Empty means any plan with siteTags . CBFOccupierExtension On the SitePartDef . Read by QuestNode CBFOccupier . Field Type Default Meaning --- --- --- --- occupiers list of FactionDef none Factions allowed as occupier or attacker. Empty means any faction hostile to the player. xml <SitePartDef <defName CBF MO Quest RaidedSite</defName <requiresFaction true</requiresFaction <wantsThreatPoints true</wantsThreatPoints <minThreatPoints 300</minThreatPoints <modExtensions <li Class=\"CustomBaseFramework.CBFSiteExtension\" <ruin CBF MO Quest Ruin Raided</ruin <planTags <li Dorf</li <li Marktstadt</li </planTags </li <li Class=\"CustomBaseFramework.CBFOccupierExtension\" <occupiers <li DankPyon BrigandFaction</li </occupiers </li </modExtensions </SitePartDef RuinDef and garrison The RuinDef field occupation decides who garrisons the built settlement: Value Garrison --- --- Occupier The site faction, sized by the points from QuestNode CBFSite . Prisoners from prisoners are only placed in this mode. Builder The faction set as builder , scaled by occupationChance . None No garrison. The shipped raided settlement uses Occupier . The contested settlement uses Builder with occupationChance 0.3. Quest nodes QuestNode CBFClient Picks the quest giver. Fails the test run if there is no candidate, so the quest is not offered. A candidate faction is visible, not the player, not defeated, not hidden, not a permanent enemy, not hostile to the player and has a living leader. Empire vassal colonies are skipped. It also needs a plan that fits sitePart : - With plan set in CBFSiteExtension , that plan only has to be usable . - Otherwise the faction needs a plan with siteTags that match planTags . Factions switched off in the mod settings have no plans. Field Type Meaning --- --- --- storeAs slate name Stores the faction leader (Pawn). storeFactionAs slate name Stores the faction. sitePart SitePartDef Its CBFSiteExtension decides which plans fit. QuestNode CBFOccupier Picks the hostile faction. Fails the test run without sitePart or without a candidate. A candidate is not the player, not defeated, hostile to the player, listed in CBFOccupierExtension.besatzer if that list is set, and hostile to enemyOf if set. Field Type Meaning --- --- --- storeAs slate name Stores the faction. storeSitePartDefsAs slate name Stores a list with sitePart as its only entry, for QuestNode GetDefaultSitePartsParams . sitePart SitePartDef Required. enemyOf Faction Optional. The picked faction must be hostile to it. QuestNode CBFSite Tells CBF how to build the site. Place it after QuestNode GenerateSite . Field Type Meaning --- --- --- site Site The site. builder Faction Builder. The settlement is built in this faction's style, independent of the site faction. points number Garrison points. 0 or less means CBF's normal garrison. ruin RuinDef Optional. Overrides CBFSiteExtension.ruin . plan SettlementPlanDef Optional. A fixed plan instead of the tag selection. QuestNode CBFAttack Adds an attack on the settlement. Needs WorldObjectCompProperties CBFAttack on the WorldObjectDef , otherwise it logs a warning. Field Type Meaning --- --- --- site Site The site. attacker Faction Attacking faction. Also used for the attacker corpses of the ruin. points number Points for the attacking combat group. WorldObjectCompProperties CBFAttack has one field: Field Type Default Meaning --- --- --- --- approachDistance number 25 Distance in cells in front of each breach where attackers spawn. Behaviour of WorldObjectComp CBFAngriff : - After CBF has built the settlement, a combat group of attacker with points spawns in front of the breaches of the ruin. If no cell in front is found, it spawns at the breaches. Without breaches it spawns at the map edge. - Defenders are the garrison pawns CBF placed for the settlement faction. Those without a lord and able to fight get a guard duty per ward and do not flee on their own. - The attackers go for the defenders. Without defenders they assault the map. - Every 250 ticks it checks the defenders. When none is on the map, alive and standing while hostiles are still active, it sends the site signal VerteidigerGefallen once. Listen to it as site.VerteidigerGefallen . - If the map was generated without a CBF settlement, the attack starts at the map edge and a warning is logged. QuestNode CBFChoice A choice letter after the fight: leave the site to a faction or take the loot. Field Type Meaning --- --- --- inSignalDefeated signal Sends the letter. inSignalGone signal After inSignalDefeated : picks the loot option if no choice was made. outSignalFaction signal Sent after \"leave to faction\". outSignalLoot signal Sent after \"take loot\". site Site The site. faction Faction Receives goodwill and, on \"leave to faction\", the site. lord Pawn Named in the letter. Falls back to the faction name. goodwillFaction whole number Goodwill for \"leave to faction\". goodwillLoot whole number Goodwill for \"take loot\". keyLabel text key Letter label. Default CBF Quest Wahl Label , argument {0} site. keyText text key Letter text. Default CBF Quest Wahl Text , arguments {0} lord, {1} faction, {2} site, {3} goodwillFaction , {4} goodwillLoot . keyFaction text key Option button. Default CBF Quest Wahl Fraktion , argument {0} goodwill. keyLoot text key Option button. Default CBF Quest Wahl Beute , argument {0} goodwill. On \"leave to faction\" the site and all artificial buildings of the old site faction pass to faction . If the site has WorldObjectComp CBFOrtsgut , its goods are forbidden and belong to faction . Goods already carried by player pawns are charged at once. The letter cannot be dismissed with a right click. WorldObjectComp CBFOrtsgut Charges goodwill for goods the player takes from the site. Added with WorldObjectCompProperties CBFSiteGoods . Field Type Default Meaning --- --- --- --- ansehenJe1000Silber number 1.5 Goodwill lost per 1000 silver of market value. 0 turns the cost off. ownedFromStart bool false The site faction owns the goods from map generation on. Otherwise an owner is only set by QuestNode CBFChoice . - At map generation it records every haulable item on the map. Pawns, corpses and chunks are left out. Stacks split from a recorded item are recorded too. - Nothing is charged while there is no owner. - A recorded item counts as taken when a player pawn gets it into inventory, carry slot, equipment or apparel. Partial stack merges are charged by count. - A spawned thing of the owner that changes to the player faction is charged at its market value. - Fractions add up. Goodwill drops in whole points with the history event CBF Quest SiteGoodsTaken . - The inspect string shows the owner with the keys CBF Quest Ortsgut Inspect or CBF Quest Ortsgut Inspect Eigen . CBFRewardExtension On the QuestScriptDef . Filters item rewards generated for this quest. Field Type Default Meaning --- --- --- --- maxTechLevel TechLevel Medieval Highest allowed tech level. forbiddenThings list of ThingDef none Always excluded. The tech level of a thing is the highest of its own techLevel , its research prerequisites and the research of its recipeMaker . Things with a power comp count as at least Industrial. Excluded things are added to the reward's disallowed things and removed from the market value fillers while rewards are generated. xml <QuestScriptDef <defName CBF MO Quest ContestedSite</defName <modExtensions <li Class=\"CustomBaseFramework.CBFRewardExtension\" <maxTechLevel Medieval</maxTechLevel <forbiddenThings <li Plasteel</li <li ComponentIndustrial</li <li MayRequire=\"Ludeon.RimWorld.Biotech\" PowerfocusChip</li </forbiddenThings </li </modExtensions </QuestScriptDef Example: contested settlement Trimmed from CBF MO Quest ContestedSite.xml . Name rules, description rules, the timeout and the leave and reward branches are left out. xml <QuestScriptDef <defName CBF MO Quest ContestedSite</defName <rootSelectionWeight 1.0</rootSelectionWeight <rootMinPoints 300</rootMinPoints <root Class=\"QuestNode Sequence\" <nodes <li Class=\"QuestNode GetMap\" / <li Class=\"CustomBaseFramework.QuestNode CBFClient\" <storeAs asker</storeAs <storeFactionAs askerFaction</storeFactionAs <sitePart CBF MO Quest ContestedSite</sitePart </li <li Class=\"QuestNode GetSiteTile\" <storeAs siteTile</storeAs <preferCloserTiles true</preferCloserTiles </li <li Class=\"CustomBaseFramework.QuestNode CBFOccupier\" <storeAs angreiferFaction</storeAs <storeSitePartDefsAs sitePartDefs</storeSitePartDefsAs <sitePart CBF MO Quest ContestedSite</sitePart <enemyOf $askerFaction</enemyOf </li <li Class=\"QuestNode GetDefaultSitePartsParams\" <tile $siteTile</tile <faction $askerFaction</faction <sitePartDefs $sitePartDefs</sitePartDefs <storeSitePartsParamsAs sitePartsParams</storeSitePartsParamsAs </li <li Class=\"QuestNode GetSiteThreatPoints\" <storeAs sitePoints</storeAs <sitePartsParams $sitePartsParams</sitePartsParams </li <li Class=\"QuestNode Multiply\" <value1 $sitePoints</value1 <value2 3</value2 <storeAs angreiferPunkte</storeAs </li <li Class=\"QuestNode GenerateSite\" <sitePartsParams $sitePartsParams</sitePartsParams <faction $askerFaction</faction <tile $siteTile</tile <worldObjectDef CBF MO Quest ContestedSite</worldObjectDef <storeAs site</storeAs </li <li Class=\"CustomBaseFramework.QuestNode CBFSite\" <site $site</site <builder $askerFaction</builder <points $sitePoints</points </li <li Class=\"CustomBaseFramework.QuestNode CBFAttack\" <site $site</site <attacker $angreiferFaction</attacker <points $angreiferPunkte</points </li <li Class=\"QuestNode SpawnWorldObjects\" <worldObjects $site</worldObjects </li <li Class=\"QuestNode Signal\" <inSignal site.VerteidigerGefallen</inSignal <node Class=\"QuestNode End\" <outcome Fail</outcome </node </li <li Class=\"QuestNode End\" <inSignal site.AllEnemiesDefeated</inSignal <outcome Success</outcome </li </nodes </root </QuestScriptDef Step by step: 1. QuestNode CBFClient picks a friendly faction that has a village or market town plan ( planTags of the site part) and stores it with its leader. 2. QuestNode CBFOccupier picks a brigand faction hostile to the player and to the quest giver. 3. The site belongs to the quest giver ( faction is $askerFaction ). The attackers get three times the site points. 4. QuestNode CBFSite builds the settlement in the quest giver's style. The site part's ruin CBF MO Quest Ruin Battle uses occupation Builder , so the defenders are the quest giver's soldiers. 5. QuestNode CBFAttack spawns the attackers in front of the breaches once the settlement is built. 6. site.VerteidigerGefallen fails the quest, site.AllEnemiesDefeated completes it. Example: the choice From CBF MO Quest RaidedSite.xml . Here the site belongs to the occupier ( QuestNode GenerateSite with $siteFaction ), QuestNode CBFSite sets builder to the quest giver and points to 1.5 times the site points. xml <li Class=\"CustomBaseFramework.QuestNode CBFChoice\" <inSignalDefeated site.AllEnemiesDefeated</inSignalDefeated <inSignalGone site.Destroyed</inSignalGone <outSignalFaction WahlFraktion</outSignalFaction <outSignalLoot WahlBeute</outSignalLoot <site $site</site <faction $askerFaction</faction <lord $asker</lord <goodwillFaction 20</goodwillFaction <goodwillLoot 3</goodwillLoot </li <li Class=\"QuestNode End\" <inSignal WahlFraktion</inSignal <outcome Success</outcome </li <li Class=\"QuestNode End\" <inSignal WahlBeute</inSignal <outcome Success</outcome </li The WorldObjectDef of this site has WorldObjectCompProperties CBFSiteGoods without ownedFromStart . Taking goods is free until the player picks \"leave to faction\". Debug actions Under \"CBF Quests\" in the debug menu, while on a map: - Enter raided settlement: generates the map of a site with WorldObjectComp CBFOrtsgut and moves the free colonists there. - Defeat occupiers: kills all pawns hostile to the player on the current map. - Log raided settlement: writes site, plan, builder, ruin, points and recorded goods to the log. - Carry settlement item: a free colonist picks up the most valuable recorded item and the goodwill change is logged."}, {"t": "Settlement types in XML", "u": "pages/Modding-Settlements.html", "x": "Writing a settlement type in XML This page is for modders who write settlement types by hand. The in-game editor writes the same XML, see Settlements. Every node on this page exists in the code. The full list is in the field reference, linked at the end. What a settlement type is A settlement type is a CustomBaseFramework.SettlementPlanDef . It does not contain a map. It is a building program: which wards the place has, which walls, towers and gates protect them, which buildings go where, and which fields and pens lie around it. When a caravan or raid first reaches a base, the generator plans the settlement on the real map of that tile: 1. It picks one settlement type for the faction. 2. It lays out the wards as rings: the town in the middle, the suburb around it, the countryside outside. 3. It builds the walls, places towers and gatehouses, and runs the main road and lanes through the gates. 4. It splits each ward into districts and places the buildings each district asks for. 5. It adds fields next to farmhouses and pens in the countryside. 6. It fills every planned spot with a piece (a PieceDef, see Pieces) and spawns the garrison. The settlement type decides what is asked for. The pieces decide what each building looks like. Minimal example The shortest settlement type that loads without errors and is actually used: xml <Defs <CustomBaseFramework.SettlementPlanDef <defName MyMod Plan Hamlet</defName <label hamlet</label <kind ModernTown</kind <styles <li CBF ModernVanilla</li </styles <techLevels <li Industrial</li </techLevels <forAllFactions true</forAllFactions <wards <li <kind Town</kind <districts <li <entries <li <tag CBF Vanilla HouseSmall</tag <count 6~10</count <filler true</filler </li </entries </li </districts </li </wards </CustomBaseFramework.SettlementPlanDef </Defs - kind is set to ModernTown because the default kind, MedievalTown , needs a wall on the town ward. Without one the planner stops with \"The fortified city requires a wall template\". - styles names one of the shipped styles. - materialList names a material list; without one the plan is not used. - techLevels together with forAllFactions makes the plan available to every industrial faction that has no plans of its own. Without faction settings the plan is only used for places that have no faction. - The load check needs at least one building entry, a mapSize of 150 or more (default 175) and road widths within the limit of the kind. - There is no garrison, so the defenders come from defenderPoints (default 180 to 420). Header fields From CBF Plan Village New (Medieval Overhaul village), trimmed: xml <CustomBaseFramework.SettlementPlanDef MayRequire=\"DankPyon.Medieval.Overhaul\" <defName CBF Plan Village New</defName <label village (MO)</label <kind MedievalTown</kind <styles <li Rustical</li <li Standard</li </styles <coreSize 50~60</coreSize <mapSize 200</mapSize <factionTags <li DankPyon NobleHouseSavage</li <li DankPyon NobleHouseCivil</li </factionTags <forAllFactions true</forAllFactions <techLevels <li Medieval</li </techLevels <share 80</share <worldTexture World/WorldObjects/CBF/CBF VillagePalisade</worldTexture <wdLevel 1</wdLevel </CustomBaseFramework.SettlementPlanDef Node Editor name What it does Default --- --- --- --- defName Unique name. Saved on the world object, so do not rename it after release. required label Label Name players see on the world map. none kind Settlement kind MedievalTown (medieval, town ward needs a wall) or ModernTown (modern, walls optional, roads up to 6 wide). Camp and Dungeon exist but are never picked in the current version. MedievalTown styles Styles used to pick the wall profile. They do not filter which pieces are used. none materialList Material list Required. The material list for walls, doors, gates, fences, roads and roofs the generator builds itself. none coreSize Core size Smallest edge length of the settlement. If the buildings need more room the settlement grows, up to what the map allows. 66~74 mapSize Map size Edge length of the map this settlement asks for. The larger of this and the world setting is used. Below 150 is a load error. 175 factionTags Factions FactionDef defNames or faction categoryTag values that get this plan. none excludeFactions Excluded factions FactionDef defNames that never get this plan. none techLevels Tech levels Tech levels this plan fits. Only used together with forAllFactions . none forAllFactions For every faction of these levels Offers the plan to every faction whose tech level is in techLevels , unless that faction has plans of its own. false share Weight Chance against the other plans a faction can get. 0 counts as 1. 0 worldTexture World map icon Texture path of the world map icon, tinted in the faction color. none wdLevel WD tier World Domination tier this plan stands for, 0 to 4, as a number or a range like 2~3 . 0 means no tier. See Compatibility. 0 requiredMods Needs these mods Read by the editor only. When the editor saves the plan, it writes these mods and the ones it detects into MayRequire on the def node. It does not stop a hand-written def from loading. none siteTags Plans with site tags are the only ones used for quest sites. Leave it empty for faction bases. none MayRequire MayRequire is RimWorld's own attribute. On the def node it skips the whole plan when the mod is missing. On a list item it skips only that item: xml <plants <li MayRequire=\"DankPyon.Medieval.Overhaul\" DankPyon Plant Wheat</li <li Plant Corn</li </plants Put MayRequire on the def node whenever the plan names pieces, categories or pawn lists from another mod. requiredMods alone does not protect a hand-written plan. Wards A ward is one ring of the settlement. There are three kinds, set with kind : kind Editor name Layer Notes --- --- --- --- Town Town inner Required. In a medieval plan it must have walls. Suburb Suburb middle Optional. Without walls it is an open suburb. Without the ward there is no suburb. Rural Countryside outer Optional. Never walled. Pens are only placed here. Write each kind once. The enum also lists Castle , but the planner has no layer of its own for it; do not use it. From CBF Plan FortressCity New , the town ward, trimmed: xml <wards <li <kind Town</kind <label Old town</label <roads <li DankPyon Floor Cobblestone Granite</li </roads <walls <li <piece TownWall 1x5</piece <weight 0.75</weight </li <li <piece CBF MO TownWall 1x5 Embrasure</piece <weight 0.25</weight </li </walls <emplacements <li <tag CBF MO FortressTower</tag <count 4~6</count </li </emplacements <gatehouses <li <tag CBF MO FortressGate</tag <count 1~2</count </li </gatehouses <garrison <li <list CBF MO Garrison Walkway</list <count 16~21</count <post Walkway</post </li <li <list CBF MO Garrison Gate</list <count 5~8</count <post Gate</post </li <li <list CBF MO Garrison Inside</list <count 8~13</count <post Inside</post </li </garrison <districts ... </districts </li </wards The shipped file has no label line; it is added here to show the node. Node Editor name What it does Default --- --- --- --- kind Kind Ward kind, see above. Town label Label Name shown for the ward. Empty uses the kind name. none name Name Replaced by the kind at load. Use label instead. Town walls Walls Wall pieces of this ring, see below. none emplacements Defensive positions Towers and other works on the wall. none gatehouses Gatehouses Pieces built where a road crosses the wall. Empty takes the gatehouse of the wall profile. none gate Gate ThingDef that closes the gate passages. Empty uses the gate of the material list. none garrison Garrison Defenders of this ward, see below. none ground Ground TerrainDefs for the open ground between the houses. Empty keeps the map terrain. none roads Paths TerrainDefs for lanes, squares and gate passages. Empty uses the road of the material list. none roadEdges Kerb TerrainDefs for the road edge. none buildingGap Gap between buildings in this ward, as a range. The planner uses the lower value, 0 to 4. 0~0 clearRubble false leaves some plants and trees standing on open ground. true size , wallDepth Size, Wall thickness Kept for the editor. The planner sizes a ward from its buildings and takes the wall thickness from the wall pieces. 1 , 0 Walls, emplacements and gatehouses All three are lists of wall entries. An entry is either plain text or a node with fields: xml <walls <li MayRequire=\"DankPyon.Medieval.Overhaul\" Palisade</li <li <piece CBF MO TownWall Embrasure</piece <weight 0.25</weight </li </walls <emplacements <li <tag CBF MO PalisadeTower</tag <count 1~2</count </li </emplacements <gatehouses <li <piece CBF MO Gatehouse</piece </li </gatehouses - walls : the pieces the wall is built from. The first entry is plain text, the second names one piece with piece and a weight, so it makes up about a fifth of the wall. - emplacements : towers and other pieces set into the wall. Here one or two pieces from the category CBF MO PalisadeTower , picked with tag . - gatehouses : the pieces placed where roads cross the wall. Here always the piece CBF MO Gatehouse . Node What it does Default --- --- --- plain text A PieceDef or PieceCategoryDef name. CBF looks for a piece first, then a category. piece One PieceDef. none tag A PieceCategoryDef. Every non-template piece in it can be used. none weight Share of this entry. For walls it sets how often each piece appears along the wall. 1 count For emplacements and gatehouses: how many. 0~0 lets the planner place as many as the wall needs. 0~0 - A wall piece must be one cell wide in one direction. Its length in the other direction is the wall thickness: 1 is a palisade, 3 a wall with a walkway, 5 a broad fortress wall. - Use pieces of the same thickness within one ward. The thickness comes from the wall pieces, not from any wallDepth node. - Tower sizes are read from square pieces with an odd edge length. Garrison Node What it does Default --- --- --- list A PawnListDef, see Pawns. A missing list leaves the entry empty and logs a warning. none count How many pawns from this list. 1~1 post Post: Inside (inside), Walkway (on the wall walkway) or Gate (at the gates). Inside A ward with a garrison uses only its own entries. A ward without one takes its share of the plan's defenderPoints . defendersOnWall (default 0.25) sets how many of those point-based defenders stand on the wall. Districts and building entries A district is a group of buildings that stay together. Each ward has one or more districts. From CBF Plan FortressCity New , town ward: xml <districts <li <name Castle</name <prefCenter 1</prefCenter <entries <li <tag CBF MO FortressKeep</tag <count 1~1</count <unique true</unique </li <li <tag CBF MO Barracks</tag <count 2~2</count </li <li <piece CBF MO Dungeon</piece <count 1~2</count </li </entries </li <li <name Center</name <entries <li <tag CBF MO Tavern</tag <count 1~1</count </li <li <tag CBF MO Church</tag <count 1~1</count </li <li <tag CBF MO BurgherHouse</tag <count 6~14</count <filler true</filler </li <li <tag CBF MO GapYard</tag <count 0~6</count <filler true</filler </li </entries </li </districts The prefCenter line is added for this page; the shipped plan leaves the location to the planner. District nodes Node Editor name What it does Default --- --- --- --- name Name Internal name. Must be unique across the whole plan, a duplicate logs a warning. Fields and pens use it to find their buildings. Default label Label Display name. none entries Buildings The building entries. none prefCenter Toward the center Pull toward the center of the ward. 0 prefGate Toward the gate Pull toward the gates. 0 prefWall Toward the wall Pull toward the wall. 0 prefMain Along the main road Pull toward the main road. 0 fields , pens Fields, Pens See the next section. none The four location values are weighed against each other, so 1 and 0 means the same as 0.5 and 0 . When all four are 0, the district starts as far as possible from the districts already placed. Building entries Node Editor name What it does Default --- --- --- --- tag Category A PieceCategoryDef. The planner picks pieces from this category. none piece One specific PieceDef. none count Count How many, as a range. More than 150 is cut to 150. 1~1 unique Only once Sets the count to exactly 1. false filler Filler After the fixed buildings are placed, filler entries fill the free space until the target built share is reached. They may go above their maximum. A filler counts as missing only below half its minimum. false weight Weight Only on piece entries. Changes how often that piece is chosen wherever its category is drawn in this plan. 0 turns the piece off. On tag entries it has no effect. 1 lootTable A LootTableDef. Used for loot spots in these pieces that name no table of their own. Only on tag entries. A missing table logs a warning. none - Name either tag or piece . With both, the piece wins and a warning is logged. With neither, the entry is skipped. - Use tag for anything that should vary. Use piece for one building that must appear as drawn, such as a keep or a dungeon. - The minimum of an entry is a wish, not a guarantee. If the space runs out, the planner reports what it could not place. Fields and pens Fields and pens belong to a district in the suburb or countryside ward. From CBF Plan Village New : xml <li <kind Rural</kind <districts <li <name Mill</name <fields <li <count 2~3</count <fence false</fence <plants <li MayRequire=\"DankPyon.Medieval.Overhaul\" DankPyon Plant Wheat</li </plants <tags <li CBF MO Mill</li </tags </li </fields <entries <li <tag CBF MO Mill</tag <count 1~1</count </li </entries </li <li <name Pasture</name <pens <li <count 1~1</count <herd 2~5</herd <pawns Hardanimals 1</pawns <terrain Soil</terrain <plants <li Plant Haygrass</li </plants </li </pens <entries <li <tag CBF MO Barn</tag <count 1~1</count </li </entries </li </districts </li Field nodes Fields are read from the Suburb and Rural wards. Fields in the town ward are ignored. Node Editor name What it does Default --- --- --- --- count Count How many fields this entry adds to the ward. 1~1 tags Categories (Farmsteads) Categories whose buildings get a field next to them. Empty in a district takes the categories of the district's entries. none plants Plants ThingDefs of the crops. none crops Crop list A CropListDef with weighted plants. Used instead of plants when set, see Loot. none fence Fence around the field. false weight Weight Chance of this field kind when a district has several. 1 The field needs a building from tags to grow next to. List that category in the district's entries, or the field has no farmhouse. Pen nodes Pens are read from the Rural ward only. They are placed near the buildings of their district. Node Editor name What it does Default --- --- --- --- count Count How many pens. 1~1 pawns Animals A PawnListDef with the animals. A missing list gives an empty pen. none herd Animals per pen. Capped by the pen area. 3~7 plants Plants What grows inside the pen. none crops Crop list A CropListDef, used instead of plants when set. none terrain Floor TerrainDef for the pen floor. Empty keeps the map terrain. none weight Weight Makes this pen larger or smaller. 1 The ward node supply (default 1.2) scales the size of all pens in the countryside. Harbors A medieval settlement at the coast or on a river of at least minRiverWidth cells gets a harbor at the shore: jetties, fishing huts, harbor store, harbor master and boat sheds, with a path to the town. xml <harbor <entries <li <tag CBF MO Harbormaster</tag <count 1~1</count </li <li <tag CBF MO HarborStore</tag <count 1~1</count </li <li <tag CBF MO Jetty</tag <count 1~2</count </li <li <tag CBF MO Boathouse</tag <count 0~1</count </li </entries </harbor Node What it does Default --- --- --- entries Categories ( tag ) with a count range. none minRiverWidth Smallest river width in cells that counts. The sea always counts. 10 range How far from the settlement harbor pieces may stand, in cells. 45 Harbor pieces need a Connect anchor named Water on their water side, see Pieces and categories in XML. Without suitable water no harbor is built. Tuning fields These sit on the plan, next to the header fields: xml <roadMain 2</roadMain <roadLane 2</roadLane <targetBuilt 0.62</targetBuilt <defenderPoints 400~700</defenderPoints <defendersOnWall 0.28</defendersOnWall Node Editor name What it does Default --- --- --- --- roadMain Main road width Width of the main road. At most 2 for medieval plans, 6 for modern ones. More is a load error. 2 roadLane Lane width Width of the side lanes. Same limits. 1 targetBuilt Target built share Share of the town and suburb area covered by buildings. It sets how large the settlement is planned and how long filler entries keep filling. 0 uses the planner default of 0.62. Shipped medieval plans use 0.5 to 0.62. 0 defenderPoints Defender strength Points for wards without a garrison. 180~420 defendersOnWall Defenders on the wall Share of point-based defenders on the wall, 0 to 1. 0.25 assaultDelayTicks Assault delay Ticks before the defenders attack. 25000 Gaps between buildings are set per ward with buildingGap . The plan nodes minBuildingGap and maxBuildingGap are only used when the plan has no town ward. Use targetBuilt to change density. Older plans put walls , emplacements , gatehouses and districts directly on the plan. That still loads as a single town ward. Use wards for new plans. How CBF picks a plan for a faction For every faction base CBF builds a pool of plans, then draws one: 1. Own plans. Every plan whose factionTags names the faction's defName or its categoryTag . 2. General plans. Only if step 1 found nothing: every plan with forAllFactions set and the faction's tech level in techLevels . 3. Exclusions. A faction listed in excludeFactions never gets that plan, in either step. 4. Unusable plans. Plans without a usable material list and plans of the hidden kinds Camp and Dungeon are left out. 5. Tier filter. With World Domination 2.0, only the plans whose wdLevel is closest to the base's tier stay; plans without wdLevel drop out as soon as one plan in the pool has one. With A Rim of Ice and Fire, the faction's main seat prefers plans with wdLevel 3 or more, other bases plans below 3. 6. Draw. One plan by weight, share (0 counts as 1). The draw depends on the world object, so the same base always gets the same plan. A faction that ends up with an empty pool keeps RimWorld's own base. A place without a faction takes the plans that have no factionTags . Players can switch single factions back to RimWorld's bases in the mod settings, see Mod settings. A switched-off faction gets no plan, whatever the XML says. Quest sites work differently. They only take plans that have siteTags . Tips - Start from a shipped plan. Copy it, change defName , label and factionTags , then change one thing at a time. - Give a plan for your own faction factionTags and leave forAllFactions off. Then it cannot spill over to other factions of the same tech level. - Setting forAllFactions without techLevels does nothing. - A faction with own plans never gets the general ones. If you add one plan for a faction, it loses the shipped general plans. Add enough plans of your own, or none. - Use share to make a large town rare. The Medieval Overhaul village uses 80, the fortress city 10. - Every tag must match a category that has pieces for the loaded mods. A category without pieces places nothing. - Put MayRequire on the def node and on every list item that names content from another mod. - A very large program on a small map gets cut, and CBF logs a warning with the edge length it needed. Raise mapSize , lower the counts, or raise targetBuilt . - Mark the bulk housing of each ward as filler . Fixed buildings are placed first, filler takes what is left. - Keep unique for keeps, town halls and similar single buildings. - Pens need an Rural ward. Fields need a Suburb or Rural ward and a farmhouse category in the same district. - The editor preview shows the plan on a flat map. Build a real base afterwards; the terrain of a tile changes a lot. - defName is saved with the world. Renaming it later makes existing bases draw a new plan. Field reference Every node with its type and default: - SettlementPlanDef - Stadtteil (ward) - WallEntry (walls, emplacements, gatehouses) - Garnisonseintrag (garrison) - DistrictSpec (district) - BudgetEntry (building entry) - Ackerspec (field) - Gehegespec (pen) - Field reference (all pages)"}, {"t": "PawnListDef", "u": "pages/PawnListDef.html", "x": "PawnListDef XML node: <CustomBaseFramework.PawnListDef . Also takes the common fields defName , label and description . Field Type Default Meaning --- --- --- --- members list of PawnListEntry none Which buildings belong to this loot category. radius number 4 How far the pawns spread around their spot, in cells, at least 1. Guards join the general defence and ignore it. role <br Role Pawnrolle (Guard, Civilian, Sentry, Gunner) Civilian How the pawns behave. Guard joins the general defence, Sentry holds its spot, Gunner mans the guns around its spot, Civilian defends its spot but may flee and need not be able to fight. techLevel TechLevel (Undefined, Animal, Neolithic, Medieval, Industrial, Spacer, Ultra, Archotech) Undefined The tech level this list counts as. Set, the settlement draws from the whole list without working out levels from the gear. Empty works the level out per member. Back to the field reference"}, {"t": "PawnListEntry", "u": "pages/PawnListEntry.html", "x": "PawnListEntry Field Type Default Meaning --- --- --- --- ideoRole text none Role of the faction's ideoligion for pawns of this entry: empty, Kampf (shooting or melee specialist by weapon) or a role precept defName. Needs Ideology. kind <br Settlement kind text none On a settlement type: MedievalTown or ModernTown. ModernTown allows roads up to 6 wide and lays power conduits. Camp and Dungeon are switched off at the moment. On a pawn list entry: the pawn kind (PawnKindDef defName). weight number 1 How likely this is picked over its siblings. Belongs on the settlement, not on the piece. Back to the field reference"}, {"t": "Pawn kinds and lists", "u": "pages/Pawns.html", "x": "Pawns The Pawn tab edits pawn kinds and pawn lists. Pawn lists are what garrisons, Pawn anchors and pens use. The chips at the top switch between Mod (only what your mod contains) and All mods . Kinds from other mods can be viewed and copied into your mod, not changed in place. Pawn kind from selection copies the kind of the pawn selected on the map. Pawn kinds 1. Portrait of an example pawn, turnable. It updates with every change. 2. Key figures : combat power and the tech level, with the reason for it. 3. Gear grid : layers from outside in, body parts across. A free cell opens a picker with only what fits there. Hatched cells are blocked by the piece of the same color. hide empty hides rows and columns nobody uses. 4. Gear list : each piece with layer, stuff, quality and color. Swap replaces a piece with another that fits the same layers and keeps stuff, quality and color where possible. 5. Right side : weapon tags and budget, skills, traits (forbidden and forced, with degree), implants, and All fields for everything else. 6. Place pawn puts one on the map. Add to pawn list adds the kind to a list. The check at the bottom is the same one the game runs when it loads. As long as it finds errors, saving is blocked and the reason is shown. Pawn lists 1. Members as tiles with portrait, weight and share. + Pawn kind opens a tile picker with portraits. Pick several at once, filter by People or Animals and by mod. Kinds already in the list are hidden. With Ideology active, the R button on a tile gives pawns of that kind a role of their faction's ideoligion: a role precept, or Combat role by weapon for the shooting or melee specialist. 2. Role : Guard patrols, Post holds its position, Gunner mans artillery, Civilian flees. 3. Radius : how far they spread around their anchor. 4. Tech level : from gear works the level out from each member's weapons and apparel, see below. A fixed level makes the settlement draw from the whole list, for example tribal warriors in medieval leather for a tribal camp. 5. Used by : the settlements and pieces that use this list. A list uses the members with the highest tech level at or below the tech level of the settlement. Kinds without a tech level always count. One list of guards can therefore serve every age."}, {"t": "PieceCategoryDef", "u": "pages/PieceCategoryDef.html", "x": "PieceCategoryDef XML node: <CustomBaseFramework.PieceCategoryDef . Also takes the common fields defName , label and description . Field Type Default Meaning --- --- --- --- annexChance number, optional none Chance per annex anchor of a house that an annex is built there, 0 to 1. Empty takes the parent category, then 0.6. annexCornerPath true/false, optional none Where an annex of this category attaches, the two inner corner wall cells at the ends of the straight house wall become floor. Used for lean-to tents. annexMax whole number, optional none At most this many annexes per house. Empty takes the parent category, then 2. annexMultiple true/false, optional none Set on the category of the annex. True lets the same annex piece attach to one house several times. Default false. annexes list of PieceCategoryDef none Categories whose pieces may attach as annexes at the Anbau anchors of houses in this category. Empty takes the list of the parent category. annexOverhang whole number, optional none With annexFill: how many cells the annex must reach past the house wall at each end. 0 means flush. annexFill true/false, optional none Set on the annex category. True: the annex only attaches where it covers the whole wall side of the house. blockMax whole number -1 How many buildings of this category may stand wall to wall in one block. Empty takes the planner default of 3, 0 means no limit, 1 keeps them apart. wallAccess true/false, optional none Pieces of this category are joined to the wall walkway. A door is set where the walkway passes them, and their open sides away from the wall are closed. Empty takes the parent category, then false. needsRoadAccess <br Needs a road true/false, optional none Must be reachable from the road network. parent PieceCategoryDef none The category this one inherits from. Fields left unset here come from it. merge true/false, optional none True joins buildings of this category wall to wall with neighbours of the same category, false never does. Empty joins them only where the plan is dense. wallContact <br Touches the wall true/false, optional none Must sit against the town wall. weight number, optional none How likely this is picked over its siblings. Belongs on the settlement, not on the piece. zones list of text none Only Outside has an effect: together with needsRoadAccess set to false, the building needs no road and may be entered from every side. Inside and Center do nothing. Where a building stands is decided by the budget of its ward. Back to the field reference"}, {"t": "PieceDef", "u": "pages/PieceDef.html", "x": "PieceDef XML node: <CustomBaseFramework.PieceDef . Also takes the common fields defName , label and description . Field Type Default Meaning --- --- --- --- allowMirror true/false true May the generator mirror this piece. Switch off for pieces that only work one way round. allowRotation <br Rotatable true/false true May the generator turn this piece. Switch off for pieces that only read right way up. anchors list of text none Cells that must sit on the town wall. A barracks anchors here so it merges into the wall instead of standing in front of it. anchorsOver list of text none A second anchor layer above the first. Its anchors may overlap others and clear nothing underneath, for example a tent over a market stall. offIfMod text none packageIds of mods, separated by commas. The piece is not placed when one of them is active. categories list of PieceCategoryDef none What this piece is. The first one is the primary: on conflict its rules win, field by field, then its parents, then the next category. clearFootprint <br Clear ground true/false true Remove whatever stands on the cells before stamping. content <br Content zones list of text none Cells that get loot at spawn time. Furniture is never chosen automatically, only loot is. contentZones <br Content zone rules list of ContentZoneSpec none What goes into each content zone: which loot table, which building category. drawnOutward text none Which side of this gatehouse faces out of the town as drawn: North, East, South or West. An Outside anchor wins. Empty takes gatehouseOutward of the wall profile, else South. legend list of LegendEntry none What each character in the layers means: a thing, a terrain, a role. mask <br Shape list of text none Which cells belong to the piece at all. Everything outside stays untouched. onlyWithMod text none packageIds of mods, separated by commas. The piece is only placed when all of them are active. outwardIsLow true/false, optional none Which end of a wall strip faces out of the town. True means the bottom of an upright strip or the left end of a flat one. An Outside anchor in the strip wins. On a piece, empty takes the value of the wall profile, whose default is true. placement <br Placement rules PlacementRules none Overrides the rules of the category for this one piece. Leave it out and the category decides. plants list of text none The plant layer, for gardens and fields. roof list of text none Where a roof is built. schemaVersion <br Schema version whole number 0 Which format this file was written in. Older files are migrated on load. style StyleDef none Old field. It only still picks the wall profile and the standard materials. Does not restrict piece selection. tag <br Category (old) PieceCategoryDef none The first category. Kept so old files still load; write categories instead. template true/false false A piece to copy from. It is never placed itself. terrain <br Floor list of text none The floor layer. One character per cell, resolved through the legend. things <br Objects list of text none The load-bearing layer: walls, doors, anything a cell is built out of. things2 <br Objects, second layer list of text none What hangs on the first layer, such as a wall torch. RimWorld allows a wall and something attached to it in the same cell; with only one layer the wall would get a hole. things3 <br Objects, third layer list of text none A third pass for the rare cell that carries three things. eaves <br Eaves true/false false Places an eave from Architecture of the Rim on every outer wall and outer door. Does nothing without that mod. eavesMaterial <br Eave material text none Material of the eaves. Empty takes the material of the wall they sit on. type PieceType (Building, WallPart, DungeonRoom) Building Who consumes this piece: the settlement generator, the wall profile, or the dungeon generator. Back to the field reference"}, {"t": "Draw and edit pieces", "u": "pages/Pieces.html", "x": "Pieces A piece is one building or one stretch of wall, drawn on the map and saved as a PieceDef. Houses, workshops, towers, gatehouses and wall segments are all pieces. A piece is a template. Object and Loot anchors, alternatives in the legend, materials from the map and annexes make every placed copy different, see Every building differs. Draw one good house with several anchors and annex points rather than several fixed ones. 1. List grouped by category. The chips filter by size (small up to 8 cells, medium 9 to 14, large 15 and more) or show templates only. All mods below them shows only the pieces of one mod; pieces from a subfolder such as Mods/MedievalOverhaul are listed separately. Right-click an entry to rename, duplicate, mark as template or delete it. 2. Image drawn from the piece's data: floor, walls, furniture and the anchors on top. The dashed line is the outline of the piece. Small tiles elsewhere in the editor show anchors by color only, without names. 3. Basics. Size, categories, rotation, clearing the footprint, and for buildings At the wall and Weight . Only with mod and Off with mod tie the piece to other mods: it is only placed when all mods of the first row are active, and not placed when one mod of the second row is active. Pick the mods from the list of installed mods; an inactive mod can be picked too. 4. Anchors of the piece and the details of the selected one. 5. Check runs the same check the game runs on load. Click the result at the bottom left to see every finding. Save writes the piece back into its file. The mod filter lists every mod that brings pieces, with the number of pieces: Draw a new piece A piece is drawn in the normal game, on the map of your colony, and then taken into the editor. 1. Build the house first, with the normal architect tools, in god mode if you like. Build it exactly as it should appear: the walls, floors, doors, furniture and stuff you place are what the piece will contain. Pick concrete things here, such as a wooden wall or a specific bed, because the export takes them as they stand. 2. Click + New in the Piece tab. The editor folds away and the toolbox Piece on the map opens on the right. 3. Paint area marks the cells that belong to the piece. The area does not have to be a rectangle. Cut takes cells out again. 4. Empty cell marks cells that belong to the piece but stay empty, for example a yard. 5. Give the piece a Name and pick a Category in the picker, or create a new one there. Without a category it cannot be exported. 6. Check , then Export . The piece is written into your mod and appears in the list. 7. Back to the editor opens the window again with the new piece selected. A gatehouse needs two anchors named Wall and one named Outside. You set them in the image after the export, until then the check reports them as notes. Change an existing piece - Place to edit puts the piece on the map so you can change its structure, then export it again. Its anchors are kept. - Place puts a copy on the map the way the generator would build it, with variants and chances rolled. Good for a look, not for exporting. Anchors An anchor marks cells where something happens once the piece stands: a wall docks on, loot appears, people wait. 1. + Anchor creates an anchor and lets you drag its area in the image right away. The size shows while you drag. 2. Paint anchors lays a cell grid over the image. Drag rectangles to add cells to the selected anchor. Right-click or Escape ends it. 3. Erase anchors removes anchor cells inside a rectangle. 4. From another piece copies all anchors of another piece with their settings, without areas. 5. Each row has Area (drag it again), Copy (same settings, no area) and × . Anchor kinds - Connect ties the piece to its surroundings. The name decides what for: - Wall : the town wall docks here. Gatehouses need at least two cells. - Outside : which side faces out. Gatehouses need it. - Anbau : annexes attach here, see Annexes. - Loot places objects from an object list and fills them from a loot table. Loot decoration only leaves them empty, unchanged leaves them to the mod that brings them. - Object places objects from an object list with a chance, without loot. With Ideology active, Ideoligion building places a building of the owning faction's ideoligion there instead, such as an altar or a ritual spot. - Pawn places pawns from a pawn list, or a single pawn kind, with a count and a chance. The ... button in front of a list opens a preview of what is in it. - An anchor whose list does not exist, or whose list holds nothing that fits the anchor area, marks the piece red in the list. - If only some objects of the list are too large for the area, the anchor shows a yellow note. Only what fits is placed. Things from other mods - A thing drawn directly into the piece is required. If its mod or DLC is missing, the generator does not use the piece at all, because a missing wall or door would leave a hole. - A thing placed through an object list is optional. Entries whose mod is missing drop out. If nothing is left, nothing appears at the anchor and the rest of the piece is built. The placeholder drawn under the anchor may come from that mod; it does not block the piece. - So: draw walls, doors and anything the building needs directly. Put devices, furniture and decoration that only some mods bring into an object list."}, {"t": "PlacementRules", "u": "pages/PlacementRules.html", "x": "PlacementRules Field Type Default Meaning --- --- --- --- atWall <br At wall text none How much this piece wants to touch the town wall. Resolved from the piece, then its categories. countPerSettlement <br Count per settlement IntRange, optional none How many of these a settlement wants, as a range. needsRoadAccess <br Needs a road true/false, optional none Must be reachable from the road network. weight number, optional none How likely this is picked over its siblings. Belongs on the settlement, not on the piece. zones list of text none Only Outside has an effect: together with needsRoadAccess set to false, the building needs no road and may be entered from every side. Inside and Center do nothing. Where a building stands is decided by the budget of its ward. Back to the field reference"}, {"t": "Quests", "u": "pages/Quests.html", "x": "Quests CBF ships two quests for Medieval Overhaul. Both send you to a settlement of a friendly faction that CBF builds as a full town. For building your own quests see Modding quests. Requirements - Medieval Overhaul must be active. The quests are in 1.6/Mods/MedievalOverhaul . - The quest giver is a faction that is not hostile to you, has a living leader and can get a CBF village or market town. In practice these are the Medieval Overhaul noble houses. With VFE Medieval 2 active, its kingdoms qualify too. Medieval factions of other mods that get the shared village plan qualify as well. - The enemy is the Medieval Overhaul brigands faction. It must exist in the world and be hostile to you and to the quest giver. - Factions switched off in the mod settings and Empire vassal colonies are never quest givers. - Both quests enter the normal random quest pool from 300 threat points. The settlement is a village or a market town in the style of the quest giver, see Settlement types. The map is prepared in the background when you arrive by caravan, transport pod, shuttle or gravship, so the game does not freeze on arrival. Raided settlement Quest names: \"The Raided Village\", \"Bandits in [lord]'s Town\", \"[lord] and the Raided Settlement\" and similar. On the world map the site is called \"raided settlement\". Brigands have overrun a settlement of the quest giver. The town is damaged: breached walls, a destroyed gate in some cases, burnt rooms, fires, rubble, dead residents and dead brigands, trampled fields and missing livestock. Part of the loot is gone or piled up with the brigands. A few residents may be held prisoner. The brigands hold the town as its garrison. What you do Attack the settlement and defeat all brigands. There is no fixed reward. What is left in the settlement is the reward. The choice When the last brigand is beaten, a letter asks what to do with the settlement: Option Goodwill with the quest giver Effect --- --- --- Leave it to the lord +20 The settlement and its buildings go back to the quest giver. Everything still there is forbidden and belongs to them. Taking items or claiming buildings there costs goodwill. Take what is left +3 The settlement stays with the brigands. You loot freely. - Items your colonists already carry when you choose the first option count as taken. - If you leave the map without deciding, you take what is left (+3). - Both options complete the quest. Failure - The site disappears after 12 to 28 days if you have not arrived. The brigands have moved on. - You leave the map before all brigands are defeated. Contested settlement Quest names: \"The Siege of [lord]'s Village\", \"Town under Attack\", \"Relief for [lord]\" and similar. On the world map the site is called \"settlement under attack\". Brigands are storming a settlement of the quest giver. The walls are breached, parts are burning. The defenders are the quest giver's own soldiers, a reduced garrison. The attackers are about three times the site threat points strong and start in front of the breaches. What you do Travel there and help the defenders drive off the attackers. Entering the map does not count as an attack on the quest giver. Reward - You choose the reward when you accept the quest, as with vanilla quests. - The reward is about 1.75 times the normal value for the threat points. - Item rewards are medieval only. Things above the medieval tech level are excluded, as are powered things and a fixed list such as plasteel, components, industrial medicine, drugs like go-juice and orbital targeters. - Goodwill and, with Royalty, honor are possible. For honor you pick the colonist who receives it. Taking things The settlement belongs to the quest giver from the start. Taking items from it or claiming its buildings costs goodwill. The same rules apply as after \"Leave it to the lord\" in the raided settlement, see below. Failure - You do not arrive within 3 to 6 days. The settlement has fallen. - All defenders are dead, downed or gone while attackers are still on the map. - You leave the map while attackers are still there. Goodwill for taken goods Applies to the contested settlement and to a raided settlement left to the lord. Inspecting the site on the world map shows \"Belongs to [faction]. Taking items costs goodwill.\" while it applies. - Counted are items that lay in the settlement when the map was generated. Pawns, corpses and chunks do not count. - Items that come to the map later do not count, for example the gear of dead attackers. - An item counts as taken when one of your colonists picks it up, carries it, equips it or wears it. - Claiming a building of the owning faction counts at its market value. - The cost is 1.5 goodwill per 1000 silver of market value. Fractions add up until they reach a full point. - The history entry reads \"took goods from their settlement\"."}, {"t": "RuinDef", "u": "pages/RuinDef.html", "x": "RuinDef XML node: <CustomBaseFramework.RuinDef . Also takes the common fields defName , label and description . Field Type Default Meaning --- --- --- --- attackSide Rot4 200 Side the attackers came from: North, East, South or West. Breaches and the worst damage gather there. Empty picks a side at random. occupation Besatzungsart (None, Builder, Occupier) Occupier Who holds the ruin. None leaves it empty, Builder keeps the original owners, Occupier puts the conquering faction in, drawn from raid points only, without ward garrisons or the units set on anchors. occupationChance number 1 Scales how many defenders the ruin gets. 1 is the full number, 0.5 half of it. lootWithOccupiers number 0 Share of the remaining loot, 0 to 1, that is carried off and piled up on a square near the centre. lootFactor number 1 How much loot remains, 1 keeps all of it. Stacks shrink by this factor, single items survive with this chance. burnMarks number 0 How likely a room is burnt out, 0 to 1, more often near the attack. Burnt rooms get charred floors and lose flammable contents. breaches IntRange 0 How many breaches are torn into the outer wall, as a range. They favour the attack side and the gates. fieldsRavaged number 0 Chance per field cell that its crop is destroyed, 0 to 1. fires IntRange 0 How many fires still burn, as a range. They only start in burnt rooms whose walls do not burn. buildingDamage FloatRange 0~0 Share of hit points buildings lose, as a range from 0 to 1. Buildings near the attack lose more. prisoners IntRange 0 How many former inhabitants the occupiers hold prisoner, as a range. Only when occupation is Occupier. turretsDestroyed number 0 How likely a turret is destroyed, 0 to 1, more often near the attack. clustering number 0.6 How strongly the damage gathers around the breaches and points of attack, 0 to 1. 0 spreads it evenly. attackerCorpses IntRange 0 How many dead attackers lie around, as a range. They have been dead for rotDays. residentCorpses IntRange 0 How many dead inhabitants lie around, as a range. They have been dead for rotDays. rubble number 0 Rubble on open ground and roads, 0 to 1, thicker near the attack. gateDestroyed number 0 Chance per gate that it is torn down, 0 to 1. rotDays FloatRange 0~0 How long the bodies have been dead, in days, as a range. livestockGone number 0 Chance per animal of the owners or occupiers that it is gone, 0 to 1. wallDemolition number 0 How likely a building wall is torn down, 0 to 1, more often near the attack. Back to the field reference"}, {"t": "What you can change", "u": "pages/Settings-Overview.html", "x": "What you can change An overview of everything you can set, which values have a fixed meaning, and which names you should not change. Each field is described in detail in the field reference. Values in XML are case-sensitive and partly German, because they are the names the code uses. Overview by area Area Editor tab What you set there --- --- --- Settlement type Settlement Map size, core size, density, roads, defender points, weight, factions, tech levels, material list, world icon, World Domination tier and type Ward Settlement, tabs on the right Walls, towers, gatehouses, garrison, districts, ground, roads, lanterns, distance, fields and pens District Settlement, Districts Buildings and categories with count, filler, weight and loot table, location preferences Piece Piece The building itself, categories, rotation, wall preference, anchors Category Categories Parent, weight, road access, annexes, blocks Loot Loot Object lists, loot tables, containers, crop lists, material lists Pawns Pawn Pawn kinds, pawn lists with role, radius and tech level Unit swap Unit swap Which units and things a faction uses instead of the general ones Free numbers and texts are safe to change at any time: labels, sizes, counts, weights, chances, distances, gaps and radii. The editor limits them to sensible ranges and marks invalid values red. Values with a fixed meaning These fields only accept certain words. Anything else is an error or is ignored. Ward kind ( kind ) Every settlement type has up to three rings. The kind decides which ring a ward is: Kind Ring Default distance Clears plants --- --- --- --- Town Old town, innermost, protected best, filled first 1 to 2 yes Suburb Suburb around the old town 3 to 5 no Rural Countryside outside, farmsteads and pens 4 to 8 no - The kind is the identity of a ward. A settlement type has at most one ward of each kind. That is why the editor shows it as a fixed tab (Old town, Suburb, Countryside) and does not let you change it. - A missing ward is added with + next to the ward tabs. You cannot add a second ward of the same kind; in XML a second one gets no area. - A walled settlement type needs a Town ward. Without one the plan fails. Without a Suburb there is simply no suburb. - Fields work in the suburb and the countryside, pens only in the countryside. - Castle exists in the code but is not planned as a ring of its own. Do not use it. - name of a ward is replaced when the game loads. Use label for your own display name. Kind of place ( kind ) Kind What changes --- --- MedievalTown Walls with towers and gatehouses, dense wall pass. The default. ModernTown Street grid up to 6 wide, power conduits, no gatehouses, wards without walls allowed, fields not fenced. Camp Like a modern town, but without drawn roads. Only used for the quest sites listed under Camp types ( campTypes ), never for normal settlements. Garrison post ( post ) Post Where the pawns stand --- --- Inside On lanes, squares and open ground inside the ward Walkway On the wall walk at the embrasures Gate At the gates If a post has no cells, for example no wall walk, the pawns stand inside instead. Pawn list role ( role ) Role Behaviour --- --- Guard Guard: joins the defence of the settlement Sentry Post: holds its spot around the anchor Gunner Gunner: mans artillery Civilian Civilian: may flee. Warns when fighters are in the list. The default. Anchors Kind Does --- --- Connect Ties the piece to its surroundings, see the names below Fill Places objects and fills them with loot Object Places objects, or an ideoligion building Spawn Places pawns Anchor names are free labels, except these four: Name Meaning --- --- Wall The town wall docks here. Gatehouses need at least two cells. Outside The side that faces out; gives the piece its direction Annex Annexes attach here, see Annexes Water Water cells of harbour pieces Pieces Field Values --- --- type Building , WallPart (drawn as wall), DungeonRoom rotation North , East , South , West At the wall ( placement.atWall ) Forbidden , Avoid , Allowed , Preferred , StronglyPreferred , Required The terrain CBF EmptyCell marks a cell that belongs to the piece but keeps the natural ground of the map. Material lists - criticality : Optional or Critical . If a critical role finds nothing, the whole list is unusable, and with it every settlement type that uses it. - stuffChain knows two special words: MapStone (stone blocks of the map) and MapWood (wood). - district : Town , Suburb or Rural . - Roles the generator uses itself: WALL , DOOR , GATE , FLOOR ROAD , FENCE , FENCE GATE , PEN MARKER , BRIDGE , ROOF , CONDUIT and the others shown in the list. Other role names work in pieces as long as the list defines them. - Applies to and Ward only take effect for cells the generator draws itself, not for roles inside pieces. Loot Field Values --- --- Missing things ( onMissing ) Skip (default), Fallback (uses the fallback things), Silver Object list display ( kind ) Fill fills the containers (default), Empty places them empty Container flags empty empty, stays stays closed, foreign leaves the loot to the mod that brings the container Other fixed values Field Values --- --- techLevels RimWorld tech levels: Neolithic , Medieval , Industrial , Spacer , Ultra . They only matter together with For all factions of these levels . wdLevel 1 to 4 wdType Tier 1 Logging , Mining , Farming ; tier 2 Production , Slavery ; tier 3 Fortress ; tier 4 Citadel ; any tier Vanguard riafHolding Village (village), Town (town), Garrison (garrison); empty for none. Only with A Rim of Ice and Fire. campTypes Quest site names: Outpost , BanditCamp , RaidSource , WorkSite Mining , WorkSite Logging , WorkSite Hunting , WorkSite Farming Ideoligion role A role precept, or Kampf for the shooting or melee specialist by weapon Ruin occupation ( occupation ) None , Builder (the owner defends), Occupier (occupiers, default) Do not change These names are used by other content, by the code or by savegames. Renaming or deleting them breaks something. - defNames of settlement types in use. Savegames store the settlement type of each settlement by name. A renamed type loads with a warning, and the settlement gets a different type. - District names within a settlement type. Fields and pens belong to their district by name, and names must be unique in the whole plan. - Shared material lists: CBF MaterialList Base , CBF MO MaterialList , CBF Modern MaterialList , CBF VFEM2 MaterialList . Most shipped settlement types use them. - Abstract parents: CBF BeutebehaelterBase , CBF Ruin Basis , CBF Modern SoldatBasis , CBF MO NeutralerSoldatBasis , CBF MO StammBasis . Inherit from them, do not change them. - Categories the generator looks for by name: WallTower , WallGateHouse , GateHouse , WallSegment , CBF ModernDefense , CBF Vanilla Gatehouse , Market , Farm , Landmark , HouseSmall , DungeonRoom , CBF Baubar . - Defs the code looks up: CBF EmptyCell , CBF MapSteps , CBF Base Faction , CBF Terrain , CBF Hold , CBF Walkway , CBF Editor . - The prefix CBF on map generators and map steps marks a map as a CBF settlement, and the uninstaller removes every CBF def. Do not give defs of your own mod this prefix; use your own. - Styles Standard , Rustical , Fancy , CBF Medieval , CBF VFEM2 , CBF ModernVanilla : many pieces refer to them. - packageIds in LoadFolders.xml of other mods: they decide which content folders load. Changing content of other mods You can change settlement types, pieces, categories, loot tables, object lists, crop lists, material lists and pawn lists of CBF or of other mods. The original files stay untouched. When you save, the editor writes a patch into your own mod: Patches/CBF Changes/<type <defName .xml in the version folder of your mod. - Only what you changed is patched: single fields, and single entries of lists. Remove one faction from a list, and the patch removes exactly that entry; add one district entry, and the patch adds exactly that one. Other mods that patch the same def keep working, and later CBF updates to fields you did not touch still arrive. - Every operation only runs when the mod it patches is active ( MayRequire ). For content that CBF or another mod only loads together with a further mod, such as the Medieval Overhaul settlement types, that mod is required too. - Every operation also checks first that its target exists. If a mod renames or removes something, the operation does nothing instead of causing errors. - Saving again rewrites the patch file for that def from the current state. There is no button to undo all changes; to return to the original, delete the patch file. - Renaming and deleting defs of other mods is not possible, because other content and savegames refer to their names. Use Copy into my mod or Duplicate for an independent version under a new name. - Changing the structure of a piece of another mod (exporting it again from the map) is also saved as a patch. The new structure takes effect after restarting the game. Your mod has to load after the mod it patches. The editor adds this to your About.xml."}, {"t": "Shipped settlements", "u": "pages/Settlement-Types.html", "x": "Settlement types The settlement types CBF ships with, their strength and their cost. Every settlement is planned fresh on its own map, so the pictures show one example each. Overview Type Content Tech level WD tier Garrison Buildings Map Walls --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- Village Medieval Overhaul Medieval 1 7 to 17 40 to 56 200 Palisade Market town Medieval Overhaul Medieval 2 24 to 34 93 to 120 200 Town wall, suburb with palisade Large market town Medieval Overhaul Medieval 3 38 to 51 168 to 179 250 Town wall Fortress city Medieval Overhaul Medieval 4 43 to 62 129 to 155 200 Thick town wall Village VFE Medieval 2 Medieval 1 7 to 17 44 to 55 200 Palisade Market town VFE Medieval 2 Medieval 2 24 to 34 84 to 102 200 Town wall Large market town VFE Medieval 2 Medieval 3 37 to 53 103 to 125 250 Town wall Fortress city VFE Medieval 2 Medieval 4 48 to 69 124 to 158 200 Thick town wall Tribal camp Medieval Overhaul Neolithic 200 Stake wall, barricade or palisade Modern village base game Industrial to Ultra 1 200 open Modern town base game Industrial to Ultra 2 20 to 30 66 to 80 225 open Large town base game Industrial to Ultra 3 27 to 41 113 to 118 225 open Metropolis base game Industrial to Ultra 4 50 to 71 137 to 145 250 Sandbag and barricade line - Garrison is the sum over all wards. Guards in towers and buildings come on top: in tests a modern town had about 40 soldiers in total, a metropolis about 100. - Buildings is the range from mass tests. Empty cells: not measured yet. - Map is the edge length the settlement asks for. The larger of this and the world setting is used. - WD tier only matters with World Domination 2.0, see Compatibility. World Domination outposts These settlement types load only with World Domination 2.0. They are small camps for the World Domination types of tier 1 and 2, and war camps for vanguards at any tier. The map is 175 cells. WD type Tier Medieval Overhaul VFE Medieval 2 Modern --- --- --- --- --- Farming 1 Farm camp Farm camp Farm outpost Logging 1 Logging camp Logging camp Logging outpost Mining 1 Mining camp none Mining outpost Production 2 Production village Production village Production town Slavery 2 Slave village Slave village Prison camp Vanguard 1 to 4 War camp War camp Field camp Tier 3 and 4 use the large market town, fortress city, large town and metropolis. A World Domination settlement without a matching outpost gets the settlement type of its tier, see Compatibility. Defenders - Medieval Overhaul: archers, crossbowmen, pikemen and knights. - VFE Medieval 2: the fighters of VFE Medieval 2. - Modern: riflemen, snipers, assaulters, machine gunners, grenadiers, police and officers. Spacer and ultra factions field the spacer versions of the same roles, for example charge riflemen and marine assaulters. The metropolis adds 42 to 59 sandbag positions with mini turrets or autocannons. It has no gatehouses: roads run through gaps in the sandbag and barricade line. Searching a settlement Once no hostile pawn is left standing on a CBF settlement, Search site under Orders marks every closed container on the map for opening. It takes one click, no area. Your colonists then open them with the normal open job. Containers in unexplored rooms are skipped; the message says how many, so press again once they are explored. The button only shows on CBF settlements and is greyed out while hostiles are still standing. Medieval Overhaul Village Market town --- --- Large market town Fortress city VFE Medieval 2 Village Market town --- --- Large market town Fortress city Modern Modern town Large town --- --- Metropolis Performance Measured on 6 identical world tiles per type, compared with a vanilla faction base. The current version plans faster than the build these numbers come from. Type Map generation Memory Save file Pawns on the map --- --- --- --- --- Vanilla faction base 5.6 s +136 MB +8.8 MB 57 Village (MO) 21 s +135 MB +10 MB 103 Market town (MO) 17 s +148 MB +13 MB 150 Large market town (MO) 25 s +159 MB +16 MB 187 Fortress city (MO) 30 s +158 MB +16 MB 226 Modern town 10 s +147 MB +15 MB 110 Large town 16 s +168 MB +20 MB 157 Values are medians. Map generation happens once, when you first arrive at the settlement. It runs in the background for caravans, transport pods, shuttles and gravships, so the game does not freeze on arrival. While playing: - Colony without a CBF settlement: no measurable difference. - On a fortress city with 121 defenders: CBF's own code takes 0.05 to 0.09 % of the tick time. - World map: +0.1 ms per frame. - Game start: about +20 MB memory."}, {"t": "SettlementPlanDef", "u": "pages/SettlementPlanDef.html", "x": "SettlementPlanDef XML node: <CustomBaseFramework.SettlementPlanDef . Also takes the common fields defName , label and description . Field Type Default Meaning --- --- --- --- maxSoldiers whole number 40 Upper limit on fighters. Once the settlement has this many, further spawn anchors for fighters stay empty; gunner anchors still get theirs. 0 means no limit. maxCivilians whole number 30 Upper limit on civilians. Once the settlement has this many pawns that are not fighters, further civilian spawn anchors stay empty. 0 means no limit. share <br Weight number 0 Weight of this settlement type against the other types the same faction may get. 0 counts as 1. assaultDelayTicks <br Assault delay whole number 25000 How long the defenders wait before they attack, in ticks. treeShare number 0 Share of wild trees inside the settlement that are kept when it is built, 0 to 1. 0 clears them. minHouseSize <br Smallest house whole number 5 Edge length of the smallest house. Bays narrower than this are dropped. budget list of BudgetEntry none Which categories the settlement wants, how many of each, and what share of the area. coreSize <br Core size IntRange 66~74 Edge length of the settlement core, as a range. Everything else grows around it. defenderGroupKind <br Defender group text none Which group kind the defenders come from. Empty means Settlement, then Combat. defenderPoints <br Defender strength FloatRange 180~420 How strong the defenders are, as a point range. defendersOnWall <br Defenders on the wall number 0.25 What share of the defenders stands on the walkway rather than on the ground. districts list of DistrictSpec none How many districts this settlement has, and how many entries in total. embrasureEvery <br Embrasure spacing whole number 5 Every how many cells the wall gets an embrasure. 0 means none. Without the EMBRASURE role plain wall is used. emplacements <br Defensive positions list of WallEntry none Which pieces may serve as towers, gun batteries and the like. excludeFactions <br Excluded factions list of text none Factions (FactionDef defName) that never get this settlement type, even when their tags or tech level fit. factionTags <br Factions list of text none Which factions may use this settlement type. forAllFactions <br For every faction of these levels true/false false Also offers this type to factions that have no settlement type of their own, if their tech level is in techLevels. gatehouses list of WallEntry none Which pieces may serve as gatehouses. openTerrain true/false false Lets the planner choose a site that needs earthwork first, such as rock cleared away. Off by default; the planner still does it as a last resort. harbor HafenSpec none Harbor at the coast or on a wide river: which harbor buildings the settlement gets. Only for medieval settlements. ideoStyle true/false false Furniture gets the style of the owning faction's ideoligion. Needs Ideology. notchMax <br Maximum notch whole number 6 How deep the outline may cut inwards where the terrain calls for it. kind <br Settlement kind Ortsart (MedievalTown, ModernTown, Camp, Dungeon) MedievalTown On a settlement type: MedievalTown or ModernTown. ModernTown allows roads up to 6 wide and lays power conduits. Camp and Dungeon are switched off at the moment. On a pawn list entry: the pawn kind (PawnKindDef defName). campTypes list of text none SitePartDef names, such as BanditCamp or Outpost. This camp plan builds these quest sites. Only for the kind Camp. runMax <br Longest wall run whole number 6 Longest straight piece of wall before it must step. runMin <br Shortest wall run whole number 1 Shortest straight piece of wall before it may turn. mapSize <br Map size whole number 175 How large the map is that this settlement is generated on. materialList <br Material list MaterialListDef none Which material list this settlement type uses for walls, floors, doors, gates and the other roles. maxBuildingGap <br Largest gap between buildings whole number 0 Keeps enough room between houses for a path. minBuildingGap <br Smallest gap between buildings whole number 0 0 lets the framework decide from the loaded mods. minFillRatio <br Minimum fill number 0.85 How full the outline must be against its convex hull. Lower allows more ragged shapes. minLobe <br Smallest bulge whole number 5 Smallest usable bulge of the outline. 0 turns the check off. noWdLoot true/false false Switches off the World Domination loot budget for this settlement type. requiredMods <br Needs these mods list of text none Mods this settlement type needs beyond those the editor detects. The editor adds them to the dependencies when packaging. The game does not read the list. riafHolding text none Holding kind of A Rim of Ice and Fire this type is the plan for: Village, Town or Garrison. Empty means none. A type with a holding kind is only used for such holdings. roadLane <br Lane width whole number 1 Width of the side lanes. roadMain <br Main road width whole number 2 Width of the main road. There are only two widths on purpose; more cuts the map up visually. siteTags list of text none Tags that make this settlement type usable for quest sites. A site that asks for tags only takes types with a matching one. Empty means it is never used for sites. stepMax <br Largest step whole number 3 How far the wall may step sideways at once. style StyleDef none Old field. It only still picks the wall profile and the standard materials. Does not restrict piece selection. styles list of StyleDef none Old field. It only still picks the wall profile and the standard materials. Does not filter pieces. suburbIslands whole number 0 How many separate suburb areas grow outside the town, at most one per open map side. 0 turns this off. targetBuilt <br Target built share number 0 How densely built the settlement should feel. Hand-built MO settlements sit at 0.61. techLevels <br Tech levels list of TechLevel (Undefined, Animal, Neolithic, Medieval, Industrial, Spacer, Ultra, Archotech) none Tech levels this type is meant for. With forAllFactions they decide which factions qualify. Pawns from pawn lists are capped at the faction's level if it is listed, otherwise at the highest listed one. Empty means no cap. towerCapTotal <br Tower limit whole number 0 Upper limit on towers. 0 means as many as there are corners. towerCorner <br Corner tower size whole number 7 Edge length of a corner tower, measured on the centre line of the wall band. towerCurtain <br Curtain tower size whole number 7 Edge length of the towers that sit between the corners. towerRun <br Straight run before a tower whole number 6 How much straight wall must run before a tower may be placed. towerSpacing <br Tower spacing whole number 24 How far apart curtain towers stand. Larger means fewer towers. wallGap <br Gap to the wall whole number 1 How much room stays free inside the wall. Only pieces with wallContact may come closer. walls list of WallEntry none Wall strips, each one cell wide or one cell high, repeated along the wall by weight. The thickest decides how deep the wall is. On a ward, empty means no wall. On the settlement type they replace the strips of the wall profile. wards list of Stadtteil none The parts of the settlement: Town, Suburb and Rural, each with its own wall, garrison, districts and surfaces. wdLevel IntRange 0 World Domination tier this type stands for, as a range from 1 to 4. A settlement with a tier prefers the types closest to it. 0 means no tier. wdType list of text none World Domination settlement types this plan stands for, such as Mining or Slavery. Empty means any type. Only used with World Domination 2.0. weightHilly <br Prefers hills number 0 How strongly this settlement type wants hilly terrain. weightNearRoad <br Prefers roads number 1 How strongly this settlement type wants to sit near a road on the world map. weightNearWater <br Prefers water number 0 How strongly this settlement type wants to sit near water. worldTexture <br World map icon text none Image of this settlement on the world map when zoomed in. It is tinted in the faction colour.<br <br All images in Textures/World/WorldObjects/CBF can be chosen, including those of other mods. Back to the field reference"}, {"t": "Settlement types", "u": "pages/Settlements.html", "x": "Settlements A settlement plan describes one kind of place: a market town, a fortress city, a village, a camp. The generator builds it fresh for every faction base, so no two look the same. 1. List grouped by tech level. The number is the weight, a red row has an error that stops the plan from being built. 2. Preview. Off by default, because planning a large town takes a few seconds. Show preview draws the plan on a flat map, Reroll tries a new seed. 3. General : name, kind, tech levels, factions and more. 4. Wards on the right: one tab per ring of the settlement. 5. Faction overview below the list shows which faction gets which settlement, see Factions. Settlement types from CBF or other mods can be changed too. Save as patch stores your changes as a patch in your own mod, see Changing content of other mods. Copy into my mod instead makes an independent copy under a new name. Loot of a built settlement After Build , the button Show loot at the bottom shows what the loot of the settlement built last is worth in silver: in total, per ward and per kind of goods. The value is counted while the settlement is built and saved with it. Medieval Overhaul containers that only fill when searched are not included. What the generator handles by itself - Buildings that belong at the wall, such as barracks, dock onto the town wall. The wall stays and the building gets a door onto the walkway. - An open side of such a building that does not touch the wall is closed with the building's own wall. - Wall parts cut off by a mountain turn into rock. Large rock pockets behind the wall get a postern. Small rock islands that touch the planned wall stay on the wall line and outside the town and replace the wall there; only the part inside the town is removed. - Rubble inside the settlement is cleared. - Modern towns always have wards shaped like a spider web: 7 to 10 corners with sagging sides, also with a sandbag or barricade line. They never get gatehouses; roads run through gaps. - Medieval settlements at the coast or on a wide river get a harbor with jetties, fishing huts, harbor store, harbor master and boat sheds, linked to the town by a path. The settlement type lists the harbor buildings under harbor , see Settlement types in XML. - Buildings whose graphic only has one direction, such as Medieval Overhaul carts, are never rotated. - Stoves, ovens and workbenches face into the room, and every thing gets a stuff it can be made of. General - Label : the name players see. - Kind of place : medieval town, modern town or camp. Click to switch. - Weight : how often this plan is drawn against other plans of the same faction. - Map size : edge length of the map the settlement type asks for, 150 to 400. The larger of this and the world setting is used. - Core size : edge length of the settlement core in cells, as a range. The suburb and the countryside grow around it. - Tree share : share of wild trees inside the settlement that stay standing. - Material list : what the generator uses for walls, doors, gates, fences, roads and roofs it builds itself, see Material lists. Required; the row is red without one. - WD tier : only with World Domination 2.0 active. A dropdown with the tiers of World Domination, a single tier or a range such as Tier 1 to 2. The tier this plan stands for, see Compatibility. - No WD loot : only with World Domination 2.0 active. Switches off the extra loot budget World Domination gives settlements of this type. - Ideoligion style : only with Ideology active. Furniture gets the style of the owning faction's ideoligion. - RIAF holding : only with A Rim of Ice and Fire active. None, Village, Town or Garrison. Makes this type the plan for holdings of that kind, see Compatibility. A type with a holding kind is not used for normal settlements. - Camp types : only for plans of the kind camp. The quest sites this plan builds, such as bandit camps, outposts or work sites. - WD type : only with World Domination 2.0 active. The World Domination settlement types this plan stands for, such as mining camp or slave village. Only the types of the chosen tier are offered; empty means any type. - Tech levels : which ages the plan fits. Click switches one level, right-click picks this level and all above. Pawn lists use their members of the highest tech level at or below the settlement's level. - For all factions of these levels : the plan serves every faction of a ticked tech level, unless that faction has plans of its own. - Factions : factions or faction categories that get this plan in any case. - Excluded : factions that never get it. - Mods : the mods the plan needs. Most are found automatically, + by hand adds more. - World map icon : the symbol of this place on the world map, tinted in the faction color. Wards A ward is one ring: the old town behind the high wall, the suburb behind a lighter one, the countryside around them. The innermost ward is protected best and filled first. In the preview, Wards colours the three rings: the old town red, the suburb ochre and the countryside green. Each settlement type has at most one ward of each kind; the kind is fixed, see What you can change. Fortification - Walls : the wall pieces of this ring. Several entries give a wall that varies along its length. - Emplacements : towers and other works in the ring. The generator spaces them itself, corners first. - Gatehouses : built wherever a road crosses the wall. - Door : the door that closes the passages in the wall. Empty uses the door of the material list. Gatehouses are set under Fortification . Modern towns and the countryside have no fortification. Garrison Pawn lists that defend this ward, with a count and a post: Inside , Wall or Gate . The editor shows the point value. A ward with a garrison ignores the defender points of the settlement type; without entries the ward takes its share of them. How the defenders behave: - Every ward defends itself. Defenders do not gather in the center and do not storm out together. - Guards on the wall and at the gates hold their spot, shooters stay at the embrasures. - Crews of ballistas, catapults and trebuchets stay at their gun, even when many defenders have fallen. - Defenders of a settlement cannot be recruited as prisoners. Defences Defences spread over the whole ward: turret posts, sandbag nests, bunkers. Each entry names a category or one piece and a count. The first one stands at a random spot along a road; every further one goes where it is farthest from those already placed. Use districts instead when a defence belongs to one district only. Districts A district is a building programme. Districts without a name of their own are numbered, District 1, District 2 and so on. 1. Buildings : a category or one specific piece, with min, max and weight. Filler entries fill the space left at the end. 2. Field : a crop plot grown around the buildings named under Farmsteads . The plants come from single plants or from a Crop list , see Loot. 3. Pen : animals from a pawn list, the herd size, and what grows inside. 4. Mine : a tunnel with chambers dug into the rock from a building of the district. You set the ore and its share, length and width of the tunnel, number and size of the chambers, rock chunks, the buildings it may start at, and the things inside. Things opens a window with a sketch of the mine and one column per place: Entrance (the first cells of the tunnel right behind the entrance at the hut), Tunnel (along the tunnels, only where there is room to pass) and Chamber (in a chamber at the end of a tunnel, small things by the wall). Each entry is a thing or an object list, with material (empty takes stone or wood of the map), count, and optional content with an amount, for example iron on a shelf. ⇄ moves an entry to another place. 5. Location of the district : tick where the district should start: near the center, near the gate, near the wall or on the main road. Several ticks count equally, the district looks for a spot that suits all of them. Nothing ticked means a random spot away from the other districts. In XML the fields are numbers; only their ratio counts, so 1 and 0 cover every normal case. + Building , + Field , + Pen and + Mine add rows. The ... button next to a category shows the pieces it covers. Ideoligion conditions on entries With Ideology active, every building entry of a district has a small I button. It adds memes or precepts the owning faction's ideoligion must have ( only with ) or must not have ( not with ). The entry is then only built when the condition holds, for example a slaughterhouse only for factions with a meat preference. The button is highlighted when a condition is set; its tooltip lists them."}, {"t": "SocketSpec", "u": "pages/SocketSpec.html", "x": "SocketSpec Field Type Default Meaning --- --- --- --- cells IntRange 0 Not used at the moment. edge text South Not used at the moment. type text RoadFront Not used at the moment. Sockets are not evaluated. Back to the field reference"}, {"t": "Stadtteil", "u": "pages/Stadtteil.html", "x": "Stadtteil Field Type Default Meaning --- --- --- --- buildingGap IntRange 0 Gap between buildings in this ward, in cells, as a range. 0~0 takes minBuildingGap and maxBuildingGap of the settlement. kind <br Kind Stadtteilart (Town, Castle, Suburb, Rural) Town On a ward: which part of the settlement it is, Town, Suburb or Rural. Castle is accepted but not planned as a part of its own. On an object list: Fill fills the containers, Empty places them without loot or occupants. decor list of Dekospec none Pieces set along the lanes and squares of this ward. Spacing and upper limit come from each entry. defenses list of Dekospec none Defences spread over the lanes of this ward, each as far from the others as possible. count gives how many, else max, else one. districts list of DistrictSpec none How many districts this settlement has, and how many entries in total. emplacements <br Defensive positions list of WallEntry none Which pieces may serve as towers, gun batteries and the like. fields list of Ackerspec none Field types. Only Suburb and Rural grow fields. On a district the fields are laid by its own houses. garrison list of Garnisonseintrag none Pawns stationed in this ward. Each entry names a pawn list, a count and a post. gatehouses list of WallEntry none Which pieces may serve as gatehouses. ground list of text none The ground between the houses. Empty means the map stays as it is. Lanes, squares and fields keep their own surface and are left alone. label text none The name shown on the world map and in the settlement tooltip. The defName stays as it is. lanternGap whole number 0 Distance between lanterns along the lanes, in cells. 0 means 12. lanterns list of text none Lantern things for the lanes of this ward. The first one that exists is used. Empty leaves the ward dark. name text Town Name of the entry. On a district it links fields and pens to its houses and must be unique. On a pen it appears in messages. On a ward it is replaced by the ward kind, on a field it is not read, and in wall lists piece is written instead. pens list of Gehegespec none Animal pen types. Only the Rural ward builds pens. On a district they go near its own houses. clearRubble true/false true Clears plants from open ground in this ward. Off keeps the plants and a share of the trees, the more the further out the ward lies. roadEdges <br Kerb list of text none Surface of the outermost cells of lanes, squares and gate passages in this ward. The first terrain that exists is used. Empty keeps the road surface. roads <br Paths list of text none The surface of lanes, squares and gate passages in this ward. Empty means the standard surface. gate text none Door for the passages in this ward's wall. Empty takes it from the material list. Not the gatehouse, that is set under gatehouses. supply number 1.2 Size of the pens in the Rural ward relative to the built area. Default 1.2. A pen covers 60 to 240 cells. walls list of WallEntry none Wall strips, each one cell wide or one cell high, repeated along the wall by weight. The thickest decides how deep the wall is. On a ward, empty means no wall. On the settlement type they replace the strips of the wall profile. Back to the field reference"}, {"t": "StyleDef", "u": "pages/StyleDef.html", "x": "StyleDef XML node: <CustomBaseFramework.StyleDef . Also takes the common fields defName , label and description . Field Type Default Meaning --- --- --- --- fallback <br Falls back to StyleDef none Used when this style has nothing for a role. Back to the field reference"}, {"t": "Tauscheintrag", "u": "pages/Tauscheintrag.html", "x": "Tauscheintrag Field Type Default Meaning --- --- --- --- weight number 1 Weight of this target against the other targets for the same from. 0 or less drops it. from text none defName of the pawn kind or thing to replace. to text none defName of the replacement. Targets that do not exist are skipped. Back to the field reference"}, {"t": "Techschritt", "u": "pages/Techschritt.html", "x": "Techschritt Field Type Default Meaning --- --- --- --- step text none The map generation step (GenStepDef defName). techLevel TechLevel (Undefined, Animal, Neolithic, Medieval, Industrial, Spacer, Ultra, Archotech) Industrial Lowest faction tech level at which the step runs. Default Industrial. Back to the field reference"}, {"t": "TerrainWeight", "u": "pages/TerrainWeight.html", "x": "TerrainWeight Field Type Default Meaning --- --- --- --- terrain <br Floor text none The floor layer. One character per cell, resolved through the legend. weight number 1 How likely this is picked over its siblings. Belongs on the settlement, not on the piece. Back to the field reference"}, {"t": "Unit swap", "u": "pages/Unit-Swap.html", "x": "Unit swap Pawn lists and pieces name general units and things: an archer, a footman, a banner. Unit swap decides what each faction uses instead, so a noble house sends its own knights and hangs its own banners, without a copy of every list and piece per faction. 1. Factions on the left. Below each name: which mods have swap rules for it. With swap shows only those. 2. Units or Things : every unit from pawn lists and pieces, every thing from pieces and object lists. Animals also shows animals from pens and vermin. 3. Becomes for this faction : the targets. + adds a target from the faction's own units, several targets are drawn by weight. For things, ... picks one target of the same kind. 4. Without rule : what the faction would get without a rule. The fallback takes the faction's own unit with the same way of fighting and the closest combat power. Things stay as they are. 5. Your rule : name, Priority and the mod it is saved in. 6. Rules of other mods : shown, never changed. 1. + opens the picker with the faction's own units, with combat power. 2. Search in names. 3. Mod filter . 4. Unit from all mods when the faction has nothing fitting. Priority Several mods can bring rules for the same faction. Per unit or thing, the rule with the highest priority applies. To override a rule of another mod, give yours a higher priority. Saving writes a file of its own into your mod, under Defs/CustomBaseFramework/Einheitentausch . Files of other mods are never touched."}, {"t": "UnitSwapDef", "u": "pages/UnitSwapDef.html", "x": "UnitSwapDef XML node: <CustomBaseFramework.UnitSwapDef . Also takes the common fields defName , label and description . Field Type Default Meaning --- --- --- --- things list of Tauscheintrag none Thing replacements for this faction. Each entry swaps the thing in from for the thing in to. units list of Tauscheintrag none Pawn kind replacements for this faction, each entry swaps the kind in from for the kind in to. Humanlike kinds without a rule that the faction does not use become its closest own kind, unless they are listed in neutral. faction text none The faction (FactionDef defName) these rules apply to. It may stay empty when the def only lists neutral pawn kinds. neutral list of text none Pawn kinds (defName) that the fallback swap never changes, for any faction. priority whole number 0 When several rule sets cover the same faction and the same from, only the highest priority counts. Equal priorities are merged. Back to the field reference"}, {"t": "WallEntry", "u": "pages/WallEntry.html", "x": "WallEntry Field Type Default Meaning --- --- --- --- count IntRange 0 How many of these the settlement wants, as a range. name text none Name of the entry. On a district it links fields and pens to its houses and must be unique. On a pen it appears in messages. On a ward it is replaced by the ward kind, on a field it is not read, and in wall lists piece is written instead. piece PieceDef none One specific piece by defName. It wins over a category in the same entry. In wall lists the name may also be a category. tag <br Category (old) PieceCategoryDef none The first category. Kept so old files still load; write categories instead. weight number 1 How likely this is picked over its siblings. Belongs on the settlement, not on the piece. Back to the field reference"}, {"t": "WallProfileDef", "u": "pages/WallProfileDef.html", "x": "WallProfileDef XML node: <CustomBaseFramework.WallProfileDef . Also takes the common fields defName , label and description . Field Type Default Meaning --- --- --- --- depth whole number 3 Wall thickness in cells this profile is drawn for: 1, 3 or 5. A settlement takes the profile whose depth matches its wall. gatehouse text none Piece placed centred on each gate. Both sides must be odd. Empty builds a plain gate. gatehouseOutward text South Which side of the gatehouse piece faces out as drawn: North, East, South or West. Default South. outwardIsLow true/false true Which end of a wall strip faces out of the town. True means the bottom of an upright strip or the left end of a flat one. An Outside anchor in the strip wins. On a piece, empty takes the value of the wall profile, whose default is true. segments list of text none Wall strips, each one cell wide or one cell high, repeated along the wall in this order. style StyleDef none Old field. It only still picks the wall profile and the standard materials. Does not restrict piece selection. tower text none Piece used for towers. It must be square with an odd edge length. Empty lets the generator build a round tower. usable true/false true False switches the profile off. It is also switched off when none of its segments is usable. Back to the field reference"}];